window.HU_ESAT_PAPERS={"EC1": {"title": "ESAT Set C Mathematics 1", "questions": 27, "rating": 8.1}, "EC2": {"title": "ESAT Set C Mathematics 2", "questions": 27, "rating": 8.1}, "ED1": {"title": "ESAT Set D Mathematics 1", "questions": 27, "rating": 8.0}, "ED2": {"title": "ESAT Set D Mathematics 2", "questions": 27, "rating": 8.1}, "EB1": {"title": "ESAT Set B Mathematics 1", "questions": 27, "rating": 8.4}, "EB2": {"title": "ESAT Set B Mathematics 2", "questions": 27, "rating": 8.3}, "EA1": {"title": "ESAT Set A Mathematics 1", "questions": 27, "rating": 7.7}, "EA2": {"title": "ESAT Set A Mathematics 2", "questions": 27, "rating": 7.6}};
window.HU_ESAT_TOPICS={"units": "Units", "number": "Number", "ratio": "Ratio and proportion", "algebra": "Algebra", "geometry": "Geometry", "stats": "Statistics", "prob": "Probability", "alg": "Algebra and functions", "seq": "Sequences and series", "coord": "Coordinate geometry", "trig": "Trigonometry", "explog": "Exponentials and logarithms", "diff": "Differentiation", "int": "Integration", "graphs": "Graphs of functions"};
window.HU_ESAT=[{"id": "EA1-1", "paper": "EA1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>A tank is a cuboid with a horizontal rectangular base measuring $2\\text{ m}$ by $1.5\\text{ m}$, and it starts empty. Tap $A$ delivers $15$ litres per minute and tap $B$ delivers $9$ litres per minute. A drain at the bottom removes water at a constant $18$ litres per minute whenever it is open and there is water in the tank.<\/p><p>At the start, tap $A$ is turned on, but the drain has been left open by mistake. Ten minutes later tap $B$ is also turned on. When the water first reaches a depth of $12\\text{ cm}$, the drain is closed. How long after the start does the water reach a depth of $60\\text{ cm}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$ hours $10$ minutes", "$2$ hours $15$ minutes", "$2$ hours $10$ minutes", "$2$ hours $20$ minutes", "$2$ hours", "$1$ hour $15$ minutes"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The base has area $3\\text{ m}^2$, so each centimetre of depth is $0.03\\text{ m}^3 = 30$ litres.<\/p><p>For the first $10$ minutes tap $A$ alone cannot beat the drain ($15 &lt; 18$), so the tank stays empty: the water level cannot go negative. From then on the net rate is $15 + 9 - 18 = 6$ litres per minute, so reaching $12\\text{ cm}$ ($360$ litres) takes $60$ minutes. With the drain closed the rate is $24$ litres per minute, and the remaining $48\\text{ cm}$ ($1440$ litres) takes another $60$ minutes. The total is $10 + 60 + 60 = 130$ minutes.<\/p><p>The trap is to let the tank go to $-30$ litres in the first ten minutes, which adds $5$ minutes and gives $2$ hours $15$ minutes.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "ratio"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA1-2", "paper": "EA1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>The times, $t$ minutes, taken by a group of runners to complete a course are shown in a histogram with four bars. The bar for $0\\le t&lt;10$ is $1\\text{ cm}$ high, the bar for $10\\le t&lt;15$ is $3\\text{ cm}$ high, the bar for $15\\le t&lt;20$ is $4\\text{ cm}$ high and the bar for $20\\le t&lt;40$ is $0.75\\text{ cm}$ high. Exactly $30$ runners had times in the interval $10\\le t&lt;15$.<\/p><p>Using linear interpolation, and taking the lower and upper quartiles to be the $\\tfrac{n}{4}$th and $\\tfrac{3n}{4}$th values, where $n$ is the number of runners, estimate the interquartile range in minutes.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{15}{2}$", "$9$", "$\\dfrac{20}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{35}{3}$", "$10$", "$\\dfrac{25}{3}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>In a histogram, frequency is proportional to area, not height. The $10\\le t&lt;15$ bar has area $5\\times 3 = 15$ units for $30$ runners, so each unit of area is $2$ runners. The frequencies are $10\\times 1\\times 2 = 20$, $30$, $5\\times 4\\times 2 = 40$ and $20\\times 0.75\\times 2 = 30$, so $n = 120$.<\/p><p>The cumulative frequencies at $t = 10, 15, 20, 40$ are $20, 50, 90, 120$. The lower quartile (the $30$th value) is $10$ runners into the second class: $10 + 5\\times\\tfrac{10}{30} = \\tfrac{35}{3}$. The upper quartile (the $90$th value) is exactly at $t = 20$. So the interquartile range is $20 - \\tfrac{35}{3} = \\tfrac{25}{3}$.<\/p><p>Taking the bar heights as proportional to frequency, or reading off the lower quartile at a class boundary, gives the popular wrong answers.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA1-3", "paper": "EA1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>For how many real values of $k$ does the equation<\/p>\\[(k-1)x^2 - 4x + (k+2) = 0\\]<p>have exactly one real solution for $x$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$4$", "$1$", "$3$", "$2$", "$6$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>If $k = 1$ the equation is linear, $-4x + 3 = 0$, which has exactly one solution. Otherwise it is a quadratic, with exactly one real solution when the discriminant is zero:<\/p>\\[16 - 4(k-1)(k+2) = 0 \\iff k^2 + k - 6 = 0 \\iff k = -3 \\text{ or } k = 2.\\]<p>So there are $3$ values. The usual answer is $2$, from forgetting that the $x^2$ term can vanish.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA1-4", "paper": "EA1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Find the sum of all real solutions of the equation<\/p>\\[\\left(x^2 - 3x\\right)^2 + \\left(x^2 - 3x\\right) - 6 = 0.\\]", "opts": ["$-3$", "$2$", "$6$", "$3$", "$0$", "$-1$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Put $u = x^2 - 3x$. Then $u^2 + u - 6 = 0$, so $u = 2$ or $u = -3$.<\/p><p>The equation $x^2 - 3x = 2$ has discriminant $9 + 8 = 17 > 0$, so two real roots with sum $3$. The equation $x^2 - 3x + 3 = 0$ has discriminant $9 - 12 &lt; 0$, so no real roots. (Equivalently, $x^2 - 3x = \\left(x - \\tfrac32\\right)^2 - \\tfrac94 \\ge -\\tfrac94$, so $u = -3$ is impossible.)<\/p><p>The sum is $3$. Adding the root sums of both quadratics without checking gives $6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA1-5", "paper": "EA1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Four people are chosen at random. Assume that each person is equally likely to have been born in any of the $12$ months, independently of the others. What is the probability that their four birth months include exactly three different months?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{55}{864}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{55}{144}$", "$\\dfrac{55}{72}$", "$\\dfrac{55}{216}$", "$\\dfrac{41}{96}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Exactly three months means one pair shares a month and the other two people have different months from each other and from the pair. Choose the pair in $\\binom42 = 6$ ways, then the months for the pair and the two others in $12\\times 11\\times 10$ ways.<\/p>\\[P = \\frac{6\\times 12\\times 11\\times 10}{12^4} = \\frac{7920}{20736} = \\frac{55}{144}.\\]<p>Leaving out the factor $6$ gives $\\tfrac{55}{864}$. The option $\\tfrac{41}{96}$ is the probability that at least two share a month, which also includes three or four sharing and two separate pairs.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA1-6", "paper": "EA1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>The numbers $x = 12$ and $y = 4$ are each given correct to the nearest integer. What is the upper bound of<\/p>\\[\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\,?\\]", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{16}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{15}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{17}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{16}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{17}{7}$", "$2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Since $x$ and $y$ both appear in the numerator and the denominator, you cannot simply take the largest numerator over the smallest denominator: that would need $x$ at its upper bound and at its lower bound at the same time. Instead write<\/p>\\[\\frac{x+y}{x-y} = 1 + \\frac{2y}{x-y},\\]<p>which increases as $y$ increases and as $x$ decreases. So take $x = 11.5$ and $y = 4.5$, giving $\\dfrac{16}{7}$. The tempting $\\dfrac{17}{7}$ comes from $\\dfrac{12.5+4.5}{11.5-4.5}$, which uses two different values of $x$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA1-7", "paper": "EA1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>How many of the following eight numbers are factors of $2^{20} - 1$?<\/p>\\[3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 11,\\quad 13,\\quad 25,\\quad 31,\\quad 41\\]", "opts": ["$3$", "$6$", "$5$", "$8$", "$7$", "$4$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Use the difference of two squares: $2^{20} - 1 = (2^{10} - 1)(2^{10} + 1) = 1023\\times 1025$. Now $1023 = 3\\times 11\\times 31$ and $1025 = 25\\times 41$, so<\/p>\\[2^{20} - 1 = 3\\times 5^2\\times 11\\times 31\\times 41.\\]<p>So $3, 5, 11, 25, 31, 41$ are factors and $7$ and $13$ are not: $6$ in total. Splitting further as $(2^5-1)(2^5+1)(2^{10}+1)$ gives the same factors; it is easy to forget that $25$, not just $5$, divides $1025$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "algebra"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-8", "paper": "EA1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Priya cycles from $P$ to $Q$ and Quentin cycles from $Q$ to $P$ along the same road. They set off at the same time and each rides at a constant speed. They pass each other after $2$ hours, and Priya reaches $Q$ exactly $1$ hour $20$ minutes after they pass. How long does Quentin's whole journey take?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$ hours", "$4$ hours $40$ minutes", "$3$ hours $20$ minutes", "$3$ hours", "$4$ hours", "$5$ hours"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Let the speeds be $p$ and $q$. After they pass, Priya still has to cover the stretch Quentin rode in the first $2$ hours, namely $2q$, and she does it in $\\tfrac43$ hours: $2q = \\tfrac43 p$, so $q = \\tfrac23 p$.<\/p><p>Quentin must similarly cover Priya's first stretch $2p$ at speed $q$, which takes $\\dfrac{2p}{q} = 3$ hours. His whole journey is $2 + 3 = 5$ hours. (In general the meeting time is the geometric mean of the two times after meeting: $2^2 = \\tfrac43\\times 3$.) The option $3$ hours stops at the time after passing; $3$ hours $20$ minutes is Priya's journey.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio", "algebra"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-9", "paper": "EA1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>$AB$ is a diameter of a circle, and the tangent to the circle at $B$ is drawn. A straight line through $A$ meets the circle again at $C$ and meets the tangent at $T$, with $C$ between $A$ and $T$. Given that $AC = 9$ and $CT = 16$, find the distance from $C$ to the line $AB$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$12$", "$\\dfrac{48}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{36}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{15}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{27}{5}$", "$9$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA1-9.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Circle with diameter AB, the tangent at B, and the line from A through C to T on the tangent, with right angles at B and C, AC = 9, CT = 16, and the perpendicular h from C to AB highlighted.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The tangent is perpendicular to the diameter, so $\\angle ABT = 90^\\circ$, and the angle in a semicircle gives $\\angle ACB = 90^\\circ$. So $BC$ is the altitude from the right angle of triangle $ABT$, whose hypotenuse is $AT = 25$.<\/p><p>Triangles $ACB$ and $ABT$ are similar (common angle $A$, each with a right angle), so $AB^2 = AC\\times AT = 225$ and $AB = 15$; then $BC = \\sqrt{225 - 81} = 12$. In the right-angled triangle $ACB$, the distance from $C$ to $AB$ is the altitude to the hypotenuse:<\/p>\\[h = \\frac{AC\\times BC}{AB} = \\frac{9\\times 12}{15} = \\frac{36}{5}.\\]<p>The options $\\tfrac{27}{5}$ and $\\tfrac{48}{5}$ are the two pieces into which this altitude cuts $AB$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-10", "paper": "EA1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>A regular hexagon has area $60\\text{ cm}^2$. Label its sides $1$ to $6$ in order around the hexagon. The midpoints of sides $1$, $3$ and $5$ are joined to form a triangle. What is the area of this triangle in $\\text{cm}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$15$", "$22.5$", "$20$", "$24$", "$30$", "$25$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA1-10.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Regular hexagon with sides numbered 1 to 6 and the shaded triangle joining the midpoints of sides 1, 3 and 5; dashed lines of length root 3 join the centre to these midpoints, 120 degrees apart.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Take the hexagon with side $2$, made of six equilateral triangles of side $2$, so its area is $6\\sqrt3$. The midpoint of each side is at distance $\\sqrt3$ from the centre (the height of one of those equilateral triangles), and the three chosen midpoints are $120^\\circ$ apart.<\/p><p>So the triangle is equilateral with circumradius $\\sqrt3$, hence side $\\sqrt3\\times\\sqrt3 = 3$ and area $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\times 9 = \\tfrac{9\\sqrt3}{4}$. The ratio to the hexagon is $\\tfrac{9}{24} = \\tfrac38$, and $\\tfrac38\\times 60 = 22.5$. The option $30$ is the triangle on alternate vertices, which is half the hexagon.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-11", "paper": "EA1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>What is the smallest positive integer $n$ such that $12n$ is a perfect square and $18n$ is a perfect cube?<\/p>", "opts": ["$36$", "$144$", "$24$", "$72$", "$12$", "$6$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Any prime other than $2$ or $3$ in $n$ would only make $n$ bigger, so let $n = 2^a 3^b$. Then $12n = 2^{a+2}3^{b+1}$ is a square when $a$ is even and $b$ is odd, and $18n = 2^{a+1}3^{b+2}$ is a cube when $a\\equiv 2$ and $b\\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$.<\/p><p>The smallest choices are $a = 2$ and $b = 1$, so $n = 12$. Check: $12\\times 12 = 144 = 12^2$ and $18\\times 12 = 216 = 6^3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA1-12", "paper": "EA1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Evaluate<\/p>\\[\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}+1\\sqrt{2}} + \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{3}} + \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{4}} + \\cdots + \\frac{1}{100\\sqrt{99}+99\\sqrt{100}}.\\]", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{11}{10}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{10}{11}$", "$9$", "$\\dfrac{99}{100}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{10}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The general term is $\\dfrac{1}{(k+1)\\sqrt{k} + k\\sqrt{k+1}}$. Take out $\\sqrt{k}\\sqrt{k+1}$ and rationalise:<\/p>\\[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}\\sqrt{k+1}\\left(\\sqrt{k+1}+\\sqrt{k}\\right)} = \\frac{\\sqrt{k+1}-\\sqrt{k}}{\\sqrt{k}\\sqrt{k+1}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k+1}}.\\]<p>Summing from $k = 1$ to $99$ telescopes to $1 - \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{100}} = \\dfrac{9}{10}$. Writing $\\tfrac1k - \\tfrac1{k+1}$ by mistake gives $\\tfrac{99}{100}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "algebra"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA1-13", "paper": "EA1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>The positive real number $x$ satisfies $x - \\dfrac{1}{x} = 3$. Find the value of<\/p>\\[x^6 - \\frac{1}{x^6}.\\]", "opts": ["$396$", "$120\\sqrt{13}$", "$36\\sqrt{13}$", "$360\\sqrt{13}$", "$-360\\sqrt{13}$", "$1296$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Factorise: $x^6 - \\dfrac{1}{x^6} = \\left(x^3 - \\dfrac{1}{x^3}\\right)\\left(x^3 + \\dfrac{1}{x^3}\\right)$.<\/p><p>With $t = x - \\tfrac1x = 3$, cubing gives $x^3 - \\tfrac1{x^3} = t^3 + 3t = 36$. Next, $\\left(x + \\tfrac1x\\right)^2 = t^2 + 4 = 13$, and since $x>0$, $s = x + \\tfrac1x = \\sqrt{13}$. Then $x^3 + \\tfrac1{x^3} = s^3 - 3s = 13\\sqrt{13} - 3\\sqrt{13} = 10\\sqrt{13}$.<\/p><p>So the answer is $36\\times 10\\sqrt{13} = 360\\sqrt{13}$. Ignoring the condition $x>0$ allows the negative value; using $x^3 + \\tfrac1{x^3} = 36$ as well gives $1296$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA1-14", "paper": "EA1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A triangle has sides of length $5$, $7$ and $8$. What is the radius of the circle inscribed in the triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$", "$\\sqrt{3}$", "$\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{4}$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}$", "$2\\sqrt{3}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA1-14.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Triangle with sides 5, 7 and 8, the angle theta between the sides 5 and 8, and its incircle with the three radii r to the touching points; dashed lines join the incentre to the vertices.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>By the cosine rule, the angle $\\theta$ opposite the side $7$ has $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{25 + 64 - 49}{2\\times 5\\times 8} = \\dfrac12$, so $\\theta = 60^\\circ$ and the area is $\\tfrac12\\times 5\\times 8\\times\\sin 60^\\circ = 10\\sqrt3$.<\/p><p>Splitting the triangle into three triangles from the incentre gives area $= r\\times s$, where $s = 10$ is the semi-perimeter. So $r = \\dfrac{10\\sqrt3}{10} = \\sqrt3$. Using the full perimeter gives $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$; the option $\\tfrac{7\\sqrt3}{3}$ is the circumradius.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA1-15", "paper": "EA1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The five numbers $3$, $7$, $12$, $15$ and $x$, where $x$ is a real number, have a mean equal to their median. What is the sum of all possible values of $x$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$21$", "$30$", "$23$", "$\\dfrac{37}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{121}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{129}{4}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The mean is $\\dfrac{37 + x}{5}$. The median depends on where $x$ sits.<\/p><p>If $x\\le 7$ the median is $7$, so $37 + x = 35$ and $x = -2$, which is valid. If $7\\le x\\le 12$ the median is $x$, so $37 + x = 5x$ and $x = \\tfrac{37}{4}$, valid. If $x\\ge 12$ the median is $12$, so $x = 23$, valid.<\/p><p>The sum is $-2 + \\tfrac{37}{4} + 23 = \\tfrac{121}{4}$. Assuming $x$ must be positive (it need not be) loses $-2$ and gives $\\tfrac{129}{4}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EA1-16", "paper": "EA1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>A solid wooden cube has edges of length $10\\text{ cm}$. Three square tunnels, each with cross-section $4\\text{ cm}$ by $4\\text{ cm}$, are cut right through the cube. Each tunnel runs between the centres of a pair of opposite faces, with its sides parallel to the edges of the cube. What is the total surface area, inside and outside, of the resulting solid, in $\\text{cm}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$888$", "$984$", "$1080$", "$600$", "$696$", "$792$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA1-16.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Cross-section through the centre of the cube parallel to a face: a 10 by 10 square whose four 3 by 3 corner squares are wood, with the tunnel arms and the 4 by 4 central cube left open.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The figure shows the cross-section through the centre of the cube, parallel to one face: the shaded corner squares are wood and the white cross is the tunnels.<\/p><p>Outside: each face loses a $4\\times 4$ square, leaving $6\\times(100 - 16) = 504$.<\/p><p>Inside: the three tunnels share a central $4\\times 4\\times 4$ cube, all of whose faces are open, so there are no walls there. From each face, a tunnel arm runs $\\tfrac{10 - 4}{2} = 3\\text{ cm}$ in to the central cube, and has four walls of $4\\times 3$, so area $48$. There are $6$ arms, giving $288$.<\/p><p>The total is $504 + 288 = 792$. Treating each tunnel as having full length walls ($3\\times 4\\times 40 = 480$) gives $984$, which counts walls through the hollow centre.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EA1-17", "paper": "EA1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A bag contains $4$ red and $3$ blue counters. Counters are taken out one at a time, at random and without replacement, until the bag is empty. What is the probability that the second blue counter is taken out before the third red counter?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{22}{35}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{13}{35}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{18}{35}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The second blue comes before the third red exactly when at least two of the first four counters are blue (in the first four draws, one of these two events must happen, and they cannot both).<\/p><p>Every set of four counters is equally likely to be the first four, out of $\\binom74 = 35$. All four red: $1$ way. Exactly one blue: $3\\times\\binom43 = 12$ ways. So the probability is $1 - \\tfrac{13}{35} = \\tfrac{22}{35}$.<\/p><p>The option $\\tfrac{13}{35}$ is the complement, and $\\tfrac{18}{35}$ counts only exactly two blues among the first four.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA1-18", "paper": "EA1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Three different vertices of a cube are chosen at random, with every set of three vertices equally likely. What is the probability that they form a right-angled triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{3}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{6}{7}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA1-18.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Two cubes: in the first, the three neighbours of a vertex V form a shaded equilateral triangle; in the second, a shaded right-angled triangle with sides 1, root 2 and root 3 made of an edge, a face diagonal and a space diagonal.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>There are $\\binom83 = 56$ sets, and no three vertices of a cube are collinear. It is easier to count the triangles that are <em>not<\/em> right-angled.<\/p><p>Take a vertex $V$ and its three neighbours: the neighbours are joined to each other by face diagonals, so they form an equilateral triangle. This gives $8$ equilateral triangles, one for each vertex. Every other triangle contains an edge of the cube, and its sides are either $1, 1, \\sqrt2$ (half a face) or $1, \\sqrt2, \\sqrt3$ (an edge, a face diagonal and a space diagonal), taking the edge as $1$. Both satisfy Pythagoras, so all of these are right-angled. So the probability is $\\dfrac{56 - 8}{56} = \\dfrac67$.<\/p><p>Counting only the triangles lying in a face, $6\\times 4 = 24$, gives the trap answer $\\tfrac37$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob", "geometry"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA1-19", "paper": "EA1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Two chords $AC$ and $BD$ of a circle are perpendicular and meet at a point $X$ inside the circle. Given that $AX = 2$, $XC = 6$ and $BX = 3$, find the radius of the circle.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt{5}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{65}}{2}$", "$4$", "$\\dfrac{7}{2}$", "$\\sqrt{65}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA1-19.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Circle with perpendicular chords AC and BD meeting at X, with AX = 2, XC = 6, BX = 3 and XD = 4, the dashed perpendicular bisectors x = 2 and y = -1/2 meeting at the centre, and a radius r.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Triangles $AXB$ and $DXC$ are similar (angles in the same segment), so $AX\\times XC = BX\\times XD$, giving $XD = \\tfrac{12}{3} = 4$.<\/p><p>Now use coordinates with $X$ at the origin: $A = (-2, 0)$, $C = (6, 0)$, $B = (0, 3)$, $D = (0, -4)$. The centre lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AC$, the line $x = 2$, and on that of $BD$, the line $y = -\\tfrac12$. So the centre is $\\left(2, -\\tfrac12\\right)$ and<\/p>\\[r^2 = (6-2)^2 + \\left(\\tfrac12\\right)^2 = \\tfrac{65}{4}, \\qquad r = \\tfrac{\\sqrt{65}}{2}.\\]<p>This is just over $4$, which is the tempting answer if you assume $AC$ is a diameter.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA1-20", "paper": "EA1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>A sealed hollow cone of height $12\\text{ cm}$ contains some water. When the cone stands on its circular base, with its vertex pointing up, the water is $6\\text{ cm}$ deep. The cone is turned over so that it balances with its vertex pointing down and its base horizontal. How deep, in cm, is the water now? (Ignore the thickness of the walls.)<\/p>", "opts": ["$3\\sqrt[3]{7}$", "$6\\sqrt[3]{7}$", "$6$", "$3\\sqrt{14}$", "$6\\sqrt[3]{4}$", "$10.5$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA1-20.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Two views of the cone of height 12: vertex up with water 6 deep and air filling the top cone of height 6, and vertex down with the water forming a cone of depth d.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>With the vertex up, the air fills a small cone of height $6$, which is similar to the whole cone with scale factor $\\tfrac12$, so it has $\\tfrac18$ of the volume. The water is therefore $\\tfrac78$ of the cone.<\/p><p>With the vertex down, the water forms a cone of depth $d$ similar to the whole cone, so $\\left(\\dfrac{d}{12}\\right)^3 = \\dfrac78$ and $d = 12\\times\\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{7}}{2} = 6\\sqrt[3]{7}\\approx 11.5$. Thinking the water fills half the volume gives $6\\sqrt[3]{4}$; using the scale factor linearly gives $10.5$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA1-21", "paper": "EA1", "n": 21, "q": "<p>An escalator moves downwards at a constant speed. Ria walks down it, from top to bottom, while it is moving. When she walks at $1$ step per second she takes $30$ steps to reach the bottom. When she walks at $3$ steps per second she takes $45$ steps. How many steps would she take to walk down the escalator if it were switched off?<\/p>", "opts": ["$45$", "$90$", "$60$", "$75$", "$50$", "$40$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let the escalator have $N$ visible steps and move at $e$ steps per second. At $1$ step per second Ria walks for $30$ seconds, while the escalator carries her $30e$ steps, so $N = 30 + 30e$. At $3$ steps per second she walks for $15$ seconds, so $N = 45 + 15e$.<\/p><p>Equating, $15e = 15$, so $e = 1$ and $N = 60$. The common error is to treat the two trips as taking the same time.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio", "algebra"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EA1-22", "paper": "EA1", "n": 22, "q": "<p>How many ordered pairs $(x, y)$ of integers satisfy<\/p>\\[xy = 4x + 6y\\,?\\]", "opts": ["$4$", "$8$", "$12$", "$15$", "$24$", "$16$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Rearrange and factorise: $xy - 4x - 6y + 24 = 24$, so<\/p>\\[(x - 6)(y - 4) = 24.\\]<p>Every way of writing $24$ as a product of two integers $d\\times \\tfrac{24}{d}$ gives exactly one pair, with $x = 6 + d$. The number $24$ has $8$ positive divisors, and $d$ may also be negative, so there are $16$ pairs (including $(0,0)$). Counting only positive divisors gives $8$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra", "number"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EA1-23", "paper": "EA1", "n": 23, "q": "<p>Asha and Bilal take turns to roll a fair six-sided die, with Asha going first. Asha wins if she rolls a $6$ on her turn; Bilal wins if he rolls a $5$ or a $6$ on his turn. They keep going until someone wins. What is the probability that Asha wins?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{6}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let $p$ be the probability that Asha wins. A full round with no winner happens with probability $\\tfrac56\\times\\tfrac46 = \\tfrac59$, after which the game is in its starting position again. So<\/p>\\[p = \\frac16 + \\frac59 p \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac49 p = \\frac16 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p = \\frac38.\\]<p>Going first does not make up for Bilal's doubled chance on each turn. The option $\\tfrac{6}{11}$ is the answer when both need a $6$; $\\tfrac13$ is Asha's share of the single roll chances $\\tfrac16 : \\tfrac26$, which ignores the turn order.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EA1-24", "paper": "EA1", "n": 24, "q": "<p>Triangle $ABC$ has $AB = 13$, $BC = 14$ and $CA = 15$. The bisector of angle $BAC$ meets $BC$ at $E$, and $M$ is the midpoint of $BC$. What is the area of triangle $AEM$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$3$", "$6$", "$\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "$12$", "$\\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$9$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA1-24.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Triangle ABC with AB = 13, BC = 14, CA = 15, the altitude AD = 12, the angle bisector AE and the median AM, with the thin triangle AEM shaded.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Drop the perpendicular $AD$ to $BC$ and let $BD = p$. Then $13^2 - p^2 = 15^2 - (14-p)^2$, giving $p = 5$ and $AD = 12$.<\/p><p>The angle bisector theorem gives $BE : EC = AB : AC = 13 : 15$, so $BE = \\tfrac{13}{28}\\times 14 = 6.5$, while $BM = 7$. Hence $EM = 0.5$, and triangle $AEM$ has base $EM$ on $BC$ and height $AD$:<\/p>\\[\\text{area} = \\tfrac12\\times 0.5\\times 12 = 3.\\]<p>The option $9$ is the area of triangle $ADE$ (with $DE = 1.5$), and $12$ is the area of triangle $ADM$ (with $DM = 2$).<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EA1-25", "paper": "EA1", "n": 25, "q": "<p>$ABCD$ is a square of side $12$, labelled in order. $E$ is the midpoint of $BC$ and $F$ is the midpoint of $CD$. The line segments $AE$ and $BF$ meet at $P$. What is the area of the quadrilateral $APFD$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$76$", "$72$", "$84$", "$\\dfrac{384}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{432}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{396}{5}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA1-25.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Square ABCD of side 12 with E the midpoint of BC and F the midpoint of CD; AE and BF meet at right angles at P, the quadrilateral APFD is shaded blue and triangle BPE is shaded orange.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>$APFD$ is what is left of the square after removing the union of triangles $ABE$ and $BCF$. Each has area $\\tfrac12\\times 12\\times 6 = 36$, and they overlap in triangle $BPE$.<\/p><p>Triangles $ABE$ and $BCF$ are congruent, and rotating one onto the other by $90^\\circ$ shows $AE\\perp BF$. So $BPE$ is right-angled at $P$ and similar to $ABE$ (which is right-angled at $B$), with scale factor $\\dfrac{BE}{AE} = \\dfrac{6}{6\\sqrt5} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}$. Its area is $\\dfrac{36}{5}$.<\/p>\\[\\text{area of } APFD = 144 - \\left(36 + 36 - \\frac{36}{5}\\right) = 72 + \\frac{36}{5} = \\frac{396}{5}.\\]<p>Forgetting the overlap gives $72$; adding it twice gives $\\tfrac{432}{5}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EA1-26", "paper": "EA1", "n": 26, "q": "<p>A right-angled triangle has hypotenuse of length $10$, and the circle inscribed in it has radius $1$. What is the area of the triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$11$", "$\\dfrac{21}{2}$", "$22$", "$10$", "$12$", "$24$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA1-26.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Right-angled triangle with legs a and b and its incircle of radius 1; the tangent lengths are 1 from the right angle, a - 1 and b - 1 from the other two vertices.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Let the legs be $a$ and $b$. The incircle touches the two legs at distance $r = 1$ from the right-angled vertex (the centre and the two touching points form a square), and the two tangents from each other vertex are equal. So the hypotenuse is $(a - 1) + (b - 1)$, giving $a + b = 12$.<\/p><p>Then $2ab = (a+b)^2 - (a^2 + b^2) = 144 - 100 = 44$, so the area is $\\tfrac12 ab = 11$. (The legs are $6\\pm\\sqrt{14}$, so such a triangle exists.) Guessing the familiar $6, 8, 10$ triangle gives $24$, but that triangle has inradius $2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EA1-27", "paper": "EA1", "n": 27, "q": "<p>Two circles, of radii $4$ and $9$, touch each other externally, and both touch a straight line $\\ell$, on the same side of it. A third circle, smaller than both, fits in the gap enclosed by the two circles and $\\ell$: it touches $\\ell$ and touches each of the other two circles externally. What is the radius of the third circle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{36}{13}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{13}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{36}{25}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{6}{5}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA1-27.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Circles of radii 4 and 9 touching each other and the line l, with the small circle of radius x in the gap; the centres are joined, the horizontal distance 12 is marked, and the touching points on l are 4 root x and 6 root x apart.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>First find the distance between the points where two touching circles of radii $R$ and $r$ meet a common line. Their centres are $R + r$ apart and at heights $R$ and $r$, so by Pythagoras the horizontal distance is $\\sqrt{(R+r)^2 - (R-r)^2} = 2\\sqrt{Rr}$.<\/p><p>The touching points of the circles of radii $4$ and $9$ are $2\\sqrt{36} = 12$ apart. If the small circle has radius $x$, its touching point lies between them, $2\\sqrt{4x} = 4\\sqrt{x}$ from the first and $2\\sqrt{9x} = 6\\sqrt{x}$ from the second. So<\/p>\\[4\\sqrt{x} + 6\\sqrt{x} = 12 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt{x} = \\frac65 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\frac{36}{25}.\\]<p>Stopping at $\\sqrt{x}$ gives $\\tfrac65$; the option $\\tfrac{36}{13} = \\tfrac{4\\times 9}{4+9}$ is a guess at a harmonic mean.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "EA2-1", "paper": "EA2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>What is the coefficient of $x^5$ in the expansion of $(1 + x + x^2)^6(1 - x)^4$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$6$", "$-12$", "$-4$", "$12$", "$-8$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Since $(1 + x + x^2)(1 - x) = 1 - x^3$, the expression is $(1 - x^3)^4(1 + x + x^2)^2 = \\left(1 - 4x^3 + 6x^6 - \\dots\\right)\\left(1 + 2x + 3x^2 + 2x^3 + x^4\\right)$. The second bracket has no $x^5$ term, so the only contribution is $(-4x^3)(3x^2)$, giving $-12$. Taking the $x^2$ coefficient of $(1 + x + x^2)^2$ to be $2$ gives $-8$, and forgetting the square altogether gives $-4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA2-2", "paper": "EA2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>How many integers $x$ satisfy \\[\\dfrac{x^2 - 5x + 4}{x^2 - 9} \\le \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$11$", "$6$", "$5$", "$10$", "$9$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Do not multiply by $x^2 - 9$, whose sign varies. Subtracting $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ gives $\\dfrac{x^2 - 10x + 17}{2(x-3)(x+3)} \\le 0$. The numerator has roots $5 \\pm 2\\sqrt{2}$, roughly $2.17$ and $7.83$. For $-3 &lt; x &lt; 3$ the denominator is negative, so we need the numerator $\\ge 0$: $-3 &lt; x \\le 5 - 2\\sqrt{2}$. For $x > 3$ we need the numerator $\\le 0$: $3 &lt; x \\le 5 + 2\\sqrt{2}$. For $x &lt; -3$ both are positive. The integers are $-2, -1, 0, 1, 2$ and $4, 5, 6, 7$, nine in all. Multiplying through gives $5 - 2\\sqrt{2} \\le x \\le 5 + 2\\sqrt{2}$, which leads to $5$ (or $4$ after removing $x = 3$).<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA2-3", "paper": "EA2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>Water is poured at a constant rate into an empty container in the shape of an inverted right circular cone, with its vertex at the bottom and its axis vertical. When the depth of the water is $4\\text{ cm}$, the depth is increasing at $9\\text{ cm}$ per minute.<\/p><p>How long does it take for the depth to increase from $4\\text{ cm}$ to $8\\text{ cm}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{28}{27}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{7}{9}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{3}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{9}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{32}{45}\\text{ minutes}$", "$\\dfrac{16}{9}\\text{ minutes}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>By similarity the volume at depth $h$ is $V = ah^3$ for a constant $a$. The constant rate is $\\dfrac{dV}{dt} = 3ah^2\\dfrac{dh}{dt} = 3a(16)(9) = 432a$ per minute. The volume needed is $a(8^3 - 4^3) = 448a$, so the time is $\\dfrac{448}{432} = \\dfrac{28}{27}$ minutes. Assuming the level keeps rising at $9\\text{ cm}$ per minute gives $\\dfrac{4}{9}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "EA2-4", "paper": "EA2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A ball is dropped from rest from a height of $5\\text{ m}$ above a horizontal floor and first hits the floor after $1$ second. Each time it bounces, it rises to $\\dfrac{9}{16}$ of the height from which it last fell. The time taken to fall from rest through a height $h$ is proportional to $\\sqrt{h}$, and the time taken to rise to a height $h$ equals the time taken to fall from that height.<\/p><p>Ignoring the time spent in contact with the floor, what is the average speed of the ball from the moment it is dropped until it comes to rest?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{125}{28}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{80}{49}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{20}{7}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{125}{49}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$5\\text{ m s}^{-1}$", "$\\dfrac{25}{7}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Distance: $5 + 2\\left(5\\cdot\\dfrac{9}{16} + 5\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{9}{16}\\right)^2 + \\dots\\right) = 5 + 2\\cdot\\dfrac{45/16}{7/16} = 5 + \\dfrac{90}{7} = \\dfrac{125}{7}\\text{ m}$. Heights scale by $\\dfrac{9}{16}$, so times scale by $\\sqrt{9/16} = \\dfrac{3}{4}$, and the time is $1 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4} + \\dfrac{9}{16} + \\dots\\right) = 1 + 2\\cdot\\dfrac{3/4}{1/4} = 7\\text{ s}$. The average speed is $\\dfrac{125}{49}\\text{ m s}^{-1}$. Using the ratio $\\dfrac{9}{16}$ for the times as well gives a time of $\\dfrac{25}{7}\\text{ s}$ and the tempting answer $5$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-5", "paper": "EA2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Which of the following curves can be obtained from the curve $y = \\log_2 x$ by a single transformation, where the transformation is either one translation or one stretch parallel to a coordinate axis (with the other axis fixed and a positive scale factor)?<\/p><ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$y = \\log_2 (8x)$<\/li><li>$y = 3 + \\log_8 x$<\/li><li>$y = \\log_2 (x^2)$<\/li><\/ol>", "opts": ["II only", "III only", "II and III only", "I and II only", "none of them", "I only", "I, II and III", "I and III only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>I: $\\log_2(8x) = 3 + \\log_2 x$, a translation of $3$ units upwards (it is also a stretch parallel to the $x$-axis with factor $\\dfrac{1}{8}$). II: $y = 3 + \\dfrac{1}{3}\\log_2 x$. A stretch parallel to the $y$-axis keeps the point $(1,0)$, but II passes through $(1,3)$. A translation or a stretch parallel to the $x$-axis gives $y = \\log_2(x-a) + b$, with gradient $\\dfrac{1}{(x-a)\\ln 2}$, which cannot equal the gradient $\\dfrac{1}{3x\\ln 2}$ of II for every $x$. So II needs two transformations. III: $\\log_2(x^2) = 2\\log_2|x|$ is defined for every $x \\neq 0$ and has two branches, while any translation or stretch of $y = \\log_2 x$ is defined only on a half-line. It agrees with the stretch $y = 2\\log_2 x$ only for $x > 0$. So only I works.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "graphs"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-6", "paper": "EA2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>What is the sum of all real solutions of the equation \\[\\log_{x-1}(x^2 - 5x + 7) + 2\\log_{x^2 - 5x + 7}(x - 1) = 3\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$8$", "$4$", "$6$", "$11$", "There are no real solutions."], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Both bases must be positive and not equal to $1$. As $x^2 - 5x + 7 = \\left(x - \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)^2 + \\dfrac{3}{4} > 0$, this means $x > 1$, $x \\neq 2$ and $x \\neq 3$. Writing $L = \\log_{x-1}(x^2 - 5x + 7)$, the second term is $\\dfrac{2}{L}$, so $L^2 - 3L + 2 = 0$ and $L = 1$ or $L = 2$. $L = 1$ gives $x^2 - 5x + 7 = x - 1$, so $x = 2$ or $x = 4$, and only $x = 4$ is allowed (check: $\\log_3 3 + 2\\log_3 3 = 3$). $L = 2$ gives $x^2 - 5x + 7 = (x-1)^2$, so $x = 2$, which is not allowed. The only solution is $x = 4$. Forgetting that a base cannot be $1$ gives $6$ or $8$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-7", "paper": "EA2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>For which real values of $k$ does the equation \\[\\dfrac{x^2 - 4}{x^2 - 1} = k\\] have exactly two distinct real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$k \\le 1$ or $k > 4$", "$1 &lt; k &lt; 4$", "$k &lt; 1$ or $k > 4$", "$k &lt; 1$ or $k \\ge 4$", "$k > 4$", "$k &lt; 1$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Rearranging, $x^2(1 - k) = 4 - k$. For $k = 1$ there is no solution. Otherwise $x^2 = \\dfrac{4-k}{1-k}$, which is never equal to $1$, so the excluded values $x = \\pm 1$ never occur. A positive value of $x^2$ gives two solutions, zero gives one and a negative value gives none. The fraction is positive for $k &lt; 1$ or $k > 4$, zero for $k = 4$ (only $x = 0$) and negative for $1 &lt; k &lt; 4$. So there are exactly two solutions when $k &lt; 1$ or $k > 4$. The value $k = 4$ is the local maximum of the curve, touched at the single point $x = 0$.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs", "alg"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-8", "paper": "EA2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = x^2 - 6x + 10$ for $x \\ge 3$, and $f^{-1}$ is its inverse function.<\/p><p>What is the sum of all real solutions of the equation $f(x) = f^{-1}(x)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$7$", "$\\dfrac{15 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}$", "$2$", "$12$", "$\\dfrac{5 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$f$ is increasing on its domain. If $f(x) = f^{-1}(x) = y$ then $f(y) = x$; if $x &lt; y$ then $f(x) &lt; f(y)$, that is $y &lt; x$, a contradiction, and $x > y$ fails in the same way. So $y = x$ and $f(x) = x$, giving $x^2 - 7x + 10 = 0$, so $x = 2$ or $x = 5$, and only $x = 5$ lies in the domain. Solving $f(f(x)) = x$ instead also produces the roots of $x^2 - 5x + 5 = 0$; the root $\\dfrac{5+\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\approx 3.6$ is in the domain, but there $f(x) = \\dfrac{5-\\sqrt{5}}{2}$, which is not $f^{-1}(x)$ because $f^{-1}$ only takes values $\\ge 3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-9", "paper": "EA2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>How many values of $x$ in the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi$ satisfy $\\sin 5x = \\sin x$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$11$", "$10$", "$7$", "$5$", "$9$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$\\sin 5x = \\sin x$ means $5x = x + 2k\\pi$ or $5x = \\pi - x + 2k\\pi$. The first gives $x = \\dfrac{k\\pi}{2}$: five values $0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}, 2\\pi$. The second gives $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + \\dfrac{k\\pi}{3}$: six values $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}$. The values $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ appear in both lists, so there are $5 + 6 - 2 = 9$ solutions. Missing the overlap gives $11$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-10", "paper": "EA2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>The curve $y = x^2 - 2x$ and the line $y = mx$ enclose a finite region of area $36$. What is the sum of all possible values of the constant $m$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$-4$", "$-8$", "$0$", "$4$", "$12$", "$-2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA2-10.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Parabola y = x^2 - 2x with the lines y = 4x and y = -8x, meeting it at (6, 24) and (-6, 48); the two enclosed regions are shaded.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>They meet where $x^2 - (m+2)x = 0$, at $x = 0$ and $x = m + 2$. The area between $y = mx$ and the parabola is $\\left|\\displaystyle\\int_0^{m+2} \\left((m+2)x - x^2\\right) dx\\right| = \\dfrac{|m+2|^3}{6}$. Setting this equal to $36$ gives $|m + 2| = 6$, so $m = 4$ or $m = -8$, with sum $-4$. The negative value is easy to miss, as is the shift by $2$ (which gives $m = \\pm 6$ and sum $0$).<\/p>", "tags": ["int"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "EA2-11", "paper": "EA2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>For a real constant $c$, the shortest distance from the point $(0, c)$ to the curve $y = x^2$ is exactly $1$. What is the sum of all possible values of $c$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$-1$", "$1$", "$0$", "$\\dfrac{5}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{4}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA2-11.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Parabola y = x^2 with two unit circles: one centred at (0, 5/4) touching the parabola at two symmetric points, and one centred at (0, -1) touching it at the origin.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The squared distance to $(x, x^2)$ is $x^2 + (x^2 - c)^2 = u^2 + (1 - 2c)u + c^2$, where $u = x^2 \\ge 0$. If $c \\le \\dfrac{1}{2}$ this increases for $u \\ge 0$, so the nearest point is the origin and the distance is $|c|$; with $c \\le \\dfrac{1}{2}$ this gives only $c = -1$. If $c > \\dfrac{1}{2}$ the minimum is at $u = c - \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and equals $c^2 - \\dfrac{(1 - 2c)^2}{4} = c - \\dfrac{1}{4}$, so $c = \\dfrac{5}{4}$. The sum is $\\dfrac{1}{4}$. The tempting $c = 1$ fails: from $(0,1)$ the nearest points are $\\left(\\pm\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$, at distance $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "coord"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA2-12", "paper": "EA2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>The points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(0,0)$ and $(3,0)$. A point $P$, not on the $x$-axis, moves so that $PA = 2PB$.<\/p><p>What is the greatest possible area of triangle $PAB$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$", "$3$", "$\\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "$4$", "$2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA2-12.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Points A(0,0) and B(3,0), the circle of points with PA = 2PB centred at (4,0) with radius 2, and the shaded triangle PAB with P at (4,2), base 3 and height 2.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>With $P(x,y)$, $x^2 + y^2 = 4\\left((x-3)^2 + y^2\\right)$ simplifies to $x^2 + y^2 - 8x + 12 = 0$, that is $(x-4)^2 + y^2 = 4$, a circle of radius $2$. The base $AB = 3$ is fixed, and the height $|y|$ is at most $2$, reached at $(4, \\pm 2)$. The greatest area is $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\times 3\\times 2 = 3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EA2-13", "paper": "EA2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Let $a = \\log_2 3$, $b = \\log_3 5$ and $c = \\log_5 8$. Which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$c > b > a$", "$b > a > c$", "$a > b > c$", "$c > a > b$", "$a > c > b$", "$b > c > a$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Compare each with simple fractions. Since $3^2 > 2^3$, $a > \\dfrac{3}{2}$; since $5^2 &lt; 3^3$, $b &lt; \\dfrac{3}{2}$. So $a > b$. Next, $5^3 = 125 > 81 = 3^4$, so $b > \\dfrac{4}{3}$, while $8^3 = 512 &lt; 625 = 5^4$, so $c &lt; \\dfrac{4}{3}$. Hence $a > b > c$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA2-14", "paper": "EA2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>What is the smallest positive integer $n$ for which \\[\\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\log_2\\left(\\dfrac{k+2}{k}\\right) > 10\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$44$", "$63$", "$1023$", "$31$", "$45$", "$43$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The sum is $\\log_2$ of the product $\\dfrac{3}{1}\\cdot\\dfrac{4}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{5}{3}\\cdots\\dfrac{n+2}{n}$. The numerators are $3, 4, \\dots, n+2$ and the denominators are $1, 2, \\dots, n$, so everything cancels except $\\dfrac{(n+1)(n+2)}{1\\times 2}$. We need $(n+1)(n+2) > 2^{11} = 2048$. Since $44\\times 45 = 1980$ and $45\\times 46 = 2070$, the answer is $n = 44$. Cancelling as if adjacent terms telescoped leaves $n + 2$ and gives $1023$; forgetting the $2$ gives $31$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq", "explog"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA2-15", "paper": "EA2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The curve $y = x^4 - 4x^3 + ax^2$, where $a$ is a real constant, has exactly one stationary point. Which of the following describes all possible values of $a$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$a &lt; \\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$a \\ge \\dfrac{9}{2}$", "$a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$ or $a = 0$", "$a \\ge \\dfrac{9}{2}$ or $a = 0$", "$a > 9$", "$a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 4x^3 - 12x^2 + 2ax = 2x(2x^2 - 6x + a)$, so $x = 0$ is always a stationary point. There must be no other, so $2x^2 - 6x + a = 0$ must have no root other than $0$. If $36 - 8a &lt; 0$, that is $a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$, it has no roots. If $a = \\dfrac{9}{2}$ it has the repeated root $x = \\dfrac{3}{2}$, which is a stationary point of inflexion, still a stationary point, so there are two. If $a = 0$ the roots are $0$ and $3$, again two stationary points. So $a > \\dfrac{9}{2}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EA2-16", "paper": "EA2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>How many pairs $(n, x)$, where $n$ is an integer and $0 \\le x \\le \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$, satisfy \\[3\\sin x + 4\\cos x = n\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$14$", "$11$", "$12$", "$15$", "$13$", "$16$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Write $3\\sin x + 4\\cos x = 5\\sin(x + \\alpha)$, where $\\alpha$ is acute with $\\sin\\alpha = \\dfrac{4}{5}$. As $x$ runs from $0$ to $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$, $x + \\alpha$ runs from $\\alpha$ to $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} + \\alpha$, so the value starts at $4$, rises to $5$, falls to $-5$ (when $x + \\alpha = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$), then rises to $-3$ at $x = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$. Counting: $n = 5$ once; $n = 4$ twice ($x = 0$ and once on the way down); each of $3, 2, \\dots, -4$ once on the way down, $8$ pairs; $n = -5$ once; then $-4$ and $-3$ again on the way up. The total is $1 + 2 + 8 + 1 + 2 = 14$. Counting each attainable integer once gives $11$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EA2-17", "paper": "EA2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>What is the total area of the regions enclosed between the curves $y = \\sin x$ and $y = \\sin 2x$ for $0 \\le x \\le \\pi$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{9}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$3$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac{7}{2}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA2-17.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Graphs of y = sin x and y = sin 2x for x from 0 to pi, meeting at pi/3, with the region of area 1/4 between 0 and pi/3 and the region of area 9/4 between pi/3 and pi shaded.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The curves meet where $\\sin x(2\\cos x - 1) = 0$, at $x = 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\pi$. On $\\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right]$, $\\sin 2x$ is on top: $\\left[-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos 2x + \\cos x\\right]_0^{\\pi/3} = \\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}$. On $\\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\pi\\right]$, $\\sin x$ is on top: $\\left[-\\cos x + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\right]_{\\pi/3}^{\\pi} = \\dfrac{3}{2} + \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}$. The total is $\\dfrac{5}{2}$. Integrating $\\sin x - \\sin 2x$ over the whole interval gives $2$, because the small region is counted as negative.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "trig"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EA2-18", "paper": "EA2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Consider the equation $x^2 - kx + k + 3 = 0$, where $k$ is an integer.<\/p><p>What is the sum of all values of $k$ for which the equation has two integer roots? (The two roots are allowed to be equal.)<\/p>", "opts": ["$11$", "$10$", "$7$", "$4$", "$13$", "$8$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>If the roots are $r$ and $s$, then $r + s = k$ and $rs = k + 3$, so $rs - r - s = 3$, that is $(r-1)(s-1) = 4$. The factorisations $1\\times 4$, $2\\times 2$, $(-1)\\times(-4)$, $(-2)\\times(-2)$ give root pairs $\\{2,5\\}$, $\\{3,3\\}$, $\\{0,-3\\}$, $\\{-1,-1\\}$, and so $k = 7, 6, -3, -2$. The sum is $8$. Forgetting negative factors gives $13$; forgetting repeated roots gives $4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA2-19", "paper": "EA2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Some circles touch both of the lines $y = 0$ and $3y = 4x$, and also pass through the point $(8, 1)$. What is the sum of the radii of all such circles?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{17}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{104}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{17}{2}$", "$9$", "$17$", "$\\dfrac{65}{4}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA2-19.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The lines y = 0 and 3y = 4x with their dashed angle bisector, and the two circles centred on it at (2r, r) that touch both lines and pass through the point (8, 1).\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The point $(8,1)$ lies inside the angle between the positive $x$-axis and the ray along $(3,4)$. A circle touching both lines cannot cross either of them, so it lies inside this angle and its centre is on the angle bisector. Adding unit vectors, $(1,0) + \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{5}\\right) = \\left(\\dfrac{8}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)$, so the bisector has direction $(2,1)$ and a circle of radius $r$ has centre $(2r, r)$ (check: its distance from $4x - 3y = 0$ is $\\dfrac{|8r - 3r|}{5} = r$). Passing through $(8,1)$ gives $(8 - 2r)^2 + (1 - r)^2 = r^2$, that is $4r^2 - 34r + 65 = 0$. The discriminant $34^2 - 16\\times 65 = 116$ is positive and both roots are positive, so the sum of the radii is $\\dfrac{34}{4} = \\dfrac{17}{2}$. Taking the bisector to have the average gradient $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives $\\dfrac{104}{9}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "trig"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA2-20", "paper": "EA2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>What is the sum of all real solutions of the equation $\\sqrt{x + 6} = x^2 - 6$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{5 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$", "$3$", "$0$", "$\\dfrac{5 + \\sqrt{21}}{2}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{-1 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let $y = \\sqrt{x + 6} \\ge 0$. Then $y^2 = x + 6$ and $y = x^2 - 6$; subtracting, $y^2 - x^2 = x - y$, so $(y - x)(y + x + 1) = 0$. If $y = x$, then $x^2 - x - 6 = 0$, so $x = 3$ or $x = -2$, and $y = x \\ge 0$ leaves $x = 3$. If $y = -1 - x$, then $x^2 + x - 5 = 0$, so $x = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{21}}{2}$, and $y \\ge 0$ needs $x \\le -1$, leaving $x = \\dfrac{-1 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$. The sum is $\\dfrac{5 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}$. Squaring gives the quartic $x^4 - 12x^2 - x + 30 = 0$; all four of its roots are real and add to $0$, but two of them are extraneous.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EA2-21", "paper": "EA2", "n": 21, "q": "<p>Let $f(x) = x^3 - 3x$. How many distinct real solutions does the equation $f(f(x)) = 2$ have?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$6$", "$7$", "$3$", "$5$", "$9$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Let $u = f(x)$. Then $u^3 - 3u - 2 = (u+1)^2(u-2) = 0$, so $u = -1$ or $u = 2$. The graph of $f$ has a local maximum at $(-1, 2)$ and a local minimum at $(1, -2)$. Since $-2 &lt; -1 &lt; 2$, the line $y = -1$ meets the graph three times. The line $y = 2$ touches the graph at the maximum $x = -1$ and crosses it once more at $x = 2$, giving only two distinct solutions. The total is $3 + 2 = 5$. Assuming every horizontal line between turning values gives three solutions leads to $6$, and counting the degree gives $9$.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA2-22", "paper": "EA2", "n": 22, "q": "<p>The line $y=mx$, where $m>0$, meets the curve $y=x^3-6x^2+9x$ at three distinct points, and the line and the curve enclose two regions of equal area. Find $m$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac94$", "$3$", "$4$", "$\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA2-22.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Cubic y = x^3 - 6x^2 + 9x and the line y = x, meeting at O, (2,2) and (4,4), with the two equal shaded regions either side of (2,2).\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>No integration is needed. Every cubic is symmetric under a half turn about its point of inflection. Here $y''=6x-12=0$ at $x=2$, where $y=8-24+18=2$, so the half turn about $(2,2)$ maps the curve to itself. It also maps any line through $(2,2)$ to itself, so such a line cuts off two congruent regions, one on each side of $(2,2)$. The line through the origin and $(2,2)$ is $y=x$, so $m=1$. Check: $x^3-6x^2+9x=x$ gives $x(x-2)(x-4)=0$, three distinct points placed symmetrically about $x=2$. It is the only such line: for $0&lt;m&lt;9$ the other intersections are $x=3\\pm\\sqrt m$, and making the signed area from $0$ to $b=3+\\sqrt m$ vanish gives $b^2-8b+18-2m=0$, which with $m=(b-3)^2$ becomes $b(b-4)=0$, so $b=4$ and $m=1$; for $m>9$ the region to the left of the origin is always much the smaller. Working out one area and halving, or using the tangent gradient at the origin ($9$), leads to the wrong options.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "graphs"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EA2-23", "paper": "EA2", "n": 23, "q": "<p>What is the sum of all values of $x$ in the interval $0 \\le x \\le \\pi$ that satisfy \\[\\sin x + \\sin 2x + \\sin 3x = \\cos x + \\cos 2x + \\cos 3x\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{17\\pi}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{19\\pi}{24}$", "$\\dfrac{61\\pi}{24}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Expanding $\\sin(2x \\pm x)$ and $\\cos(2x \\pm x)$ gives $\\sin x + \\sin 3x = 2\\sin 2x\\cos x$ and $\\cos x + \\cos 3x = 2\\cos 2x\\cos x$. So the equation is $(1 + 2\\cos x)(\\sin 2x - \\cos 2x) = 0$. Either $\\cos x = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$, giving $x = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$, or $\\tan 2x = 1$ with $0 \\le 2x \\le 2\\pi$, giving $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}$. The sum is $\\dfrac{17\\pi}{12}$. Cancelling the common factor $1 + 2\\cos x$ loses $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ and gives $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EA2-24", "paper": "EA2", "n": 24, "q": "<p>What is the value of \\[\\binom{10}{0} + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\binom{10}{1} + \\dfrac{1}{3}\\binom{10}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\binom{10}{3} + \\dots + \\dfrac{1}{11}\\binom{10}{10}\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1024}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{1023}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{2047}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{2048}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{2047}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{1023}{10}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Integrate the binomial expansion $(1+x)^{10} = \\displaystyle\\sum_{k=0}^{10} \\binom{10}{k}x^k$ from $0$ to $1$. Each term gives $\\dfrac{1}{k+1}\\binom{10}{k}$, so the sum is $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 (1+x)^{10}\\,dx = \\left[\\dfrac{(1+x)^{11}}{11}\\right]_0^1 = \\dfrac{2^{11} - 1}{11} = \\dfrac{2047}{11}$. Forgetting the lower limit gives $\\dfrac{2048}{11}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq", "int"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EA2-25", "paper": "EA2", "n": 25, "q": "<p>For which positive values of $k$ does the equation $e^x = kx^2$ have exactly three real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0 &lt; k &lt; \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$", "$k > \\dfrac{e}{2}$", "$k \\ge \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$", "$k > \\dfrac{e^2}{2}$", "$k > \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$", "$k > e$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Note $x = 0$ is never a solution. For $x &lt; 0$, $g(x) = e^x - kx^2$ has $g'(x) = e^x - 2kx > 0$, with $g(0) = 1 > 0$ and $g(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$, so there is exactly one negative solution for every $k > 0$. For $x > 0$, the equation is $h(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2} = k$, and $h'(x) = \\dfrac{e^x(x - 2)}{x^3}$. So $h$ falls from $+\\infty$ to its minimum $h(2) = \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ and then rises to $+\\infty$, giving two positive solutions when $k > \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ and one when $k = \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$. Exactly three solutions occur for $k > \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$. The tempting $k \\ge \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ fails because at $k = \\dfrac{e^2}{4}$ the curves only touch at $x = 2$, giving two solutions in total.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "graphs"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EA2-26", "paper": "EA2", "n": 26, "q": "<p>The circles $x^2 + y^2 = 1$ and $(x-4)^2 + y^2 = 4$ have exactly four common tangents. What is the product of the gradients of these four lines?<\/p>", "opts": ["$-\\dfrac{3}{35}$", "$-\\dfrac{9}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{35}$", "$-\\dfrac{1}{15}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{105}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{35}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EA2-26.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Circles of radius 1 centred at O and radius 2 centred at (4,0) with their four common tangents, labelled with gradients 1/root 15, -1/root 15, 3/root 7 and -3/root 7.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>No vertical line touches both circles, so each common tangent is $y = mx + c$. Tangency to the first circle gives $c^2 = 1 + m^2$, and to the second gives $(4m + c)^2 = 4(1 + m^2) = 4c^2$. So $4m + c = \\pm 2c$, that is $c = 4m$ or $c = -\\dfrac{4m}{3}$. Then $16m^2 = 1 + m^2$ gives $m^2 = \\dfrac{1}{15}$, and $\\dfrac{16m^2}{9} = 1 + m^2$ gives $m^2 = \\dfrac{9}{7}$. The gradients are $\\pm\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}$ and $\\pm\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}$, with product $\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{15}\\right)\\left(-\\dfrac{9}{7}\\right) = \\dfrac{3}{35}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EA2-27", "paper": "EA2", "n": 27, "q": "<p>For how many integer values of $k$ do exactly three distinct tangents to the curve $y = x^3 - x$ pass through the point $(2, k)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$3$", "$9$", "$8$", "$7$", "$6$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The tangent at $x = t$ is $y = (3t^2 - 1)(x - t) + t^3 - t = (3t^2 - 1)x - 2t^3$. It passes through $(2,k)$ when $p(t) = 2t^3 - 6t^2 + 2 + k = 0$. A line cannot touch a cubic at two different points, so distinct values of $t$ give distinct tangents, and we need three distinct real roots. As $p'(t) = 6t(t - 2)$, this happens when the local maximum $p(0) = 2 + k$ is positive and the local minimum $p(2) = k - 6$ is negative, so $-2 &lt; k &lt; 6$. That is $7$ integers. At $k = -2$ and $k = 6$ (the point $(2,6)$ is on the curve) there are only two tangents.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "graphs"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "EB1-1", "paper": "EB1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>A surveyor fixes three beacons at the points $A(-1,4)$, $B(6,5)$ and $C(7,-2)$, with all distances measured in kilometres. A relay mast $M$ is to be placed so that it lies on the locus of points equidistant from $A$ and $B$, and also on the locus of points equidistant from $B$ and $C$. How far is the mast from the beacon $B$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4\\sqrt{2}\\text{ km}$", "$\\sqrt{29}\\text{ km}$", "$10\\text{ km}$", "$5\\sqrt{2}\\text{ km}$", "$5\\text{ km}$", "$\\sqrt{26}\\text{ km}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-1.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Axes with A(-1,4), B(6,5), C(7,-2) on a circle centred at M(3,1); AC is a diameter, angle ABC is marked as a right angle and MB has length 5.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>$M$ is equidistant from all three beacons, so it is the centre of the circle through $A$, $B$ and $C$. The gradient of $AB$ is $\\tfrac17$ and the gradient of $BC$ is $-7$; their product is $-1$, so $\\angle ABC=90^\\circ$. By the angle in a semicircle, $AC$ is a diameter, so $M$ is the midpoint $(3,1)$ of $AC$ and $MB=\\tfrac12AC=\\tfrac12\\sqrt{8^2+6^2}=5$ km. Solving the two perpendicular bisectors, $y=-7x+22$ and $x-7y=-4$, gives the same point, but the right angle saves the algebra. The option $10$ km is the diameter $AC$ itself.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "algebra"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EB1-2", "paper": "EB1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>A foundry makes an alloy by melting together two metals: metal $P$, of density $9\\text{ g/cm}^3$, and metal $Q$, of density $6\\text{ g/cm}^3$. Assume that when the metals are melted together their volumes simply add. An ingot of the alloy has mass $1.8\\text{ kg}$ and density $7.2\\text{ g/cm}^3$. More of metal $P$ is then melted into the ingot until the density of the new, larger ingot is $7.5\\text{ g/cm}^3$. What fraction of the mass of the new ingot is metal $P$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{3}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{16}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{5}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The original volume is $1800\\div7.2=250\\text{ cm}^3$. If it contains $v\\text{ cm}^3$ of $P$, then $9v+6(250-v)=1800$, so $v=100$: the ingot holds $900$ g of $P$ and $900$ g of $Q$. Adding $x\\text{ cm}^3$ of $P$ gives $\\dfrac{1800+9x}{250+x}=7.5$, so $1.5x=75$ and $x=50$, which is $450$ g. The new ingot contains $1350$ g of $P$ out of $2250$ g, a fraction $\\tfrac35$. The trap $\\tfrac12$ is the new <em>volume<\/em> fraction ($150$ out of $300\\text{ cm}^3$), and $\\tfrac25$ is the original volume fraction.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "ratio"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EB1-3", "paper": "EB1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>A garden is in the shape of a trapezium $ABCD$, in which the side $AB$ is parallel to the side $DC$. Two straight paths run along the diagonals $AC$ and $BD$, crossing at the point $X$, and they divide the garden into four triangular flower beds. The bed $AXD$ has area $6\\text{ m}^2$ and the bed $DXC$ has area $4\\text{ m}^2$. Ignoring the width of the paths, what is the total area of the garden?<\/p>", "opts": ["$36\\text{ m}^2$", "$24\\text{ m}^2$", "$22\\text{ m}^2$", "$30\\text{ m}^2$", "$25\\text{ m}^2$", "$20\\text{ m}^2$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-3.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Trapezium ABCD with AB parallel to DC and diagonals meeting at X; the four triangles are labelled with areas 6 (AXD), 4 (DXC), 6 (BXC) and 9 (AXB), the two given beds shaded.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Triangles $ABD$ and $ABC$ share the base $AB$ and have equal heights, so removing the common part $AXB$ shows that $BXC$ also has area $6$. Triangles $DXC$ and $BXC$ have the same height from $C$, so $DX:XB=4:6=2:3$. Triangles $DXC$ and $BXA$ are similar (alternate angles), with lengths in the ratio $2:3$, so $AXB$ has area $4\\times\\tfrac94=9$. The total is $4+6+6+9=25\\text{ m}^2$. Scaling the area linearly ($AXB=6$, total $22$) or assuming a fourth bed equal to $DXC$ (total $20$) are the traps.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio", "geometry"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EB1-4", "paper": "EB1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A cyclist rides along a measured course whose length is recorded as $0.40\\text{ km}$, correct to two significant figures. Her time is measured with a stopwatch that displays whole seconds only and works by truncation: it ignores any fraction of a second, so that, for example, a time of $50.8$ seconds is displayed as $50$. At the end of her ride the stopwatch displays $50$. What is the upper bound for her average speed over the course, in kilometres per hour?<\/p>", "opts": ["$29.52$", "$29.16$", "$32.4$", "$28.8$", "$8.1$", "$29.\\dot{4}\\dot{5}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Two significant figures in $0.40$ km means the nearest $0.01$ km, so the length $d$ satisfies $395\\le d&lt;405$ metres. Truncation means the time $t$ satisfies $50\\le t&lt;51$ seconds. The upper bound of the speed is $405\\div50=8.1\\text{ m/s}$, and $8.1\\times3.6=29.16\\text{ km/h}$. Treating the time as rounded, with lower bound $49.5$, gives $29.\\dot4\\dot5$; reading $0.40$ as one significant figure (so $d&lt;450$) gives $32.4$; and $8.1$ is the answer in metres per second.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "units"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EB1-5", "paper": "EB1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>A stone plinth has the shape of a frustum of a right pyramid with a square base: its lower face is a $2\\text{ m}$ by $2\\text{ m}$ square, its upper face is a $1\\text{ m}$ by $1\\text{ m}$ square parallel to it, and its perpendicular height is $0.9\\text{ m}$. The stone has density $2400\\text{ kg/m}^3$. The plinth stands on its lower face on level ground, and a bronze statue of mass $1200\\text{ kg}$ is fixed to its upper face. Taking the gravitational field strength to be $10\\text{ N/kg}$, what is the pressure exerted on the ground by the plinth and statue together?<\/p>", "opts": ["$12.6\\text{ kPa}$", "$15.6\\text{ kPa}$", "$15.15\\text{ kPa}$", "$62.4\\text{ kPa}$", "$16.5\\text{ kPa}$", "$24.6\\text{ kPa}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-5.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Side view of the plinth as a trapezium with base 2 m, top 1 m and height 0.9 m, its sloping sides extended (dashed) to an apex a further 0.9 m above the top face.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The figure shows a side view of the plinth with the sloping faces extended to the apex.<\/p><p>Extend the sloping faces to a complete pyramid. The side length halves, from $2$ m to $1$ m, over a height of $0.9$ m, so the apex is $1.8$ m above the ground and the missing top pyramid has height $0.9$ m. The volume is $\\tfrac13(4)(1.8)-\\tfrac13(1)(0.9)=2.4-0.3=2.1\\text{ m}^3$, a mass of $5040$ kg. The total mass is $6240$ kg, a weight of $62\\,400$ N, spread over $4\\text{ m}^2$: the pressure is $15\\,600$ Pa $=15.6$ kPa. Treating the frustum as a prism whose cross-section is the mean of the two faces ($16.5$) or the square of side $1.5$ m ($15.15$), or forgetting the statue ($12.6$), are the traps.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "geometry"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EB1-6", "paper": "EB1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>The lengths, $L$ cm, of $60$ leaves are summarised in a grouped frequency table: $8$ leaves have $0\\le L&lt;10$, $x$ leaves have $10\\le L&lt;20$, $20$ leaves have $20\\le L&lt;30$, $y$ leaves have $30\\le L&lt;40$, and $6$ leaves have $40\\le L&lt;60$. Using the midpoints of the classes, an estimate of the mean length is $24.5$ cm. The data are then drawn as a histogram, on which the bar for the class $40\\le L&lt;60$ has a height of $1.5$ cm. What is the height of the bar for the class $10\\le L&lt;20$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$3.5\\text{ cm}$", "$7\\text{ cm}$", "$10\\text{ cm}$", "$3\\text{ cm}$", "$14\\text{ cm}$", "$6\\text{ cm}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>First $x+y=26$, and using midpoints $8(5)+15x+20(25)+35y+6(50)=60\\times24.5=1470$, so $15x+35y=630$. Hence $x=14$ and $y=12$. On a histogram, height is proportional to frequency density. The last class has density $6\\div20=0.3$, drawn as $1.5$ cm, so each unit of density is $5$ cm. The class $10\\le L&lt;20$ has density $14\\div10=1.4$, so its bar is $7$ cm tall. Scaling frequencies instead of densities gives $3.5$ cm, and swapping $x$ and $y$ gives $6$ cm.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EB1-7", "paper": "EB1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>An isosceles trapezium $ABCD$ is inscribed in a circle of radius $13\\text{ cm}$ and centre $O$. Its parallel sides are $AB=24\\text{ cm}$ and $CD=10\\text{ cm}$, and the centre $O$ lies outside the trapezium. What is the area of the trapezium?<\/p>", "opts": ["$119\\text{ cm}^2$", "$170\\text{ cm}^2$", "$289\\text{ cm}^2$", "$84\\text{ cm}^2$", "$102\\text{ cm}^2$", "$238\\text{ cm}^2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-7.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Circle of radius 13 with centre O above the isosceles trapezium ABCD; the perpendicular from O meets AB at distance 5 and CD at distance 12, giving height 7, with radii OB and OC of length 13 and half-chords 12 and 5.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord, and for both parallel chords this perpendicular lies along the axis of symmetry. So $O$ is $\\sqrt{13^2-12^2}=5$ cm from $AB$ and $\\sqrt{13^2-5^2}=12$ cm from $CD$. If $O$ were inside the trapezium the height would be $5+12=17$ cm, giving the tempting $289$. Since $O$ is outside, both chords lie on the same side of $O$ and the height is $12-5=7$ cm, so the area is $\\tfrac12(24+10)(7)=119\\text{ cm}^2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EB1-8", "paper": "EB1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Which of the following correctly lists the five numbers \\[2^{150},\\qquad 3^{100},\\qquad 5^{60},\\qquad 7^{50},\\qquad 11^{40}\\] in increasing order?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2^{150}&lt;3^{100}&lt;5^{60}&lt;7^{50}&lt;11^{40}$", "$11^{40}&lt;5^{60}&lt;7^{50}&lt;3^{100}&lt;2^{150}$", "$5^{60}&lt;7^{50}&lt;11^{40}&lt;3^{100}&lt;2^{150}$", "$11^{40}&lt;7^{50}&lt;5^{60}&lt;2^{150}&lt;3^{100}$", "$11^{40}&lt;5^{60}&lt;7^{50}&lt;2^{150}&lt;3^{100}$", "$5^{60}&lt;11^{40}&lt;7^{50}&lt;2^{150}&lt;3^{100}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Every exponent is a multiple of $10$, so compare the tenth roots: $2^{15}=32768$, $3^{10}=59049$, $5^6=15625$, $7^5=16807$ and $11^4=14641$. Hence $11^{40}&lt;5^{60}&lt;7^{50}&lt;2^{150}&lt;3^{100}$. Ordering by base, or by exponent, fails, and the three smallest values are close enough that they have to be computed rather than estimated.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EB1-9", "paper": "EB1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A freight train travels from one depot to another. For the first half of the total journey <em>time<\/em> it travels at a constant $100\\text{ km/h}$. Of the distance that then remains, it covers the first half at a constant $60\\text{ km/h}$ and the second half at a constant $120\\text{ km/h}$. Ignoring the time taken to change speed, what is the average speed of the train for the whole journey?<\/p>", "opts": ["$85\\tfrac{5}{7}\\text{ km/h}$", "$95\\text{ km/h}$", "$90\\text{ km/h}$", "$80\\text{ km/h}$", "$93\\tfrac{1}{3}\\text{ km/h}$", "$100\\text{ km/h}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Over two equal distances $s$ at $60$ and $120$ km/h the time is $\\tfrac{s}{60}+\\tfrac{s}{120}=\\tfrac{s}{40}$, so the average speed for the second phase is $2s\\div\\tfrac{s}{40}=80$ km/h (the harmonic mean), not $90$. The two phases last equal times, so the overall average is the ordinary mean of $100$ and $80$, namely $90$ km/h. Using $90$ for the second phase gives $95$; averaging the three speeds gives $93\\tfrac13$; treating all three stretches as equal distances gives $85\\tfrac57$.<\/p>", "tags": ["units"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EB1-10", "paper": "EB1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>A screening test for a medical condition has a sensitivity of $90\\%$ (the probability that a person who has the condition tests positive) and a specificity of $95\\%$ (the probability that a person who does not have the condition tests negative). In a certain population, $2\\%$ of people have the condition. Everyone who tests positive is immediately tested a second time with the same test; for any given person, the results of the two tests are independent of each other, once it is known whether or not that person has the condition. A person chosen at random from the population tests positive on both occasions. What is the probability that this person has the condition?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{18}{67}$", "$\\dfrac{81}{100}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{81}{326}$", "$\\dfrac{162}{187}$", "$\\dfrac{324}{373}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Per $10\\,000$ people: $200$ have the condition and $200\\times0.9\\times0.9=162$ of them test positive twice; $9800$ do not, and $9800\\times0.05\\times0.05=24.5$ of them test positive twice. The required probability is $\\dfrac{162}{162+24.5}=\\dfrac{324}{373}$. Stopping after one test gives $\\tfrac{18}{67}$; forgetting the factor $0.98$ gives $\\tfrac{162}{187}$; and $\\tfrac{81}{100}$ is the probability of two positives given the condition, the reverse conditional.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EB1-11", "paper": "EB1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>A ship sails due east in a straight line from a buoy $A$ to a buoy $B$, which is $12\\text{ km}$ from $A$. From $A$, a lighthouse $L$ is on a bearing of $030^\\circ$. From $B$, the same lighthouse is on a bearing of $315^\\circ$. What is the shortest distance between the lighthouse and the ship's path from $A$ to $B$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6\\sqrt{3}\\text{ km}$", "$(18\\sqrt{2}-6\\sqrt{6})\\text{ km}$", "$(12\\sqrt{3}-12)\\text{ km}$", "$(6\\sqrt{3}-6)\\text{ km}$", "$4\\sqrt{3}\\text{ km}$", "$(18-6\\sqrt{3})\\text{ km}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-11.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Triangle ALB with AB = 12 km due east, north lines at A and B, the bearing angle 30 degrees at A, angles of 60 degrees at A and 45 degrees at B, and the perpendicular LH of length h to AB.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The bearing $030^\\circ$ makes $\\angle LAB=90^\\circ-30^\\circ=60^\\circ$, and from $B$ the direction back to $A$ is $270^\\circ$, so $\\angle LBA=315^\\circ-270^\\circ=45^\\circ$. Both angles are acute, so the foot $H$ of the perpendicular from $L$ lies between $A$ and $B$. With $LH=h$, $AH=\\dfrac{h}{\\tan60^\\circ}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt3}$ and $HB=h$, so $h\\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)=12$, giving $h=\\dfrac{12\\sqrt3}{\\sqrt3+1}=6\\sqrt3(\\sqrt3-1)=18-6\\sqrt3$ km. Using the bearing $30^\\circ$ itself as the angle at $A$ gives $6\\sqrt3-6$; $12\\sqrt3-12$ is the length $AL$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EB1-12", "paper": "EB1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>For each real number $k$, the parabola $C_k$ has equation \\[y=x^2-2kx+4k.\\] Consider the following statements.<\/p><ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>There is a point that lies on every one of the parabolas $C_k$.<\/li><li>As $k$ varies, the turning point of $C_k$ always lies on the curve $y=4x-x^2$.<\/li><li>No parabola $C_k$ has its turning point above the line $y=4$.<\/li><li>Every parabola $C_k$ meets the $x$-axis.<\/li><\/ol><p>Which of these statements are true?<\/p>", "opts": ["II and III only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "I and III only", "II only", "II, III and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "I and IV only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-12.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The parabolas C_0, C_1, C_2 and C_4 all passing through (2,4), with their turning points marked on the highlighted curve y = 4x - x^2, which lies below the dashed line y = 4.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Write $y=x^2+k(4-2x)$: at $x=2$ we get $y=4$ whatever the value of $k$, so every $C_k$ passes through $(2,4)$ and I is true. Completing the square, $y=(x-k)^2+4k-k^2$, so the turning point is $(k,\\,4k-k^2)$, which lies on $y=4x-x^2$: II is true. Its $y$-coordinate is $4-(k-2)^2\\le4$, so III is true. But for $0&lt;k&lt;4$ the minimum value $4k-k^2$ is positive; for example $C_2$ is $y=(x-2)^2+4$, which never meets the $x$-axis. So IV is false.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EB1-13", "paper": "EB1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>In a fairground game, three fair six-sided dice, each numbered $1$ to $6$, are rolled together and the three scores are multiplied. The player wins a prize if this product is a multiple of $10$. What is the probability of winning a prize?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{11}{36}$", "$\\dfrac{91}{216}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{637}{1728}$", "$\\dfrac{8}{27}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The product is a multiple of $10$ exactly when at least one die shows $5$ and at least one die shows an even number. For the complement: no $5$ has probability $\\tfrac{125}{216}$, no even number has probability $\\tfrac{27}{216}$, and both together (every die in $\\{1,3\\}$) has probability $\\tfrac{8}{216}$. So the answer is $1-\\tfrac{125}{216}-\\tfrac{27}{216}+\\tfrac{8}{216}=\\tfrac{72}{216}=\\tfrac13$. Forgetting to add back the overlap gives $\\tfrac{8}{27}$. Multiplying $P(\\text{some }5)=\\tfrac{91}{216}$ by $P(\\text{some even})=\\tfrac78$ gives $\\tfrac{637}{1728}$, but these events are not independent, since a die showing $5$ cannot also be even.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EB1-14", "paper": "EB1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A lampshade is the curved surface of a right circular frustum, open at both ends. The radii of its two circular ends are $6\\text{ cm}$ and $10\\text{ cm}$, and the perpendicular distance between the two ends is $3\\text{ cm}$. It is made, with no overlap and no waste, by bending a flat annular sector of card: the region between two arcs of concentric circles, cut off by two radii of the larger circle. What are the angle of the annular sector and the radius of its inner arc?<\/p>", "opts": ["$300^\\circ$ and $7.5\\text{ cm}$", "$288^\\circ$ and $7.5\\text{ cm}$", "$216^\\circ$ and $4.5\\text{ cm}$", "$240^\\circ$ and $9\\text{ cm}$", "$216^\\circ$ and $7.5\\text{ cm}$", "$288^\\circ$ and $6\\text{ cm}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-14.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Left: half of the cone's axial section, showing the frustum with radii 6 and 10, height 3 and slant 5, and the removed cone of slant 7.5. Right: the flat annular sector of angle 288 degrees with inner radius 7.5 and outer radius 12.5.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>In the figure, the left diagram is half of a vertical section through the axis of the completed cone and the right diagram is the flat piece of card.<\/p><p>The slant height of the frustum is $\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5$ cm. Complete the cone: its slant height $\\ell$ satisfies $\\dfrac{\\ell}{10}=\\dfrac{\\ell-5}{6}$, so $\\ell=12.5$, and the removed cone has slant height $7.5$. These slant heights are the outer and inner radii of the annular sector. The outer arc becomes the larger rim, of length $20\\pi$, so the angle is $\\dfrac{20\\pi}{2\\pi\\times12.5}\\times360^\\circ=288^\\circ$ (the inner arc checks: $\\tfrac{288}{360}\\times2\\pi\\times7.5=12\\pi$). Taking the inner radius to be the rim radius $6$ cm, or using $\\tfrac{6}{10}$ of $360^\\circ$, are the traps.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "ratio"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EB1-15", "paper": "EB1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>A water trough is a prism of length $3\\text{ m}$. Its cross-section is an isosceles trapezium with a horizontal base of width $40\\text{ cm}$ at the bottom, an open top of width $100\\text{ cm}$, and a depth of $60\\text{ cm}$. The trough stands on level ground and is initially empty. Water flows in at a constant rate of $15$ litres per minute. What is the depth of the water after $20$ minutes?<\/p>", "opts": ["$10\\text{ cm}$", "$20\\text{ cm}$", "$14\\tfrac{2}{7}\\text{ cm}$", "$24\\text{ cm}$", "$16\\text{ cm}$", "$25\\text{ cm}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-15.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Trapezium cross-section of the trough with base 40, top 100 and depth 60, with water shaded to depth h and the water surface of width 40 + h.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>In $20$ minutes $300$ litres, that is $300\\,000\\text{ cm}^3$, flows in; dividing by the length $300$ cm, the water's cross-section has area $1000\\text{ cm}^2$. The width increases by $60$ cm over a depth of $60$ cm, so at height $h$ above the base the width is $40+h$. The water's cross-section is a trapezium of area $\\tfrac12(40+40+h)h=1000$, so $h^2+80h-2000=0$, which factorises as $(h+100)(h-20)=0$, and $h=20$ cm. Treating the water as a rectangle of width $40$, $70$ or $100$ cm gives $25$, $14\\tfrac27$ or $10$ cm.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "algebra"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EB1-16", "paper": "EB1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>Fifteen different positive integers are arranged in increasing order. For this question the lower quartile is the $4$th value, the median is the $8$th value and the upper quartile is the $12$th value. The lower quartile is $10$, the median is $20$, the upper quartile is $30$, and the range is $40$. Let $G$ be the greatest possible value of the mean of the fifteen integers, and let $S$ be the smallest possible value. What is $G-S$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{28}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{64}{15}$", "$6$", "$\\dfrac{88}{15}$", "$\\dfrac{86}{15}$", "$\\dfrac{92}{15}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Let the smallest value be $a$, so the largest is $a+40$, and the three values below $10$ force $a\\le7$. To maximise, take $a=7$: the values are $7,8,9,10,17,18,19,20,27,28,29,30,45,46,47$, with total $360$. Note that raising $a$ also raises the largest value, so both ends push the same way. To minimise, take $a=1$: the values are $1,2,3,10,11,12,13,20,21,22,23,30,31,32,41$, with total $272$. So $G-S=\\dfrac{360-272}{15}=\\dfrac{88}{15}$. Fixing the extremes independently, for example keeping the largest value at $41$ when maximising, leads to the other options.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "EB1-17", "paper": "EB1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A bag contains at least $30$ and at most $40$ counters. Each counter is red or blue, and there are more red counters than blue counters. Two counters are taken out at random, without replacement. The probability that they are the same colour is exactly $\\tfrac12$. How many red counters are in the bag?<\/p>", "opts": ["$30$", "$18$", "$24$", "$21$", "$20$", "$15$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>With $r$ red and $b$ blue counters and $n=r+b$, the condition is $\\dfrac{r(r-1)+b(b-1)}{n(n-1)}=\\dfrac12$, so $2r^2+2b^2-2n=n^2-n=(r+b)^2-(r+b)$. This simplifies to $(r-b)^2=r+b$. So $n$ must be a perfect square, and the only square from $30$ to $40$ is $36$, giving $r-b=6$, $r+b=36$, so $r=21$. The option $15$ comes from $n=25$, which is outside the allowed range.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob", "algebra"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "EB1-18", "paper": "EB1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>A packaging company makes closed cuboid boxes whose three edge lengths, measured in centimetres, are all whole numbers, and whose volume is exactly $360\\text{ cm}^3$. Two boxes count as the same design if one is congruent to the other, that is, if they have the same three edge lengths in some order. How many different designs are possible?<\/p>", "opts": ["$30$", "$34$", "$36$", "$27$", "$180$", "$32$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Count triples $a\\le b\\le c$ with $abc=360=2^3\\times3^2\\times5$, listing by the smallest edge $a$. For $a=1$: $bc=360$, which has $24$ divisors, giving $12$ pairs. For $a=2$: $bc=180$ with $b\\ge2$, giving $9-1=8$. For $a=3$: $bc=120$ with $b\\ge3$, giving $8-2=6$. For $a=4$: $bc=90$ with $4\\le b\\le9$, so $b=5,6,9$, giving $3$. For $a=5$: $bc=72$ with $5\\le b\\le8$, so $b=6,8$, giving $2$. For $a=6$: $bc=60$ with $b=6$ only, giving $1$. For $a\\ge7$ there is nothing, since $a^3\\le360$ and $7$ does not divide $360$. The total is $12+8+6+3+2+1=32$. The number of <em>ordered<\/em> triples is $180$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EB1-19", "paper": "EB1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>$ABCDEF$ is a regular hexagon, with its vertices labelled in order. Let $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AF}=\\mathbf{b}$. The point $M$ is the midpoint of the side $CD$, and the straight line through $A$ and $M$ meets the diagonal $BF$ at the point $P$. Which of the following is equal to $\\overrightarrow{AP}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{5}{7}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{2}{7}\\mathbf{b}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{5}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{3}{5}\\mathbf{b}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{7}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{3}{7}\\mathbf{b}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{5}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{2}{5}\\mathbf{b}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-19.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Regular hexagon ABCDEF with centre O, vectors a along AB and b along AF, M the midpoint of CD, and the line AM crossing the diagonal BF at P.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The hexagon is made of six equilateral triangles about its centre $O$, so $\\overrightarrow{AO}=\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b$ and $\\overrightarrow{BC}=\\overrightarrow{AO}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{AC}=2\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b$; also $\\overrightarrow{CD}=\\overrightarrow{AF}=\\mathbf b$, so $\\overrightarrow{AM}=2\\mathbf a+\\tfrac32\\mathbf b$. A point on $AM$ is $t\\left(2\\mathbf a+\\tfrac32\\mathbf b\\right)$ and a point on $BF$ is $\\mathbf a+u(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)=(1-u)\\mathbf a+u\\mathbf b$. Since $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are not parallel, $2t=1-u$ and $\\tfrac32t=u$, so $t=\\tfrac27$ and $\\overrightarrow{AP}=\\tfrac47\\mathbf a+\\tfrac37\\mathbf b$. The option $\\tfrac35\\mathbf a+\\tfrac25\\mathbf b$ also lies on $BF$ but not on $AM$; $\\tfrac12\\mathbf a+\\tfrac12\\mathbf b$ is the midpoint of $BF$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EB1-20", "paper": "EB1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>A triangular field $ABC$ has $BC=12\\text{ m}$. Two straight fences, each parallel to $BC$ and each running from the side $AB$ to the side $AC$, divide the field into three regions whose areas, taken in order starting from the vertex $A$, are in the ratio $1:2:3$. What is the total length of the two fences?<\/p>", "opts": ["$(4\\sqrt{3}+4\\sqrt{6})\\text{ m}$", "$8\\text{ m}$", "$(2\\sqrt{3}+6\\sqrt{2})\\text{ m}$", "$(6\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{30})\\text{ m}$", "$(2\\sqrt{6}+6\\sqrt{2})\\text{ m}$", "$(4\\sqrt{6}+6\\sqrt{2})\\text{ m}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-20.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Triangle ABC with BC = 12 and two fences parallel to BC of lengths 2 root 6 and 6 root 2, dividing it into regions with areas in the ratio 1 : 2 : 3 from A.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Each fence cuts off at $A$ a triangle similar to $ABC$. The nearer fence cuts off $\\tfrac16$ of the area and the further one cuts off $\\tfrac16+\\tfrac26=\\tfrac12$. Lengths scale as the square root of areas, so the fences have lengths $12\\sqrt{\\tfrac16}=2\\sqrt6$ and $12\\sqrt{\\tfrac12}=6\\sqrt2$, a total of $2\\sqrt6+6\\sqrt2$ m. Taking lengths proportional to areas gives $2+6=8$; reading the ratio from $BC$ instead of from $A$ gives $6\\sqrt2+2\\sqrt{30}$; equal areas would give $4\\sqrt3+4\\sqrt6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio", "geometry"], "rating": 8.3}, {"id": "EB1-21", "paper": "EB1", "n": 21, "q": "<p>A rectangular sheet of card has sides in the ratio $1:\\sqrt{2}$. A guillotine is used repeatedly as follows: each time, the largest possible square is cut off from the rectangle that remains (one side of the square lying along a shorter side of the rectangle), and the square is thrown away. After three squares have been removed in this way, what fraction of the area of the original sheet remains?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{10-7\\sqrt{2}}{2}$", "$5\\sqrt{2}-7$", "$17-12\\sqrt{2}$", "$\\dfrac{7-4\\sqrt{2}}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{2}$", "$3-2\\sqrt{2}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-21.svg?v=1\" alt=\"A rectangle 1 by root 2 from which a first square of side 1 and then a second and third square of side root 2 minus 1 are cut, leaving a shaded strip of height 3 minus 2 root 2.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Take the sides as $1$ and $\\sqrt2$, area $\\sqrt2$. The first cut leaves a $1$ by $\\sqrt2-1$ strip. Since $\\sqrt2-1&lt;1$, the second square has side $\\sqrt2-1$ and leaves $(\\sqrt2-1)$ by $(2-\\sqrt2)$. Since $\\sqrt2-1&lt;2-\\sqrt2$, the third square has side $\\sqrt2-1$ again and leaves $(\\sqrt2-1)$ by $(3-2\\sqrt2)=(\\sqrt2-1)^2$. The remaining area is $(\\sqrt2-1)^3=5\\sqrt2-7$, and as a fraction of $\\sqrt2$ this is $\\dfrac{5\\sqrt2-7}{\\sqrt2}=5-\\dfrac{7}{\\sqrt2}=\\dfrac{10-7\\sqrt2}{2}$. Forgetting to divide by the original area gives $5\\sqrt2-7$; $3-2\\sqrt2$ is the fraction after only two cuts.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "ratio"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "EB1-22", "paper": "EB1", "n": 22, "q": "<p>In a Fibonacci-type sequence, each term from the third onwards is the sum of the two terms immediately before it. The first two terms of such a sequence are positive integers (not necessarily different), and its $10$th term is $2026$. How many such sequences are there?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$2$", "$1$", "$4$", "$6$", "$3$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>With first two terms $a$ and $b$, the terms are $a,\\ b,\\ a+b,\\ a+2b,\\ 2a+3b,\\ 3a+5b,\\ 5a+8b,\\ 8a+13b,\\ 13a+21b,\\ 21a+34b$. So we need $21a+34b=2026$ with $a,b\\ge1$, that is, $2026-34b$ must be a positive multiple of $21$. As $2026=21\\times96+10$ and $34b=21b+13b$, this needs $13b-10$ to be a multiple of $21$. The smallest such $b$ is $4$ (since $52-10=42$), and then $b$ can go up in steps of $21$. The values $b=4,25,46$ give $a=90,56,22$, while $b=67$ makes $a$ negative. So there are $3$ sequences.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra", "number"], "rating": 8.5}, {"id": "EB1-23", "paper": "EB1", "n": 23, "q": "<p>Seven positive integers have a mean of $10$ and a median of $9$, and the number $12$ is their only mode. Let $R$ be the greatest possible range of the seven integers, and let $r$ be the least possible range. What is $R-r$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$24$", "$27$", "$29$", "$20$", "$26$", "$30$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>In order the integers are $a\\le b\\le c\\le9\\le e\\le f\\le g$ with the middle one equal to $9$ and total $70$. Since $12$ is a mode it appears at least twice, and as $12>9$ it fills at least two of the places $e,f,g$. Greatest range: take $e=f=12$, with $12$ appearing exactly twice, so every other value may appear only once; then $a,b,c$ are at least $1,2,3$ and $g=70-(1+2+3+9+24)=31$, so $R=30$. (Taking $a=b=c=1$ would make $1$ a mode too.) Least range: with $e=f=g=12$, $a+b+c=25$ with $c\\le9$; no value other than $12$ may appear three times, so $(7,9,9)$ fails and the best is $(8,8,9)$, giving $r=12-8=4$. If $12$ appears only twice, every other value appears once, so $c\\le8$ and $a+b+c\\le6+7+8=21$; this forces $e=f=12$ and $g\\ge16$, and the range is at least $10$. So $R-r=26$.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "EB1-24", "paper": "EB1", "n": 24, "q": "<p>A tiler wants to fit three regular polygons together around a point $P$, so that each polygon has a vertex at $P$, no two of the polygons overlap, and together they fill the whole angle around $P$ with no gaps. No two of the three polygons may have the same number of sides. How many different sets of three polygons, described by their numbers of sides, are possible?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$7$", "$10$", "$8$", "$4$", "$6$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>A regular $n$-gon has interior angle $180^\\circ-\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{n}$, so the condition becomes $\\dfrac1a+\\dfrac1b+\\dfrac1c=\\dfrac12$ with $3\\le a&lt;b&lt;c$. Since $\\dfrac3a>\\dfrac12$, $a\\le5$. For $a=3$: $(b-6)(c-6)=36$, giving $(7,42),(8,24),(9,18),(10,15)$. For $a=4$: $(b-4)(c-4)=16$, giving $(5,20),(6,12)$ (the factorisation $4\\times4$ would make $b=c$). For $a=5$: $\\dfrac1b+\\dfrac1c=\\dfrac3{10}$ needs $b&lt;\\dfrac{20}{3}$, so $b=6$, but then $c=\\tfrac{15}{2}$. That makes $6$ sets. Allowing repeated polygons would add $\\{3,12,12\\},\\{4,8,8\\},\\{5,5,10\\},\\{6,6,6\\}$, giving the trap $10$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "number"], "rating": 8.7}, {"id": "EB1-25", "paper": "EB1", "n": 25, "q": "<p>In a tennis match between Ana and Bea, the first player to win three sets wins the match, and no further sets are played once the match has been decided. Ana wins each set with probability $\\tfrac23$, independently of the results of all other sets. Given that Ana wins the match, what is the probability that the match lasted exactly four sets?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{8}{27}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{10}{27}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{5}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>For Ana to win in exactly $n$ sets she must win the $n$th set and exactly two of the first $n-1$. In three sets: $\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^3=\\tfrac{8}{27}$. In four: $3\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^2\\left(\\tfrac13\\right)\\times\\tfrac23=\\tfrac{8}{27}$. In five: $6\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^2\\left(\\tfrac13\\right)^2\\times\\tfrac23=\\tfrac{16}{81}$. So $P(\\text{Ana wins})=\\tfrac{24+24+16}{81}=\\tfrac{64}{81}$, and the required probability is $\\dfrac{24/81}{64/81}=\\dfrac38$. The option $\\tfrac{8}{27}$ is the unconditional probability that Ana wins in four sets, $\\tfrac{10}{27}$ is the probability of four sets with either winner, and $\\tfrac45$ is the reverse conditional, $P(\\text{Ana wins}\\mid\\text{four sets})$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 8.8}, {"id": "EB1-26", "paper": "EB1", "n": 26, "q": "<p>Five friends each bring a wrapped present to a party and put it in a sack. The presents are thoroughly mixed, and the friends then take turns to take one present each, chosen at random from those remaining in the sack, until the sack is empty. What is the probability that at most one of the friends ends up with the present that they brought?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{7}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{89}{120}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{19}{24}$", "$\\dfrac{11}{30}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{8}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>All $5!=120$ allocations are equally likely. Let $D_n$ be the number of ways in which none of $n$ people gets their own present. Listing gives $D_2=1$, $D_3=2$ and $D_4=9$. For five people, friend $1$ receives the present of one of the other four, say friend $2$ ($4$ choices). Either friend $2$ receives friend $1$'s present, and the other three are deranged in $D_3=2$ ways, or friend $2$ does not, which is like a derangement of four in $D_4=9$ ways. So $D_5=4(2+9)=44$. Exactly one person gets their own present in $5\\times D_4=45$ ways. The answer is $\\dfrac{44+45}{120}=\\dfrac{89}{120}$. The options $\\tfrac{11}{30}$ and $\\tfrac38$ are the cases of nobody and exactly one separately.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 8.9}, {"id": "EB1-27", "paper": "EB1", "n": 27, "q": "<p>A glass paperweight is a solid right pyramid with a square base, and all eight of its edges have length $6\\text{ cm}$. A manufacturer wants to set the largest possible sphere entirely inside the paperweight. What is the radius of this sphere?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\text{ cm}$", "$(\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2})\\text{ cm}$", "$(6-3\\sqrt{2})\\text{ cm}$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}-3}{2}\\text{ cm}$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{6}-3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{ cm}$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{ cm}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB1-27.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Left: the square-based pyramid with the vertical section through the apex and the midpoints of two opposite base edges shaded. Right: that section, an isosceles triangle with base 6, sides 3 root 3 and height 3 root 2, with its inscribed circle of radius r.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The figure shows the pyramid with the section used, and the section itself with the sphere appearing as its inscribed circle.<\/p><p>By symmetry the sphere's centre is on the axis and the sphere touches the base and all four triangular faces. The half-diagonal of the base is $3\\sqrt2$, so the height is $\\sqrt{36-18}=3\\sqrt2$, and the height of each triangular face, from the apex to the midpoint of a base edge, is $\\sqrt{36-9}=3\\sqrt3$. Cut through the apex and the midpoints of two opposite base edges: the section is an isosceles triangle with base $6$, equal sides $3\\sqrt3$ and height $3\\sqrt2$, and the sphere appears as its inscribed circle. So $r=\\dfrac{\\text{area}}{\\text{semi-perimeter}}=\\dfrac{9\\sqrt2}{3+3\\sqrt3}=\\dfrac{3\\sqrt2}{1+\\sqrt3}=\\dfrac{3\\sqrt6-3\\sqrt2}{2}$. As a check, $r=\\dfrac{3V}{S}$ with $V=36\\sqrt2$ and $S=36+36\\sqrt3$. The section through two opposite vertices (sides $6,6,6\\sqrt2$) gives $6-3\\sqrt2$, but that circle would poke out through the faces; $\\tfrac{\\sqrt6}{2}$ is the answer for a regular tetrahedron of edge $6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 9.0}, {"id": "EB2-1", "paper": "EB2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>A sealed mineral sample contains two radioactive isotopes, $P$ and $Q$, neither of which is produced inside the sample. Isotope $P$ has a half-life of $1200$ years and isotope $Q$ has a half-life of $1800$ years. When the sample formed, it contained $8$ times as many atoms of $P$ as atoms of $Q$. Today it contains only one quarter as many atoms of $P$ as atoms of $Q$. How many years ago did the sample form?<\/p>", "opts": ["$18\\,000$", "$10\\,800$", "$6000$", "$9000$", "$3600$", "$3000$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>After $t$ years the numbers of atoms are proportional to $8\\cdot 2^{-t/1200}$ and $2^{-t/1800}$, so the ratio of $P$ to $Q$ is \\[8\\cdot 2^{-t/1200+t/1800}=8\\cdot 2^{-t/3600}.\\] The ratio itself halves every $3600$ years. Setting $8\\cdot 2^{-t/3600}=\\dfrac14$ gives $2^{t/3600}=32$, so $t=5\\times 3600=18\\,000$. Subtracting the half-lives ($600$ years per halving) gives $3000$, adding the decay exponents instead of subtracting them gives $3600$, following only one isotope gives $6000$ or $9000$, and reading the final ratio as $1:1$ gives $10\\,800$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EB2-2", "paper": "EB2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>A rectangular frame has perimeter $40$ dm. To fit into its packing crate, the length of its diagonal must be at most $15$ dm, and for rigidity each of its sides must be at least $7$ dm long. Which of the following describes all the possible values of the area $A$ of the frame, in $\\text{dm}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$87.5\\le A\\le 112.5$", "$91\\le A\\le 100$", "$87.5\\le A\\le 100$", "$49\\le A\\le 100$", "$0&lt;A\\le 100$", "$91\\le A\\le 112.5$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Let the sides be $a$ and $20-a$. The side condition gives $7\\le a\\le 13$. The diagonal condition is $a^2+(20-a)^2\\le 225$, that is $400-2A\\le 225$, or $A\\ge 87.5$. On $7\\le a\\le 13$ the area $A=a(20-a)=100-(a-10)^2$ takes every value from $91$ (at $a=7$ or $13$) to $100$ (the square, whose diagonal $10\\sqrt2$ is less than $15$), so the diagonal condition is automatically met and the answer is $91\\le A\\le 100$. Ignoring the side condition gives the lower bound $87.5$; turning the diagonal condition into an upper bound via $2A\\le a^2+b^2$ gives $112.5$; multiplying the two minimum sides gives $49$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EB2-3", "paper": "EB2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>A ship sails in a straight line from a harbour $H$ on a course that makes an angle of $30^\\circ$ with the line from $H$ to a lighthouse $L$, where $HL=10$ km. The ship's radar can detect the lighthouse only when the ship is within $7$ km of it. The ship keeps to its course until it is far beyond the lighthouse. What is the total distance the ship sails while it is within $7$ km of the lighthouse?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4\\sqrt6$ km", "$\\sqrt{51}$ km", "$\\left(5\\sqrt3+2\\sqrt6\\right)$ km", "$5\\sqrt3$ km", "$10\\sqrt3$ km", "$2\\sqrt6$ km"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB2-3.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Harbour H, lighthouse L with HL = 10 at 30 degrees to the course, the circle of radius 7 about L cutting the course in a highlighted chord; the perpendicular from L to the course has length 5 and the half-chord is 2 root 6.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The perpendicular distance from $L$ to the course is $10\\sin 30^\\circ=5$ km. The course cuts the circle of radius $7$ about $L$ in a chord of half-length $\\sqrt{49-25}=2\\sqrt6$, so the required distance is $4\\sqrt6$ km. Both ends of the chord are ahead of $H$, because the foot of the perpendicular is $10\\cos30^\\circ=5\\sqrt3\\approx 8.7$ km along the course, which exceeds $2\\sqrt6\\approx 4.9$. Equivalently, the cosine rule gives $s^2-10\\sqrt3\\,s+51=0$ for the distance $s$ sailed, and the roots differ by $\\sqrt{300-204}=\\sqrt{96}$. The sum of the roots, $10\\sqrt3$, the larger root $5\\sqrt3+2\\sqrt6$ and the half-chord $2\\sqrt6$ are the usual slips.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EB2-4", "paper": "EB2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A walkway runs along the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3$, with lengths in metres. Above it, the underside of a curved roof truss is modelled by \\[y=x^2-2kx+4,\\] where $k$ is a design constant. Safety rules require the truss to be strictly above the walkway at every point of it, that is, $y>0$ for every $x$ with $0\\le x\\le 3$. Which of the following describes all the values of $k$ that satisfy the rules?<\/p>", "opts": ["$k&lt;\\dfrac{13}{6}$", "$k&lt;2$", "$-2&lt;k&lt;\\dfrac{13}{6}$", "$0&lt;k&lt;2$", "$-2&lt;k&lt;2$", "$k\\le 2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>At $x=0$ we have $y=4>0$ for every $k$. For $0&lt;x\\le 3$, the condition $x^2-2kx+4>0$ is equivalent to $2k&lt;x+\\dfrac4x$. On this interval $x+\\dfrac4x$ has least value $4$, at $x=2$ (by differentiation, or since $x+\\dfrac4x-4=\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{x}$). So the rules hold exactly when $2k&lt;4$, that is $k&lt;2$. Checking only the ends of the walkway gives $k&lt;\\dfrac{13}{6}$, but $k=2.1$ fails at $x=2$. Demanding a negative discriminant gives $-2&lt;k&lt;2$, which wrongly rejects negative $k$: then the vertex is to the left of the walkway and the truss is fine. At $k=2$ the truss touches the walkway at $x=2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EB2-5", "paper": "EB2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Two arithmetic progressions are described by their partial sums. For every positive integer $n$, the sum of the first $n$ terms of the first progression is $3n^2+4n$, and the sum of the first $n$ terms of the second progression is $5n^2-2n$. How many numbers less than $1000$ are terms of both progressions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$32$", "$33$", "$16$", "$1$", "$66$", "$34$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The $n$th term of the first progression is $S_n-S_{n-1}=6n+1$ (and the first term $S_1=7$ fits), so its terms are $7,13,19,\\dots$, the numbers from $7$ upwards that leave remainder $1$ on division by $6$. Similarly the second has $n$th term $10n-7$, giving $3,13,23,\\dots$, the numbers from $3$ upwards ending in $3$. A number is in both exactly when it is odd, leaves remainder $1$ on division by $3$ and remainder $3$ on division by $5$, that is, when it leaves remainder $13$ on division by $30$. These are $13,43,\\dots,973$, which is $33$ numbers. Comparing only terms in the same position ($6n+1=10n-7$) finds just one common value, $13$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EB2-6", "paper": "EB2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>A cargo ship is to make a journey of $300$ km at a constant speed of $v$ km/h, where harbour regulations require $0&lt;v\\le 15$. While the ship is moving, its running cost is \\[\\left(\\frac{v^3}{1000}+16\\right)\\text{ hundred pounds per hour.}\\] What is the least possible running cost of the whole journey, in hundreds of pounds?<\/p>", "opts": ["$400$", "$375$", "$320$", "$360$", "$387.5$", "$510$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The journey takes $\\dfrac{300}{v}$ hours, so its cost is \\[J(v)=\\frac{300}{v}\\left(\\frac{v^3}{1000}+16\\right)=0.3v^2+\\frac{4800}{v}.\\] Then $J'(v)=0.6v-\\dfrac{4800}{v^2}$, which is zero only when $v^3=8000$, that is $v=20$, outside the permitted range. For $0&lt;v&lt;20$ we have $J'(v)&lt;0$, so $J$ is decreasing throughout $0&lt;v\\le 15$ and the least cost is $J(15)=67.5+320=387.5$. The unconstrained minimum $J(20)=360$ is the trap; $320$ counts only the fixed part of the cost.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EB2-7", "paper": "EB2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>The line $y=2x+c$, where $c$ is an integer, meets the circle \\[x^2+y^2-6x+4y-12=0\\] at two points $A$ and $B$. Given that the chord $AB$ has length at least $6$, what is the sum of all the possible values of $c$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$104$", "$-152$", "$136$", "$-136$", "$-104$", "$-120$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB2-7.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Axes with the circle of centre (3,-2) and radius 5, and a line y = 2x + c cutting it in a highlighted chord AB of half-length 3 at perpendicular distance d from the centre.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The figure shows the extreme case, a chord of length exactly 6.<\/p><p>Completing the square, the circle has centre $(3,-2)$ and radius $5$. A chord of length at least $6$ has half-length at least $3$, so its distance $d$ from the centre satisfies $d\\le\\sqrt{25-9}=4$ (and then $d&lt;5$, so there really are two points). The distance from $(3,-2)$ to $2x-y+c=0$ is $\\dfrac{|6+2+c|}{\\sqrt5}$, so we need $|c+8|\\le\\sqrt{80}$. Since $8&lt;\\sqrt{80}&lt;9$, the integers are those with $|c+8|\\le 8$, namely $c=-16,-15,\\dots,0$, with sum $-136$. A sign slip in the distance formula gives $+136$; using $d\\le 3$ gives $-104$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EB2-8", "paper": "EB2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>A long horizontal pipe has a circular internal cross-section of radius $2$ m. Water flows along the pipe, and in every cross-section the water is $3$ m deep at its deepest point. Engineers define the <em>hydraulic radius<\/em> of the flow as the cross-sectional area of the water divided by the wetted perimeter, where the wetted perimeter is the length of the part of the pipe's internal boundary, in the cross-section, that is in contact with the water. What is the hydraulic radius, in metres?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1+\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{4\\pi}$", "$\\dfrac{8\\pi+3\\sqrt3}{8\\pi+6\\sqrt3}$", "$1-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{4\\pi}$", "$1+\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{8\\pi}$", "$1+\\dfrac{3}{8\\pi}$", "$1$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB2-8.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Circular cross-section of radius 2 with centre O and water shaded up to a horizontal surface 1 above O, so 3 deep; the dry segment's chord subtends 2 pi over 3 at O and the wetted arc is highlighted.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The water surface is $1$ m above the centre, so the dry part of the cross-section is a segment whose chord is $1$ m from the centre; it subtends an angle $\\theta$ with $\\cos\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}=\\dfrac12$, so $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$. The dry segment has area $\\dfrac12\\cdot 4\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}-\\sqrt3$, so the water has area $4\\pi-\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}+\\sqrt3=\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}+\\sqrt3$. The wetted arc subtends $2\\pi-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$, so it has length $\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}$. The hydraulic radius is $\\dfrac{8\\pi/3+\\sqrt3}{8\\pi/3}=1+\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{8\\pi}$. Working with the minor segment as if the depth were $1$ m gives $1-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{4\\pi}$; including the free surface in the perimeter gives the fraction with $6\\sqrt3$ in the denominator.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EB2-9", "paper": "EB2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A circular running track passes through three marker posts with coordinates $A(1,3)$, $B(13,9)$ and $C(7,1)$, in units of $10$ m. A straight footpath runs from a gate at the origin $O$ and meets the track at a single point $T$, touching it there. What is the length $OT$, in the same units?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$5\\sqrt6$", "$10$", "$\\sqrt{10}$", "$10-5\\sqrt2$", "$5\\sqrt2$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The perpendicular bisector of $AB$ passes through $(7,6)$ with gradient $-2$, and that of $AC$ passes through $(4,2)$ with gradient $3$; they meet at the centre $(6,8)$. The radius squared is $(1-6)^2+(3-8)^2=50$. Since $OT$ is tangent, the radius to $T$ is perpendicular to $OT$, so by Pythagoras $OT^2=\\left(6^2+8^2\\right)-50=50$ and $OT=5\\sqrt2$. (Equivalently, the track is $x^2+y^2-12x-16y+50=0$ and $OT^2$ is the constant term.) The distance to the centre is $10$, and the shortest distance from $O$ to the track is $10-5\\sqrt2$; adding instead of subtracting $r^2$ gives $5\\sqrt6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EB2-10", "paper": "EB2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>What is the largest integer that is less than $\\left(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3\\right)^6$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$969$", "$968$", "$485$", "$970$", "$971$", "$484$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>First $\\left(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3\\right)^2=5+2\\sqrt6$, and by the binomial expansion \\[\\left(5+2\\sqrt6\\right)^3=125+3\\cdot25\\cdot2\\sqrt6+3\\cdot5\\cdot24+48\\sqrt6=485+198\\sqrt6.\\] Likewise $\\left(5-2\\sqrt6\\right)^3=485-198\\sqrt6$, so the two cubes add to the integer $970$. Since $\\left(5-2\\sqrt6\\right)\\left(5+2\\sqrt6\\right)=1$, we have $0&lt;5-2\\sqrt6&lt;\\dfrac19$, so $\\left(5-2\\sqrt6\\right)^3$ is a tiny positive number and $\\left(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3\\right)^6=970-\\left(5-2\\sqrt6\\right)^3$ lies just below $970$. The answer is $969$. Estimating with $\\sqrt6\\approx 2.45$ gives about $970.1$ and the wrong answer $970$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq", "alg"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EB2-11", "paper": "EB2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>Positive real numbers $x$ and $y$, with $x\\ne y$, satisfy \\[x^{y}=y^{x}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y^{2}=x^{3}.\\] What is the value of $x+y$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{45}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{81}{16}$", "$\\dfrac{27}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{20}{27}$", "$2$", "$\\dfrac{13}{4}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Since $y>0$, $y=x^{3/2}$. Taking logarithms of $x^y=y^x$ gives $y\\log x=x\\log y=\\dfrac32 x\\log x$, so \\[\\log x\\left(x^{3/2}-\\tfrac32x\\right)=0.\\] If $\\log x=0$ then $x=y=1$, which is excluded. Otherwise $x^{1/2}=\\dfrac32$, so $x=\\dfrac94$ and $y=\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)^3=\\dfrac{27}{8}$, giving $x+y=\\dfrac{45}{8}$. Check: both $x^y$ and $y^x$ equal $\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)^{27/4}$. Inverting the fraction when solving $x^{1/2}=\\dfrac32$ leads to $\\dfrac{20}{27}$, and forgetting the condition $x\\ne y$ suggests $2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "alg"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EB2-12", "paper": "EB2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>A cup of coffee cools in a room whose temperature stays constant but is not known. Assume Newton's model: the excess of the coffee's temperature over the room temperature is multiplied by the same factor in any two time intervals of equal length. A thermometer in the coffee reads $92^\\circ\\text{C}$ at 09:00, $56^\\circ\\text{C}$ at 09:10 and $38^\\circ\\text{C}$ at 09:20. According to the model, at what time will the thermometer read $22.25^\\circ\\text{C}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["10:00", "10:10", "09:40", "09:45", "09:50", "09:30"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>If the room temperature is $R$, the excesses $92-R$, $56-R$, $38-R$ form a geometric progression, so $(56-R)^2=(92-R)(38-R)$. The $R^2$ terms cancel, leaving $3136-112R=3496-130R$, so $R=20$. The excesses are $72$, $36$, $18$: the excess halves every $10$ minutes. A reading of $22.25^\\circ\\text{C}$ is an excess of $2.25=\\dfrac{72}{32}$, which is five halvings after 09:00, so the time is 09:50. Assuming a room temperature of $0^\\circ\\text{C}$ gives nonsense; measuring five halvings from the 09:20 reading gives 10:10, and confusing the $30$ minutes after the last reading with a clock time gives 09:30.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "seq"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EB2-13", "paper": "EB2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>How many values of $x$ in the interval $0\\le x&lt;2\\pi$ satisfy \\[9^{\\sin^2 x}+9^{\\cos^2 x}=4\\sqrt3\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$4$", "$6$", "$0$", "$16$", "$2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let $a=9^{\\sin^2x}$. Because $\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$, the second term is $9^{1-\\sin^2x}=\\dfrac9a$, so $a+\\dfrac9a=4\\sqrt3$, that is $a^2-4\\sqrt3\\,a+9=0$, giving $a=\\sqrt3$ or $a=3\\sqrt3$. These mean $\\sin^2x=\\dfrac14$ or $\\sin^2x=\\dfrac34$, so $\\sin x\\in\\left\\{\\pm\\dfrac12,\\pm\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right\\}$. Each of these four values of $\\sin x$ is taken exactly twice in $0\\le x&lt;2\\pi$, giving $8$ solutions. Keeping only one root of the quadratic gives $4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig", "explog"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EB2-14", "paper": "EB2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>For which real values of $m$ does the equation \\[\\sqrt{x+4}=mx+2\\] have exactly two distinct real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0&lt;m&lt;\\dfrac12$ with $m\\ne\\dfrac14$", "$0&lt;m\\le\\dfrac12$ with $m\\ne\\dfrac14$", "$m\\ne0$ with $m\\ne\\dfrac14$", "$0&lt;m&lt;\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac14&lt;m\\le\\dfrac12$", "$m>0$ with $m\\ne\\dfrac14$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The point $(0,2)$ lies on $y=\\sqrt{x+4}$, so $x=0$ is always a solution. Squaring gives $x+4=m^2x^2+4mx+4$, so $x\\left(m^2x+4m-1\\right)=0$. For $m=0$ only $x=0$ remains. For $m\\ne 0$ the other candidate is $x=\\dfrac{1-4m}{m^2}$, and it is a genuine solution exactly when $mx+2\\ge 0$ (then $x+4=(mx+2)^2\\ge0$ automatically). Here $mx+2=\\dfrac{1-2m}{m}$, which is non-negative exactly when $0&lt;m\\le\\dfrac12$. The candidate differs from $0$ unless $m=\\dfrac14$, when the line is the tangent at $(0,2)$. At $m=\\dfrac12$ the second solution is the end point $x=-4$, which counts. Accepting every root of the squared equation gives $m\\ne0,\\ \\dfrac14$; forgetting the end point gives the strict inequality.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs", "alg"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EB2-15", "paper": "EB2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The boundary of a circular arena is the circle \\[x^2+y^2-4x+2y-20=0.\\] A floodlight stands at the point $P(4,1)$. Every straight chord of the boundary circle that passes through $P$ is considered. The midpoints of all these chords form a closed curve. What is the area enclosed by this curve?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\pi$", "$2\\pi$", "$4\\pi$", "$8\\pi$", "$25\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB2-15.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Axes with the boundary circle of centre C(2,-1) and radius 5, the point P(4,1), a chord through P with midpoint M where angle CMP is a right angle, and the highlighted small circle on diameter CP traced by M.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The boundary has centre $C(2,-1)$ and radius $5$, and $CP=\\sqrt{4+4}=2\\sqrt2&lt;5$, so $P$ is inside. The line from the centre to the midpoint $M$ of a chord is perpendicular to the chord, so angle $CMP$ is a right angle (or $M$ is $C$ or $P$ itself). Hence $M$ lies on the circle with diameter $CP$, and every point of that circle arises from some chord through $P$. Its radius is $\\sqrt2$, so the area is $2\\pi$. Using $CP$ as the radius gives $8\\pi$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EB2-16", "paper": "EB2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>The volume $V$, in thousands of cubic metres, of water in a reservoir $t$ hours after midnight satisfies \\[\\frac{dV}{dt}=6\\sqrt t-t-8\\qquad\\text{for } 0\\le t\\le 25.\\] At midnight the reservoir holds $100$ thousand cubic metres. What is the least volume of water in the reservoir, in thousands of cubic metres, at any time in this $25$-hour period?<\/p>", "opts": ["$95.5$", "$88$", "$100$", "$87.5$", "$90$", "$92$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Integrating, $V=100+4t^{3/2}-\\dfrac{t^2}{2}-8t$. Writing $u=\\sqrt t$, the rate is $-(u^2-6u+8)=-(u-2)(u-4)$, so $V$ decreases until $t=4$, increases until $t=16$ and then decreases again. The candidates for the least value are the local minimum $V(4)=100+32-8-32=92$ and the end point $V(25)=100+500-312.5-200=87.5$. So the least volume is $87.5$, reached at the end of the period, not at the stationary point. (Also $V(16)=100$, so the greatest volume occurs twice.) Stopping at the stationary point gives $92$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "int"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "EB2-17", "paper": "EB2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Two vertical walls meet at a corner, making an angle of $60^\\circ$ with each other. Infinitely many circular discs lie flat on the floor in the corner. Each disc touches both walls; the first disc has radius $9$ cm, and each subsequent disc touches the previous one and lies between it and the corner. What is the total area of all the discs, in $\\text{cm}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$81\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{729\\pi}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{729\\pi}{10}$", "$27\\pi$", "$108\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{243\\pi}{2}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB2-17.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Two walls meeting at 60 degrees with the bisector dashed, half-angle 30 degrees marked, and a sequence of discs shrinking towards the corner; the first disc has radius r and the next radius s, each drawn to its tangent point on a wall.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>A disc of radius $r$ touching both walls has its centre on the bisector of the corner, at distance $\\dfrac{r}{\\sin30^\\circ}=2r$ from the corner. If the next disc has radius $s$, then the distance from the corner to the first centre is $2s+s+r$, so $2r=3s+r$ and $s=\\dfrac r3$. The areas form a geometric progression with first term $81\\pi$ and common ratio $\\dfrac19$, so the total is $\\dfrac{81\\pi}{1-1/9}=\\dfrac{729\\pi}{8}$. Using the radius ratio $\\dfrac13$ for the areas gives $\\dfrac{243\\pi}{2}$; taking the radii to halve gives $108\\pi$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq", "trig"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "EB2-18", "paper": "EB2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>A taut drive belt passes around two pulleys of radii $9$ cm and $3$ cm, whose centres are $24$ cm apart. The belt crosses over itself between the pulleys: its two straight sections are both tangent to both pulleys, and they cross each other on the line joining the centres. Ignoring the thickness of the belt, what is its total length, in cm?<\/p>", "opts": ["$24\\sqrt3+12\\pi$", "$24\\sqrt3+8\\pi$", "$12\\sqrt3+16\\pi$", "$12\\sqrt{15}+16\\pi$", "$48+16\\pi$", "$24\\sqrt3+16\\pi$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB2-18.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Pulleys of radii 9 and 3 with the crossed belt highlighted; the straight sections cross at X on the line of centres, 18 from the larger centre, making angle alpha with that line, with the radius to a tangent point at right angles to the belt.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The crossing point $X$ divides the line of centres in the ratio of the radii, so it is $18$ cm from the larger centre and $6$ cm from the smaller. Each straight section makes an angle $\\alpha$ with the line of centres where $\\sin\\alpha=\\dfrac{9}{18}=\\dfrac12$, so $\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$, and its length is $\\sqrt{18^2-9^2}+\\sqrt{6^2-3^2}=9\\sqrt3+3\\sqrt3=12\\sqrt3$. On each pulley the radii to the two points of tangency make an angle $2\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\alpha\\right)=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ on the side facing $X$, so the belt wraps through $2\\pi-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ on both pulleys. The arcs total $(9+3)\\cdot\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}=16\\pi$, and the length is $24\\sqrt3+16\\pi$. Assuming a half-turn on each pulley gives $12\\pi$ for the arcs; using the uncrossed tangents gives $12\\sqrt{15}$ for the straight parts.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "EB2-19", "paper": "EB2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>A paper drinking cup in the shape of an open right circular cone is made by cutting a sector from a circular sheet of radius $6$ cm and joining the two straight edges of the sector exactly, without overlap. What sector angle, in radians, gives the cup of greatest capacity?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\pi$", "$\\pi\\sqrt2$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi\\sqrt6}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi\\sqrt3}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi\\sqrt6}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The slant height of the cone is $6$, so its base radius $r$ and height $h$ satisfy $r^2+h^2=36$ and \\[V=\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\left(36-h^2\\right)h=\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\left(36h-h^3\\right).\\] Then $\\dfrac{dV}{dh}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\left(36-3h^2\\right)$, which is zero at $h=2\\sqrt3$, a maximum. There $r^2=24$, so $r=2\\sqrt6$. The arc of the sector becomes the base circumference: $6\\theta=2\\pi\\cdot 2\\sqrt6$, giving $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi\\sqrt6}{3}$ (about $294^\\circ$). Swapping $r$ and $h$ gives $\\dfrac{2\\pi\\sqrt3}{3}$; assuming $r=h$ at the optimum gives $\\pi\\sqrt2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "trig"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EB2-20", "paper": "EB2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>On a flat practice pitch, the two goalposts stand at points $A$ and $B$ that are $4$ m apart, and the goal line is the straight line through $A$ and $B$. For a point $P$ on the pitch side of the goal line (not on the line itself), the angle $APB$ is called the shooting angle at $P$. What is the area, in $\\text{m}^2$, of the region of the pitch from which the shooting angle is at least $45^\\circ$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$12\\pi+8$", "$8\\pi$", "$6\\pi+4$", "$6\\pi$", "$6\\pi-4$", "$2\\pi-4$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB2-20.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Goal posts A and B 4 m apart on the goal line, the circle of radius 2 root 2 centred at O, 2 m from AB, with angle AOB a right angle; the part of the disc on the pitch side is shaded, the minor segment beyond the line is hatched, and a point P on the arc shows the 45 degree angle.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>On the pitch side, the points with angle $APB=45^\\circ$ form an arc of the circle through $A$ and $B$ in which $AB$ subtends $90^\\circ$ at the centre $O$; this circle has radius $\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt2}=2\\sqrt2$, and $O$ is $2$ m from $AB$ on the pitch side. A point on the pitch side has shooting angle at least $45^\\circ$ exactly when it is inside or on this circle. So the region is the disc (area $8\\pi$) minus the minor segment beyond the goal line, whose area is $\\dfrac12\\cdot 8\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}-1\\right)=2\\pi-4$. The area is $8\\pi-(2\\pi-4)=6\\pi+4$. Taking the whole disc gives $8\\pi$, a sign slip with the segment gives $6\\pi-4$, and including the far side of the goal line doubles the answer.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "trig"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EB2-21", "paper": "EB2", "n": 21, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=x^{2/3}(x-5)$ for all real $x$, where $x^{2/3}$ means $\\left(\\sqrt[3]{x}\\right)^2$, using the real cube root. Which of the following statements are true?<\/p><ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f$ has a local maximum at $x=0$.<\/li><li>$f$ is decreasing for every $x&lt;2$.<\/li><li>The equation $f(x)=-4$ has exactly three real solutions.<\/li><\/ol>", "opts": ["none of them", "II and III only", "III only", "I, II and III", "II only", "I and III only", "I and II only", "I only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB2-21.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Graph of y = f(x) rising to a cusp at the origin, falling to a minimum at (2, -3 times 2 to the power 2/3), then rising through (5,0); the dashed line y = -4 meets the graph at three points.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>For $x\\ne0$, $f(x)=x^{5/3}-5x^{2/3}$ gives \\[f'(x)=\\tfrac53x^{2/3}-\\tfrac{10}{3}x^{-1/3}=\\frac{5(x-2)}{3\\sqrt[3]{x}}.\\] For $x&lt;0$ both $x-2$ and $\\sqrt[3]x$ are negative, so $f'>0$ and $f$ is increasing; for $0&lt;x&lt;2$, $f'&lt;0$; for $x>2$, $f'>0$. So II is false. Near $x=0$ we have $x^{2/3}>0$ and $x-5&lt;0$, so $f(x)&lt;0=f(0)$: there is a local maximum at $0$ (a cusp, not a stationary point), and I is true. The local minimum is $f(2)=-3\\cdot2^{2/3}$, and $2^{2/3}>\\dfrac43$ because $4>\\dfrac{64}{27}$, so $f(2)&lt;-4$. As $f$ rises from $-\\infty$ to $0$ on $x&lt;0$, falls to $f(2)$, then rises without bound, the line $y=-4$ is crossed three times, so III is true. The trap is to look only for stationary points and miss the cusp.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "graphs"], "rating": 8.3}, {"id": "EB2-22", "paper": "EB2", "n": 22, "q": "<p>A window has the shape of the region bounded by the $x$-axis and the parabola $y=4-x^2$, with lengths in metres. A straight horizontal glazing bar is fitted along the line $y=h$, dividing the glass into two parts of equal area. What is the value of $h$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt[3]{2}$", "$4-\\sqrt[3]{4}$", "$2$", "$4-2\\sqrt2$", "$4-\\sqrt[3]{2}$", "$4-2\\sqrt[3]{2}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB2-22.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The parabola y = 4 - x^2 above the x-axis between -2 and 2, cut by the highlighted horizontal bar y = h between x = -a and x = a into an upper part and a lower part of equal area.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The part above the bar lies between $x=\\pm a$, where $a=\\sqrt{4-h}$, and has area \\[\\int_{-a}^{a}\\left(a^2-x^2\\right)dx=\\frac43a^3=\\frac43(4-h)^{3/2}.\\] With $h=0$ the whole window has area $\\dfrac{32}{3}$, so we need $\\dfrac43(4-h)^{3/2}=\\dfrac{16}{3}$, that is $(4-h)^{3/2}=4$, so $4-h=4^{2/3}=2\\sqrt[3]2$ and $h=4-2\\sqrt[3]2\\approx1.48$. The value $2\\sqrt[3]2$ is the depth of the bar below the top, not its height; assuming the area above the bar is proportional to its depth gives $h=2$, and assuming it is proportional to the square of the depth gives $4-2\\sqrt2$.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "graphs"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "EB2-23", "paper": "EB2", "n": 23, "q": "<p>A cubic polynomial function $f$ has a local maximum at the point $(1,4)$ and a local minimum at the point $(3,0)$. Consider the following six equations.<\/p><ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(|x|)=1$<\/li><li>$|f(x)|=1$<\/li><li>$|f(|x|)|=3$<\/li><li>$f(|x|)=4$<\/li><li>$|f(x)|=4$<\/li><li>$f(|x|)=0$<\/li><\/ol><p>For how many of these equations is the number of distinct real solutions exactly four?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$6$", "$3$", "$2$", "$1$", "$5$", "$4$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>We have $f'(x)=k(x-1)(x-3)$, and $f(1)-f(3)=-\\displaystyle\\int_1^3k(x-1)(x-3)\\,dx=\\frac{4k}{3}=4$, so $k=3$, and with $f(3)=0$ this gives $f(x)=x^3-6x^2+9x=x(x-3)^2$. So $f\\ge0$ for $x\\ge0$; $f$ rises from $0$ to $4$ on $[0,1]$, falls to $0$ at $3$ and then rises, with $f(4)=4$. For $f(|x|)=c$, count the solutions with $x>0$, double them, and add $x=0$ if $c=0$. I: three positive solutions, so $6$. II: $f=1$ has three solutions and $f=-1$ has one (negative) solution, so $4$. III: $f(|x|)=-3$ is impossible and $f(|x|)=3$ gives $6$. IV: the positive solutions are $x=1$ and $x=4$, so $4$. V: $f=4$ at $x=1$ and $x=4$, and $f=-4$ once for $x&lt;0$, so $3$. VI: $x=0,\\pm3$, so $3$. Exactly two equations (II and IV) have four solutions.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs"], "rating": 8.5}, {"id": "EB2-24", "paper": "EB2", "n": 24, "q": "<p>A cable hangs between the tops of two vertical poles that stand $4$ m apart on level ground. Taking the foot of one pole as the origin, the height of the cable above the ground at horizontal distance $x$ m is $f(x)$ m, where $f$ is known to be a polynomial of degree at most $3$. An assistant measured the heights at equally spaced points and applied the trapezium rule to estimate \\[\\int_0^4 f(x)\\,dx,\\] obtaining $20$ with $2$ strips and $18.5$ with $4$ strips, and then lost the measurements. What is the exact value of $\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 f(x)\\,dx$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$17$", "$17.75$", "$19.25$", "$18$", "$18.5$", "$18.25$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Write $T_2$ and $T_4$ for the two estimates and $I$ for the integral. All three depend linearly on $f$, so it is enough to compare them for $f=1,x,x^2,x^3$. For $1$ and $x$ the trapezium rule is exact. For $x^2$: $I=\\dfrac{64}{3}$, $T_2=24$, $T_4=22$; for $x^3$: $I=64$, $T_2=80$, $T_4=68$. In both cases $T_2-I=4\\left(T_4-I\\right)$: halving the strip width divides the error by $4$. Hence $I=\\dfrac{4T_4-T_2}{3}=\\dfrac{74-20}{3}=18$ (this is Simpson's rule). Such a cable exists, for example $f(x)=3.5+0.75(x-2)^2$. Taking $T_4$ as exact gives $18.5$; assuming the error halves gives $17.75$; assuming it is unchanged gives $17$.<\/p>", "tags": ["int"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "EB2-25", "paper": "EB2", "n": 25, "q": "<p>A particle moves along a straight line. At time $t$ seconds its velocity is $v$ m/s, where $v$ is a cubic polynomial in $t$ whose $t^3$ coefficient is $4$. The particle is at a point $O$ when $t=0$, and it is back at $O$ when $t=1$, when $t=2$ and when $t=3$. What is the greatest distance of the particle from $O$, in metres, during the interval $0\\le t\\le 3$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac34$", "$\\dfrac{9}{16}$", "$\\dfrac{25}{16}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The displacement $s$ from $O$ satisfies $\\dfrac{ds}{dt}=v$, so integrating, $s$ is a polynomial of degree $4$ with $t^4$ coefficient $1$. It vanishes at $t=0,1,2,3$, so \\[s=t(t-1)(t-2)(t-3)=\\left(t^2-3t\\right)\\left(t^2-3t+2\\right).\\] With $w=t^2-3t$, which runs over $-\\dfrac94\\le w\\le0$ for $0\\le t\\le3$, we get $s=w(w+2)=(w+1)^2-1$. This ranges from $-1$ (at $w=-1$, that is $t=\\dfrac{3\\pm\\sqrt5}{2}$) up to $\\dfrac{25}{16}-1=\\dfrac{9}{16}$ (at $w=-\\dfrac94$, that is $t=\\dfrac32$). The greatest distance is $1$ m. The symmetry about $t=\\dfrac32$ tempts many to stop at the local maximum $\\dfrac{9}{16}$, but the particle goes further on the negative side.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "diff"], "rating": 8.7}, {"id": "EB2-26", "paper": "EB2", "n": 26, "q": "<p>The profile of a curved track is the curve \\[y=x^4-4x^3+3x^2+5x.\\] A straight rod is placed against the track so that it is tangent to the curve at two distinct points. What is the distance between these two points of contact?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt{15}$", "$2\\sqrt6$", "$\\sqrt{15}$", "$\\sqrt6$", "$3\\sqrt{10}$", "$\\sqrt{30}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EB2-26.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Graph of y = x^4 - 4x^3 + 3x^2 + 5x with the line y = 3x - 1/4 touching it at two points with x-coordinates p and q, whose horizontal separation is root 6.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>If the line $y=mx+c$ touches the curve at $x=p$ and $x=q$ with $p\\ne q$, then $x^4-4x^3+3x^2+(5-m)x-c$ has double roots at $p$ and $q$, so it equals $\\left(x^2-Sx+P\\right)^2$ with $S=p+q$, $P=pq$. Expanding, $\\left(x^2-Sx+P\\right)^2=x^4-2Sx^3+\\left(S^2+2P\\right)x^2-2SPx+P^2$. Comparing coefficients: $2S=4$, so $S=2$; $S^2+2P=3$, so $P=-\\dfrac12$; then $5-m=-2SP=2$, so $m=3$, and $c=-P^2=-\\dfrac14$. The points of contact satisfy $x^2-2x-\\dfrac12=0$, so they differ in $x$ by $\\sqrt{4+2}=\\sqrt6$, and since the rod has gradient $3$ their distance apart is $\\sqrt6\\cdot\\sqrt{1+3^2}=\\sqrt{60}=2\\sqrt{15}$. Stopping at the horizontal separation gives $\\sqrt6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg", "graphs"], "rating": 8.8}, {"id": "EB2-27", "paper": "EB2", "n": 27, "q": "<p>A tower is built from $10\\,000$ thin layers stacked one on top of another. For each $k=1,2,\\dots,10\\,000$, the $k$th layer from the bottom has thickness $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt k}$ cm. What is the height of the tower, correct to the nearest centimetre?<\/p>", "opts": ["$201$ cm", "$198$ cm", "$100$ cm", "$197$ cm", "$200$ cm", "$199$ cm"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The height is $H=\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{10000}k^{-1/2}$. Comparing rectangles with the area under $y=x^{-1/2}$ gives $2\\sqrt{10001}-2&lt;H&lt;1+\\displaystyle\\int_1^{10000}x^{-1/2}\\,dx=199$, so $198&lt;H&lt;199$; this fixes the integer part but not the rounding. Now apply the trapezium rule to $\\displaystyle\\int_1^{10000}x^{-1/2}\\,dx=198$ with unit strips: the estimate is $H-\\dfrac12\\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{100}\\right)=H-0.505$. Since $\\dfrac{d^2}{dx^2}x^{-1/2}=\\dfrac34x^{-5/2}>0$, the curve bends upwards and every trapezium lies above it, so the estimate exceeds the integral: $H-0.505>198$. Hence $198.505&lt;H&lt;199$ and the height is $199$ cm to the nearest centimetre (in fact $H\\approx198.54$). Quoting the integer part gives $198$, and using $2\\sqrt{10000}$ gives $200$.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "seq"], "rating": 9.0}, {"id": "ED1-1", "paper": "ED1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>How many points with integer coordinates lie inside the finite region enclosed by the curve $y=x^2-6x+5$ and the line $y=x-1$? Points lying on the curve or on the line are not counted.<\/p>", "opts": ["$14$", "$18$", "$26$", "$16$", "$20$", "$12$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED1-1.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The parabola y = x^2 - 6x + 5 and the line y = x - 1 meeting at (1,0) and (6,5), with the region between them shaded, the 16 interior lattice points shown as filled dots and the 10 boundary lattice points as hollow dots.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>In the figure, the filled dots are the points counted and the hollow dots lie on the boundary.<\/p><p>The graphs meet where $x^2-6x+5=x-1$, that is $x^2-7x+6=0$, so at $x=1$ and $x=6$; between these the line lies above the parabola. An interior point needs $2\\le x\\le5$ and $x^2-6x+5&lt; y &lt; x-1$.<\/p><p>For $x=2$: $-3&lt; y &lt; 1$, giving $3$ values. For $x=3$: $-4&lt; y &lt; 2$, giving $5$. For $x=4$: $-3&lt; y &lt; 3$, giving $5$. For $x=5$: $0&lt; y &lt; 4$, giving $3$. The total is $16$. Including the points on the boundary would give $26$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "ED1-2", "paper": "ED1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>Car $A$ has a fuel consumption of $6$ litres per $100\\text{ km}$. Car $B$ travels $45$ miles per gallon of fuel. Take $1\\text{ mile}=1.6\\text{ km}$ and $1\\text{ gallon}=4.5$ litres. Each car makes the same journey of $480\\text{ km}$.<\/p><p>Which car uses less fuel on the journey, and by how much?<\/p>", "opts": ["Car $A$, by $1.2$ litres", "Car $B$, by $4.8$ litres", "They use the same amount of fuel.", "Car $B$, by $1.2$ litres", "Car $A$, by $4.8$ litres", "Car $A$, by $48$ litres"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Car $A$ uses $6\\times4.8=28.8$ litres. For car $B$, $45$ miles per gallon is $45\\times1.6=72\\text{ km}$ per $4.5$ litres, which is $16\\text{ km}$ per litre, so it uses $\\dfrac{480}{16}=30$ litres. Car $A$ uses less, by $1.2$ litres.<\/p><p>The two measures run in opposite directions: litres per $100\\text{ km}$ is fuel per distance, while miles per gallon is distance per fuel, so a larger number is worse for $A$ and better for $B$. Converting the wrong way (dividing by $1.6$ instead of multiplying) makes car $B$ look far thirstier and gives the $48$ litre option.<\/p>", "tags": ["units"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "ED1-3", "paper": "ED1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>$ABCDE$ is a convex cyclic pentagon: its five vertices lie on a circle, in that order. The interior angle $\\angle ABC$ is $100^\\circ$ and the interior angle $\\angle CDE$ is $120^\\circ$.<\/p><p>What is the size of $\\angle ACE$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$50^\\circ$", "$20^\\circ$", "$70^\\circ$", "$40^\\circ$", "$80^\\circ$", "$60^\\circ$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED1-3.svg?v=1\" alt=\"A cyclic pentagon ABCDE in a circle with angle ABC = 100 degrees and angle CDE = 120 degrees marked, chords AC and CE highlighted, the angle ACE = 40 degrees marked, and the arc EA of 80 degrees drawn thick.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>An inscribed angle is half of the arc it stands on, the arc not containing its vertex. $\\angle ABC=100^\\circ$ stands on the arc $AEDC$, so that arc is $200^\\circ$ and the arc $ABC$ is $160^\\circ$. $\\angle CDE=120^\\circ$ stands on the arc $CBAE$, which is therefore $240^\\circ$, so the arc $CDE$ is $120^\\circ$.<\/p><p>The remaining arc $EA$, containing none of $B$, $C$, $D$, is $360^\\circ-160^\\circ-120^\\circ=80^\\circ$. The angle $\\angle ACE$ stands on this arc, so $\\angle ACE=40^\\circ$. The answer $80^\\circ$ forgets to halve the arc.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "ED1-4", "paper": "ED1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Seven students sit a test. If the lowest of the seven marks is removed, the mean of the remaining six marks is $3$ more than the mean of all seven marks. If instead the highest mark is removed, the mean of the remaining six marks is $4$ less than the mean of all seven marks.<\/p><p>What is the range of the seven marks?<\/p>", "opts": ["$49$", "$42$", "$21$", "$35$", "$7$", "$14$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Let the total of the marks be $S$, the lowest mark $L$ and the highest $H$. Then \\[S-L=6\\left(\\frac S7+3\\right)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad S-H=6\\left(\\frac S7-4\\right).\\] Subtracting, $H-L=6(3+4)=42$. The total $S$ is never needed. The multiplier is the number of marks that remain, $6$; using $7$ gives the trap $49$.<\/p><p>Such marks do exist: $10, 25, 26, 27, 28, 28, 52$ have mean $28$, and the two reduced means are $31$ and $24$.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "ED1-5", "paper": "ED1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>A clerk is asked to round a positive whole number $N$ to the nearest $1000$. Instead of doing this directly, she rounds $N$ to the nearest $10$, then rounds that answer to the nearest $100$, and then rounds that answer to the nearest $1000$. At every stage, a number exactly halfway between two candidates is rounded up.<\/p><p>For how many whole numbers $N$ from $1$ to $10\\,000$ inclusive does her method give a different answer from rounding $N$ directly to the nearest $1000$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$55$", "$50$", "$550$", "$1000$", "$450$", "$500$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Write $N=1000k+m$ with $0\\le m\\le999$. Rounding directly gives $1000k$ exactly when $m\\le499$, and $1000(k+1)$ otherwise. In the chain, $m$ first goes to the nearest $10$. If $m\\ge445$, this gives at least $450$, the next step gives $500$ or more, and the last step gives $1000(k+1)$. If $m\\le444$, the chain gives at most $440$, then at most $400$, and finally $1000k$.<\/p><p>So the two methods differ exactly when $445\\le m\\le499$: $55$ values in each block of $1000$. From $1$ to $10\\,000$ there are $10$ such blocks ($k=0$ to $9$; the number $10\\,000$ itself causes no difference), giving $10\\times55=550$. Forgetting that $445$ to $449$ round up to $450$ and then to $500$ gives $500$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "ED1-6", "paper": "ED1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>A spherical balloon is slowly leaking. It stays spherical, and during each hour its volume decreases by $27.1\\%$ of the volume it had at the start of that hour.<\/p><p>By what percentage has its surface area decreased after $2$ hours?<\/p>", "opts": ["$34.39\\%$", "$38\\%$", "$36.14\\%$", "$46.86\\%$", "$19\\%$", "$18.07\\%$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Each hour the volume is multiplied by $0.729=0.9^3$. The balloon stays spherical, so all its shapes are similar: the radius is multiplied by $0.9$ each hour and the surface area by $0.9^2=0.81$.<\/p><p>After $2$ hours the surface area has been multiplied by $0.81^2=0.6561$, which is a decrease of $34.39\\%$. Adding the hourly decreases ($2\\times19\\%=38\\%$) ignores compounding, $46.86\\%$ is the decrease in volume rather than area, and $18.07\\%$ comes from wrongly taking two thirds of the volume percentage.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio", "geometry"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "ED1-7", "paper": "ED1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>The graph of $y=x^2+bx+c$, where $b$ and $c$ are constants, passes through the point $(4,10)$, and its turning point lies on the line $y=x$.<\/p><p>What is the sum of all the possible values of $c$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$44$", "$49$", "$-14$", "$84$", "$42$", "$7$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Completing the square, a curve of this form with turning point $(h,h)$ is $y=(x-h)^2+h$. Passing through $(4,10)$ requires $(4-h)^2+h=10$, so $h^2-7h+6=0$ and $h=1$ or $h=6$.<\/p><p>Expanding, $c=h^2+h$, which is $2$ or $42$. The sum is $44$. The value $7$ is the sum of the possible values of $h$, and $-14$ is the sum of the possible values of $b$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "ED1-8", "paper": "ED1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>A hiker leaves her camp $C$ and walks $3\\text{ km}$ in a straight line on a bearing of $050^\\circ$ to a point $P$. She then walks $3\\text{ km}$ in a straight line on a bearing of $170^\\circ$ to a point $Q$. The points $C$, $P$ and $Q$ are marked on a map of scale $1:25\\,000$.<\/p><p>What is the length of the line segment $QC$ on the map, and what is the bearing of $C$ from $Q$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$12\\sqrt3\\text{ cm}$ and $290^\\circ$", "$12\\text{ cm}$ and $230^\\circ$", "$12\\text{ cm}$ and $110^\\circ$", "$1.2\\text{ cm}$ and $290^\\circ$", "$120\\text{ cm}$ and $290^\\circ$", "$12\\text{ cm}$ and $290^\\circ$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED1-8.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Triangle CPQ with north lines at C, P and Q: CP is on a bearing of 50 degrees, CP = PQ = 3 km marked equal, the angles at P and Q are 60 degrees, and QC is highlighted.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>At $P$, the direction back to $C$ is on a bearing of $050^\\circ+180^\\circ=230^\\circ$ and the direction on to $Q$ is $170^\\circ$, so $\\angle CPQ=230^\\circ-170^\\circ=60^\\circ$. Since $PC=PQ=3\\text{ km}$, triangle $CPQ$ is isosceles with a $60^\\circ$ apex angle, so it is equilateral and $QC=3\\text{ km}=300\\,000\\text{ cm}$, which is $\\dfrac{300\\,000}{25\\,000}=12\\text{ cm}$ on the map.<\/p><p>At $Q$, the bearing back to $P$ is $170^\\circ+180^\\circ=350^\\circ$, and $C$ is reached by turning a further $60^\\circ$ anticlockwise (the triangle lies to the west of $PQ$, since $C$ is west of $Q$), giving $350^\\circ-60^\\circ=290^\\circ$. The bearing $110^\\circ$ is that of $Q$ from $C$, the reverse direction.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "geometry"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "ED1-9", "paper": "ED1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A combination lock has four dials, each showing one of the digits $0$ to $9$, so that a code is an ordered list of four digits, such as $0\\,4\\,9\\,1$.<\/p><p>How many codes have the property that the product of their four digits is $36$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$48$", "$72$", "$60$", "$36$", "$84$", "$66$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Since $36=2^2\\times3^2$, the only usable digits are $1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9$. List the multisets of four digits with product $36$ systematically by the largest digit: \\[\\{1,1,4,9\\},\\ \\{1,2,2,9\\},\\ \\{1,1,6,6\\},\\ \\{1,2,3,6\\},\\ \\{1,3,3,4\\},\\ \\{2,2,3,3\\}.\\] Their numbers of different orders are $12, 12, 6, 24, 12, 6$, which total $72$.<\/p><p>The usual slips are missing $\\{2,2,3,3\\}$ (the only one with no $1$) or $\\{1,1,6,6\\}$, either of which gives $66$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "prob"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "ED1-10", "paper": "ED1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>Jug $A$ contains $600\\text{ ml}$ of a drink that is $20\\%$ fruit concentrate by volume, and jug $B$ contains $400\\text{ ml}$ of a drink that is $50\\%$ concentrate by volume. First, $200\\text{ ml}$ is poured from $A$ into $B$ and mixed thoroughly. Then some of the mixture in $B$ is poured back into $A$ and mixed thoroughly, after which the drink in $A$ is $30\\%$ concentrate. Assume that volumes simply add when liquids are mixed.<\/p><p>How much was poured back from $B$ into $A$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$500\\text{ ml}$", "$400\\text{ ml}$", "$600\\text{ ml}$", "$300\\text{ ml}$", "$250\\text{ ml}$", "$200\\text{ ml}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>After the first pour, $A$ holds $400\\text{ ml}$ containing $80\\text{ ml}$ of concentrate, and $B$ holds $600\\text{ ml}$ containing $200+40=240\\text{ ml}$, so $B$ is now $40\\%$ concentrate. Pouring back $v\\text{ ml}$ gives \\[\\frac{80+0.4v}{400+v}=0.3,\\] so $80+0.4v=120+0.3v$ and $v=400$.<\/p><p>Using $50\\%$ for $B$, forgetting that it was diluted, gives $200\\text{ ml}$; forgetting that $A$ lost $200\\text{ ml}$ gives $600\\text{ ml}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["ratio"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "ED1-11", "paper": "ED1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>A cyclist rides at a constant speed along a long straight road. Buses run along the road in both directions. In each direction the buses leave their depot at the same regular time interval, and every bus travels at the same constant speed, which is greater than the cyclist's speed. The cyclist notices that she meets an oncoming bus every $6$ minutes and is overtaken by a bus every $12$ minutes.<\/p><p>At what time interval do the buses leave each depot?<\/p>", "opts": ["$7.5$ minutes", "$4.5$ minutes", "$9$ minutes", "$10$ minutes", "$6$ minutes", "$8$ minutes"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Let the buses travel at speed $V$, the cyclist at speed $u$, and let the buses leave every $T$ minutes, so that buses travelling in the same direction are a distance $d=VT$ apart. Oncoming buses close on her at $V+u$ and overtaking buses at $V-u$, so \\[\\frac{d}{V+u}=6\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\frac{d}{V-u}=12.\\] Hence $V+u=\\dfrac d6$ and $V-u=\\dfrac d{12}$; adding, $2V=\\dfrac d4$, so $V=\\dfrac d8$ and $T=\\dfrac dV=8$ minutes.<\/p><p>The interval is the harmonic mean of $6$ and $12$, not their ordinary mean $9$. (The cyclist rides at a third of the buses' speed.)<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "ratio"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "ED1-12", "paper": "ED1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Two non-empty data sets $X$ and $Y$, each consisting of real numbers, are combined into a single data set $Z$, which contains every value of $X$ and every value of $Y$ (repeated values are kept). Which of the following statements must be true?<\/p><ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>The mean of $Z$ lies between the mean of $X$ and the mean of $Y$, inclusive.<\/li><li>The median of $Z$ lies between the median of $X$ and the median of $Y$, inclusive.<\/li><li>The range of $Z$ is at least as large as the range of $X$, and at least as large as the range of $Y$.<\/li><li>Every mode of $Z$ is a mode of $X$ or a mode of $Y$.<\/li><\/ol>", "opts": ["I, III and IV only", "I only", "I, II and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only", "I and III only", "III only", "I and II only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I is true: the mean of $Z$ is a weighted average of the two means. III is true: $Z$ contains the smallest and largest values of both sets.<\/p><p>II is true. Say $m_X\\le m_Y$. At least half of $X$ and at least half of $Y$ lie at or below $m_Y$, so at least half of $Z$ does; likewise at least half of $Z$ lies at or above $m_X$. If $Z$ has an odd number of values, this traps its middle value between $m_X$ and $m_Y$. If it has an even number, the only delicate case is when exactly half of $Z$ lies at or below $m_Y$; then the two middle values of $Z$ are at most the two middle values of $Y$, or at most the two middle values of $X$, so their mean is at most $m_Y$. The lower bound is similar.<\/p><p>IV is false: $X=\\{1,1,2,3\\}$ and $Y=\\{3,4,5,5\\}$ have modes $1$ and $5$, but in $Z$ the values $1$, $3$ and $5$ each occur twice, so $3$ is also a mode of $Z$.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "ED1-13", "paper": "ED1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>A drone flies in a straight line from a base to a beacon and then straight back, at the same constant airspeed (its speed relative to the air) throughout; ignore the time taken to turn round. A steady wind of $10\\text{ km/h}$ blows directly from the base towards the beacon, so the drone's speed over the ground is its airspeed plus $10\\text{ km/h}$ on the way out and its airspeed minus $10\\text{ km/h}$ on the way back. The round trip takes $25\\%$ longer than it would in still air.<\/p><p>What is the drone's airspeed, in km/h?<\/p>", "opts": ["$20\\sqrt2$", "$10\\sqrt6$", "$20$", "$10\\sqrt5$", "$25$", "$10\\sqrt3$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Let the airspeed be $v$ and the one-way distance $d$. Then \\[\\frac{d}{v+10}+\\frac{d}{v-10}=1.25\\times\\frac{2d}{v}.\\] The left side is $\\dfrac{2dv}{v^2-100}$, so $v^2=1.25\\left(v^2-100\\right)$, giving $0.25v^2=125$, $v^2=500$ and $v=10\\sqrt5\\approx22.4$.<\/p><p>The wind does not cancel out over the round trip: the drone spends longer on the slow leg, so the head wind costs more time than the tail wind saves.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "algebra"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "ED1-14", "paper": "ED1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>The times, $t$ minutes, that a sample of customers spent in a shop are shown in a histogram with three bars. The bar for $0\\le t&lt;10$ has frequency density $2$ customers per minute, the bar for $10\\le t&lt;15$ has frequency density $6$ customers per minute, and the bar for $15\\le t&lt; b$ has frequency density $2.5$ customers per minute, where the upper class boundary $b$ has been smudged and cannot be read. Using linear interpolation within classes, the median time is estimated to be $20$ minutes.<\/p><p>Using class midpoints, what is the estimated mean time, in minutes?<\/p>", "opts": ["$22.5$", "$20$", "$19.4$", "$27.25$", "$23.6$", "$21.8$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Frequency is frequency density times class width, so the first two classes contain $2\\times10=20$ and $6\\times5=30$ customers, and the last contains $2.5(b-15)$. The median estimate $20$ lies $5$ minutes into the last class, so half of the total is $20+30+2.5\\times5=62.5$. The total is $125$, the last class contains $75$ customers, its width is $\\dfrac{75}{2.5}=30$, so $b=45$ and its midpoint is $30$.<\/p><p>The estimated mean is \\[\\frac{20\\times5+30\\times12.5+75\\times30}{125}=\\frac{2725}{125}=21.8.\\] The median, $20$, is not the mean: the long last class pulls the mean upwards.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "ED1-15", "paper": "ED1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>A robot starts at the origin of a coordinate grid. At each move it chooses one of the three displacement vectors \\[\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\mathbf{q}=\\begin{pmatrix}-1\\\\3\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\mathbf{r}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}\\] and moves through that displacement. Each vector may be used any number of times, in any order.<\/p><p>What is the smallest number of moves after which the robot can be at the point $(11,9)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$11$", "$23$", "$15$", "$7$", "$9$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Only the number of times each vector is used matters, not the order. Using $\\mathbf p$, $\\mathbf q$, $\\mathbf r$ respectively $a$, $b$, $c$ times, we need $2a-b+c=11$ and $a+3b-c=9$. Adding eliminates $c$: $3a+2b=20$, so with $a,b\\ge0$ the pair $(a,b)$ is $(0,10)$, $(2,7)$, $(4,4)$ or $(6,1)$, and then $c=11-2a+b$ is $21$, $14$, $7$ or $0$.<\/p><p>The total numbers of moves $a+b+c$ are $31$, $23$, $15$ and $7$, so the least is $7$: six uses of $\\mathbf p$ and one of $\\mathbf q$, since $6\\mathbf p+\\mathbf q=\\begin{pmatrix}11\\\\9\\end{pmatrix}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "algebra"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "ED1-16", "paper": "ED1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>A security guard patrols the six corners of a hexagonal courtyard. She starts at one corner, and every minute she walks along a side to one of the two neighbouring corners, choosing each of the two with probability $\\tfrac12$, independently of all her previous choices.<\/p><p>What is the probability that she is back at her starting corner after exactly $6$ minutes?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{3}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{11}{64}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{32}$", "$\\dfrac{11}{32}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{16}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Record each minute as $+1$ (clockwise) or $-1$ (anticlockwise). All $2^6=64$ sequences are equally likely. She is back at the start exactly when the total is a multiple of $6$, so $0$ or $\\pm6$.<\/p><p>A total of $0$ needs three steps each way: $\\binom63=20$ sequences. A total of $\\pm6$ means walking all the way round in one direction: $2$ sequences. The probability is $\\dfrac{22}{64}=\\dfrac{11}{32}$. Forgetting the two complete laps gives $\\dfrac{20}{64}=\\dfrac5{16}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "ED1-17", "paper": "ED1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>How many different straight lines pass through the point $(2,3)$ and form, together with the $x$-axis and the $y$-axis, a triangle of area $12$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$0$", "$1$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED1-17.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Three lines through the point (2,3), each cutting off a triangle of area 12 with the coordinate axes: one in the first quadrant with intercepts 4 and 6, one in the second quadrant and a steep one in the fourth quadrant.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>In the figure, the blue triangle comes from the double root and the two orange triangles from the other quadratic.<\/p><p>Vertical and horizontal lines, and the line through the origin, do not form a triangle, so take the line $y-3=m(x-2)$ with $m\\ne0$. Its intercepts are $x=2-\\dfrac3m$ and $y=3-2m$, and their product is $\\left(2-\\dfrac3m\\right)(3-2m)=12-4m-\\dfrac9m$. The area is $12$ when this product is $24$ or $-24$.<\/p><p>Product $24$: $4m^2+12m+9=0$, so $(2m+3)^2=0$ and $m=-\\tfrac32$, a single line (in fact $12$ is the least possible area of a triangle in the first quadrant cut off by a line through $(2,3)$, and only this line achieves it). Product $-24$: $4m^2-36m+9=0$, whose discriminant $1296-144$ is positive, giving two lines with positive gradient. That is $3$ lines in total. Ignoring the sign of the intercepts gives $1$, and expecting two roots from each quadratic gives $4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "ED1-18", "paper": "ED1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>At exactly 12 noon, two analogue clocks, each with a $12$-hour dial, are both set to show the correct time. One clock runs fast, gaining $3$ minutes every hour; the other runs slow, losing $2$ minutes every hour. Each clock keeps running at its own constant rate.<\/p><p>When the two clocks next show exactly the same time as each other, what time do they show?<\/p>", "opts": ["2:24", "12:00", "6:00", "7:12", "4:48", "9:36"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The fast clock gains $5$ minutes per real hour on the slow one. Two $12$-hour dials show the same time when the gap between the clocks is a whole number of $12$ hours, which is $720$ minutes. This first happens after $\\dfrac{720}{5}=144$ hours, exactly $6$ days, when the real time is noon again.<\/p><p>By then the fast clock has gained $3\\times144=432$ minutes, which is $7$ hours $12$ minutes, so it shows 7:12. Check: the slow clock has lost $288$ minutes, which is $4$ hours $48$ minutes, and $12$ hours minus $4$ hours $48$ minutes is also 7:12. The answer 12:00 is the real time, not what the clocks show.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "ratio"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "ED1-19", "paper": "ED1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>The transformation $T$ consists of a reflection in the line $y=x$ followed by a rotation through $90^\\circ$ clockwise about the point $(2,0)$. The point $P_0=(1,0)$ is mapped by $T$ to $P_1$, then $P_1$ is mapped by $T$ to $P_2$, and so on, so that $P_{k+1}$ is the image of $P_k$ under $T$ for every $k\\ge0$.<\/p><p>What are the coordinates of $P_{2026}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$(4051,\\,2)$", "$(1,\\,4052)$", "$(4053,\\,0)$", "$(4055,\\,0)$", "$(4053,\\,2)$", "$(2027,\\,0)$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Find $T$ as a formula. The reflection sends $(x,y)$ to $(y,x)$. The rotation through $90^\\circ$ clockwise about $(2,0)$ sends $(u,v)$ to $(2+v,\\,2-u)$. So \\[T(x,y)=(2+x,\\,2-y),\\] a translation of $2$ units to the right combined with a reflection in the line $y=1$.<\/p><p>Applying $T$ twice gives $(x,y)\\mapsto(x+4,\\,y)$, a translation of $4$ units to the right. So $P_{2k}=(1+4k,\\,0)$, and $P_{2026}=(1+4\\times1013,\\,0)=(4053,0)$. The point $(4051,2)$ is $P_{2025}$, the off-by-one trap.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "ED1-20", "paper": "ED1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>In a city whose streets form a square grid, the <em>taxi distance<\/em> between the points $(x_1,y_1)$ and $(x_2,y_2)$ is defined to be $|x_1-x_2|+|y_1-y_2|$. Let $A=(0,0)$ and $B=(4,2)$.<\/p><p>How many points $P$ with integer coordinates are at the same taxi distance from $A$ as from $B$, with this common taxi distance at most $8$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$11$", "$7$", "$13$", "$15$", "$12$", "$17$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED1-20.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The set of points at equal taxi distance from A(0,0) and B(4,2): a vertical ray upwards from (1,2), the segment from (1,2) to (3,0) and a vertical ray downwards from (3,0), with the 13 counted lattice points marked from (1,7) down to (3,-5).\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Solve $|x|+|y|=|x-4|+|y-2|$ region by region. For $y\\ge2$ the $y$ terms differ by exactly $2$, so $|x|-|x-4|=-2$, which gives $x=1$: a vertical ray upwards from $(1,2)$. For $y\\le0$, similarly $|x|-|x-4|=2$, giving $x=3$: a vertical ray downwards from $(3,0)$. For $0\\le y\\le2$ the equation becomes $x+y=3$ with $1\\le x\\le3$: the segment from $(1,2)$ to $(3,0)$.<\/p><p>The common distance is $1+y$ on the upper ray, $3$ on the segment and $3+|y|$ on the lower ray. With distance at most $8$: the upper ray gives $y=2,\\dots,7$ ($6$ points), the segment adds $(2,1)$, and the lower ray gives $y=0,-1,\\dots,-5$ ($6$ points). The total is $13$. Unlike the ordinary perpendicular bisector, this set of equidistant points bends.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "number"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "ED1-21", "paper": "ED1", "n": 21, "q": "<p>Raffle tickets numbered $1, 2, 3, \\dots, n$ were sold, each number appearing on exactly one ticket. One ticket was then lost. The mean of the numbers on the remaining $n-1$ tickets is $31.25$.<\/p><p>What number was on the lost ticket?<\/p>", "opts": ["$61$", "$6$", "$46$", "$15$", "$16$", "$31$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Removing one ticket from $1, 2, \\dots, n$ leaves a mean between $\\dfrac n2$ (when $n$ is removed) and $\\dfrac{n+2}2$ (when $1$ is removed). So $\\dfrac n2\\le31.25\\le\\dfrac{n+2}2$, giving $60.5\\le n\\le62.5$, so $n$ is $61$ or $62$.<\/p><p>The total of the remaining tickets, $31.25(n-1)=\\tfrac{125}{4}(n-1)$, must be a whole number, so $n-1$ is a multiple of $4$ and $n=61$. The remaining total is $31.25\\times60=1875$, while $1+2+\\dots+61=\\tfrac12\\times61\\times62=1891$. The lost ticket is $1891-1875=16$.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats", "number"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "ED1-22", "paper": "ED1", "n": 22, "q": "<p>A sequence of positive numbers $u_1, u_2, u_3, \\dots$ satisfies \\[u_{n+2}=\\frac{u_{n+1}+1}{u_n}\\qquad\\text{for every } n\\ge1.\\] It is given that $u_{20}=4$ and $u_{21}=6$.<\/p><p>What is the value of $u_{2028}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{5}{6}$", "$4$", "$\\dfrac{11}{24}$", "$6$", "$\\dfrac{24}{11}$", "$\\dfrac{7}{4}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>There is no formula to look up, so generate terms and look for a pattern. From $u_{20}=4$ and $u_{21}=6$: \\[u_{22}=\\frac{6+1}{4}=\\frac74,\\quad u_{23}=\\frac{\\tfrac74+1}{6}=\\frac{11}{24},\\quad u_{24}=\\frac{\\tfrac{11}{24}+1}{\\tfrac74}=\\frac56,\\quad u_{25}=\\frac{\\tfrac56+1}{\\tfrac{11}{24}}=4,\\quad u_{26}=\\frac{4+1}{\\tfrac56}=6.\\] Since each term is determined by the two before it, and $u_{25}, u_{26}$ equal $u_{20}, u_{21}$, the sequence repeats with period $5$ from then on.<\/p><p>Now $2028-23=2005=5\\times401$, so $u_{2028}=u_{23}=\\dfrac{11}{24}$. Miscounting the position in the cycle by one gives $\\dfrac74$ or $\\dfrac56$, and $\\dfrac{24}{11}$ inverts the fraction.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "ED1-23", "paper": "ED1", "n": 23, "q": "<p>A calculator displays the number $1$. It has two special buttons. Button $F$ replaces the displayed number $x$ by $x+1$, and button $G$ replaces the displayed number $x$ by $\\dfrac{x}{x+1}$.<\/p><p>What is the smallest number of button presses after which the display can show $\\dfrac{29}{12}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$9$", "$7$", "$8$", "$5$", "$6$", "The display can never show $\\dfrac{29}{12}$."], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Work backwards. Every number displayed is positive, a press of $F$ always gives a number greater than $1$, and a press of $G$ always gives a number less than $1$. So from any displayed number other than the starting $1$, the previous number is forced: if the number exceeds $1$ the last press was $F$ (subtract $1$), and if it is less than $1$ the last press was $G$ (undo it by $y\\mapsto\\dfrac{y}{1-y}$).<\/p><p>This gives \\[\\tfrac{29}{12}\\leftarrow\\tfrac{17}{12}\\leftarrow\\tfrac{5}{12}\\leftarrow\\tfrac{5}{7}\\leftarrow\\tfrac{5}{2}\\leftarrow\\tfrac{3}{2}\\leftarrow\\tfrac{1}{2}\\leftarrow1,\\] which is $7$ presses (in order $G, F, F, G, G, F, F$). Since every backward step was forced, there is no other route, let alone a shorter one. The process is the Euclidean algorithm in disguise: $29=2\\times12+5$, $12=2\\times5+2$, $5=2\\times2+1$, $2=2\\times1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number", "algebra"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "ED1-24", "paper": "ED1", "n": 24, "q": "<p>Ana and Ben watch a fair coin being tossed repeatedly. Ana wins as soon as two consecutive tosses are both heads. Ben wins as soon as a tail is immediately followed by a head. The tossing stops as soon as one of them has won.<\/p><p>What is the probability that Ana wins?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac13$", "$\\dfrac23$", "$\\dfrac34$", "$\\dfrac38$", "$\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Look at the first two tosses. If they are both heads, Ana wins at once, with probability $\\tfrac14$. Otherwise a tail has appeared by the second toss, and from then on Ana cannot win: she needs a head that comes straight after another head, but the first head after any tail comes straight after a tail, which completes Ben's pattern and ends the game.<\/p><p>So Ana wins exactly when the first two tosses are heads, and the probability is $\\dfrac14$. The tempting $\\dfrac12$ assumes that the two patterns, each of probability $\\tfrac14$ at any given pair of tosses, are equally strong.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 8.3}, {"id": "ED1-25", "paper": "ED1", "n": 25, "q": "<p>Two squares, each of side length $1$, have the same centre. One is obtained from the other by a rotation through $30^\\circ$ about this centre.<\/p><p>What is the area of the region that lies inside both squares?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2-\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{3+\\sqrt3}{6}$", "$\\sqrt3-1$", "$\\dfrac56$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$", "$2\\sqrt2-2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED1-25.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Two unit squares with the same centre, one rotated by 30 degrees; their common octagon is shaded, one 30-60-90 corner triangle is highlighted, and the bottom side of the upright square is split into lengths c/2, c and root 3 c/2.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The common region is an octagon, obtained from either square by cutting off a small right-angled triangle at each of its four corners. Each cut is made by a side of the other square, which is at $30^\\circ$ to the sides it crosses, so each corner triangle has angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, $90^\\circ$. By symmetry all eight such triangles (four from each square) are congruent; call their hypotenuse $c$, so their legs are $\\tfrac12c$ and $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2c$.<\/p><p>Each side of the first square consists of a leg of one corner triangle, a side of the octagon (which is the hypotenuse of a corner triangle of the second square) and a leg of another corner triangle. So $\\tfrac12c+c+\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2c=1$, giving $c=\\dfrac{2}{3+\\sqrt3}=\\dfrac{3-\\sqrt3}{3}$ and $c^2=\\dfrac{4-2\\sqrt3}{3}$. The four corner triangles have total area $4\\times\\tfrac12\\times\\tfrac12c\\times\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2c=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}c^2=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3-3}{3}$, so the common area is $1-\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3-3}{3}=2-\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3}{3}\\approx0.845$. The value $2\\sqrt2-2$ is the answer for a $45^\\circ$ rotation.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "ED1-26", "paper": "ED1", "n": 26, "q": "<p>The numbers $1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7$ are written in a row in some order. The row is called <em>connected<\/em> if every number after the first differs by exactly $1$ from at least one of the numbers to its left. For example, $4\\,3\\,5\\,6\\,2\\,1\\,7$ is connected, but $4\\,3\\,6\\,5\\,2\\,1\\,7$ is not, because $6$ differs by more than $1$ from each of $4$ and $3$.<\/p><p>How many connected rows are there?<\/p>", "opts": ["$64$", "$32$", "$42$", "$128$", "$720$", "$49$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>In a connected row, the numbers written so far always form a block of consecutive integers: a new number differs by $1$ from something already written and is not already in the block, so it must be one less than the smallest or one more than the largest. Conversely, any row built by extending the block at one end or the other is connected.<\/p><p>Read the row from the right. The last number must be an end of the full block, so it is $1$ or $7$: two choices. Removing it leaves a block, and the second last number must be one of its two ends, and so on. There are two choices for each of the last six numbers, and the first number is then forced, giving $2^6=64$ rows. (Equivalently, starting with $a$ there are $\\binom{6}{a-1}$ ways to interleave the downward and upward extensions, and these sum to $2^6$.)<\/p>", "tags": ["prob", "number"], "rating": 8.5}, {"id": "ED1-27", "paper": "ED1", "n": 27, "q": "<p>A square array of $25$ dots is arranged in $5$ rows and $5$ columns, with neighbouring dots $1\\text{ cm}$ apart horizontally and vertically.<\/p><p>How many squares, of any size and in any orientation, have all four of their vertices at dots of the array?<\/p>", "opts": ["$40$", "$48$", "$54$", "$30$", "$60$", "$50$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED1-27.svg?v=1\" alt=\"A 5 by 5 array of dots with an upright bounding square of side k (dashed) and a tilted square inside it whose vertices lie a distance j along each side of the bounding square.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Every such square has an upright bounding square (sides parallel to the rows and columns) whose corners are dots, and each vertex of the square lies on a different side of the bounding square. If the bounding square has side $k\\text{ cm}$, then going round it, the vertices sit at the same distance $j$ along each side, where $j=0, 1, \\dots, k-1$ (with $j=0$ giving the upright square itself). So each upright $k\\times k$ bounding square holds exactly $k$ squares.<\/p><p>There are $(5-k)^2$ upright squares of side $k$ in the array, so the total is \\[\\sum_{k=1}^{4}k(5-k)^2=1\\times16+2\\times9+3\\times4+4\\times1=50.\\] Counting only the upright squares gives $16+9+4+1=30$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "number"], "rating": 8.7}, {"id": "ED2-1", "paper": "ED2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>The curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is transformed by the following three transformations, applied in this order: first a translation of $3$ units in the positive $x$-direction; then a stretch parallel to the $x$-axis with scale factor $\\tfrac12$ (the $y$-axis being invariant); then a reflection in the $y$-axis. The resulting curve has equation $y=g(x)$. What is the value of $g(-5)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\sqrt{\\tfrac{11}{2}}$", "$\\sqrt{7}$", "$g(-5)$ is not defined", "$4$", "$\\sqrt{13}$", "$2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Each transformation acts on $x$ in the current equation. The translation gives $y=\\sqrt{x-3}$. A stretch parallel to the $x$-axis with scale factor $\\tfrac12$ replaces $x$ by $2x$, giving $y=\\sqrt{2x-3}$. The reflection in the $y$-axis replaces $x$ by $-x$, giving $g(x)=\\sqrt{-2x-3}$, so $g(-5)=\\sqrt{7}$.<\/p><p>Applying the stretch as if it acted on the whole bracket $x-3$ gives $\\sqrt{-2x-6}$ and the answer $2$; translating the wrong way gives $\\sqrt{-2x+3}$ and $\\sqrt{13}$; replacing $x$ by $\\tfrac{x}{2}$ for the stretch gives $\\sqrt{-\\tfrac{x}{2}-3}$, which is undefined at $-5$.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "ED2-2", "paper": "ED2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>In an experiment, two positive quantities $x$ and $y$ are measured. A technician plots $\\log_2 y$ on the vertical axis against $\\log_4 x$ on the horizontal axis, and finds that the points lie exactly on a straight line of gradient $3$ which passes through the point $(1,5)$ of this plot. For which value of $x$ is $y=x$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac18$", "$\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac{1}{16}$", "$16$", "$\\dfrac{1}{64}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The line is $\\log_2 y=3\\log_4 x+2$. Since $\\log_4 x=\\tfrac12\\log_2 x$, this is $\\log_2 y=\\tfrac32\\log_2 x+2$, so $y=4x^{3/2}$. Then $y=x$ gives $4x^{1/2}=1$, so $x=\\tfrac{1}{16}$.<\/p><p>The trap is to treat the horizontal axis as $\\log_2 x$, which gives $y=4x^3$ and the answer $\\tfrac12$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "ED2-3", "paper": "ED2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>The point $P(16,4)$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$. The tangent to the curve at $P$ meets the $x$-axis at $T$, and the normal to the curve at $P$ meets the $x$-axis at $N$. What is the area of the triangle $PTN$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$128$", "$65$", "$66$", "$64$", "$\\dfrac{65}{2}$", "$130$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED2-3.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The curve y = root x with the tangent and normal at P(16,4) meeting the x-axis at T(-16,0) and N(16.5,0); the thin triangle PTN with its right angle at P is shaded, drawn to true scale.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Here $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\tfrac12x^{-1/2}=\\tfrac18$ at $P$. The tangent $y-4=\\tfrac18(x-16)$ meets $y=0$ at $x=-16$. The normal has gradient $-8$, so $y-4=-8(x-16)$ meets $y=0$ at $x=16.5$. The base $TN$ is $32.5$ and the height is $4$, so the area is $65$.<\/p><p>Using $-\\tfrac18$ as the normal gradient (forgetting the reciprocal) puts $N$ at $x=48$ and gives $128$; forgetting the half gives $130$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "ED2-4", "paper": "ED2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A workshop's weekly profit, in thousands of pounds, when it produces $x$ hundred components is modelled by \\[P(x)=-x^3+9x^2-23x+15,\\qquad x>0.\\] For which values of $x$ does the model predict a profit, that is, $P(x)>0$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$3&lt;x&lt;5$ only", "$0&lt;x&lt;1$ or $3&lt;x&lt;5$", "$1&lt;x&lt;3$ or $x>5$", "$1&lt;x&lt;5$", "$1&lt;x&lt;3$ only", "$0&lt;x&lt;1$ or $x>5$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$P(1)=-1+9-23+15=0$, so $x-1$ is a factor, and division gives $P(x)=-(x-1)(x^2-8x+15)=-(x-1)(x-3)(x-5)$. So $P(x)>0$ exactly when $(x-1)(x-3)(x-5)&lt;0$, which is $x&lt;1$ or $3&lt;x&lt;5$. With $x>0$ the answer is $0&lt;x&lt;1$ or $3&lt;x&lt;5$.<\/p><p>Forgetting the leading minus sign reverses every region and gives $1&lt;x&lt;3$ or $x>5$; ignoring the small region near the origin gives $3&lt;x&lt;5$ only.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "ED2-5", "paper": "ED2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Find the sum of all the solutions of the equation \\[\\tan 2x\\,\\sin 2x=\\tfrac32\\] in the interval $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi$, where $x$ is in radians.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\pi$", "$3\\pi$", "$4\\pi$", "$6\\pi$", "$\\pi$", "$8\\pi$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Write $c=\\cos 2x$ (non-zero, as $\\tan 2x$ must be defined). Then $\\dfrac{\\sin^2 2x}{\\cos 2x}=\\tfrac32$ gives $2(1-c^2)=3c$, so $2c^2+3c-2=0$ and $(2c-1)(c+2)=0$. Only $c=\\tfrac12$ is possible. As $x$ runs over $[0,2\\pi]$, $2x$ runs over $[0,4\\pi]$, giving $2x=\\tfrac{\\pi}{3},\\tfrac{5\\pi}{3},\\tfrac{7\\pi}{3},\\tfrac{11\\pi}{3}$, so $x=\\tfrac{\\pi}{6},\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6},\\tfrac{7\\pi}{6},\\tfrac{11\\pi}{6}$, with sum $4\\pi$.<\/p><p>Working only over $0\\le 2x\\le 2\\pi$ gives $2\\pi$; summing the values of $2x$ instead of $x$ gives $8\\pi$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "ED2-6", "paper": "ED2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $x$ for which \\[\\left|x^3-4x\\right|&lt;3x.\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$-\\sqrt7&lt;x&lt;-1$ or $1&lt;x&lt;\\sqrt7$", "$0&lt;x&lt;\\sqrt7$", "$1&lt;x&lt;\\sqrt7$", "$1&lt;x&lt;7$", "$1&lt;x&lt;\\sqrt7$ and $x\\ne 2$", "$x&lt;-\\sqrt7$ or $1&lt;x&lt;\\sqrt7$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The left side is never negative, so we need $3x>0$, that is $x>0$. Then $\\left|x^3-4x\\right|=x\\left|x^2-4\\right|$, and dividing by $x>0$ gives $\\left|x^2-4\\right|&lt;3$, so $1&lt;x^2&lt;7$, that is $1&lt;x&lt;\\sqrt7$.<\/p><p>At $x=2$ the left side is $0&lt;6$, so $2$ is included. Dividing by $x$ without first noting $x>0$ produces the spurious negative interval.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg", "graphs"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "ED2-7", "paper": "ED2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>A family of circles is defined as follows: each circle has its centre on the parabola $y=\\tfrac14x^2$, and each is tangent to the line $y=-1$. There is exactly one point that lies on every circle of the family. What are its coordinates?<\/p>", "opts": ["$(0,0)$", "$(0,2)$", "$\\left(0,\\tfrac14\\right)$", "$(1,0)$", "$(0,1)$", "$(0,-1)$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED2-7.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The parabola y = x^2/4 and the line y = -1, with the circles centred at (0,0), (2,1) and (-2,1) each touching y = -1 and all passing through the point (0,1).\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>A circle with centre $\\left(t,\\tfrac14t^2\\right)$ touching $y=-1$ has radius $\\tfrac14t^2+1$. The squared distance from the centre to $(0,1)$ is $t^2+\\left(\\tfrac14t^2-1\\right)^2=\\left(\\tfrac14t^2+1\\right)^2$, so $(0,1)$ is on every circle: it is the focus of the parabola, whose directrix is $y=-1$.<\/p><p>The circles with $t=0$ and $t=\\pm2$ already have $(0,1)$ as their only common point. The point $\\left(0,\\tfrac14\\right)$ is the focus of $y=x^2$, not of this parabola.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "ED2-8", "paper": "ED2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>On a large table, two straight cushions lie along the positive $x$-axis and the positive $y$-axis. A small ball is struck from the point $A(2,3)$. It travels in straight lines, rebounds first off the cushion along the $y$-axis and then off the cushion along the $x$-axis, and then passes through the point $B(5,1)$. At each rebound the path makes equal angles with the cushion before and after impact. What is the total distance travelled by the ball from $A$ to $B$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\sqrt{65}$", "$\\sqrt{53}$", "$5$", "$\\sqrt{13}$", "$\\sqrt{85}$", "$9$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED2-8.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The ball's path from A(2,3) to the y-axis at (0,13/7), then to the x-axis at (13/4,0), then to B(5,1), with the reflected points (5,-1) and B''(-5,-1) and the dashed unfolded straight path to B''.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Unfold the path. Reflect $B$ in the $x$-axis (the last cushion) to $(5,-1)$, then reflect that in the $y$-axis to $B''(-5,-1)$. The ball's path has the same length as the straight segment $AB''$, which is $\\sqrt{7^2+4^2}=\\sqrt{65}$. Checking: $AB''$ meets the $y$-axis at $\\left(0,\\tfrac{13}{7}\\right)$ and the next leg meets the $x$-axis at $\\left(\\tfrac{13}{4},0\\right)$, both on the positive half-axes, so the path is genuine.<\/p><p>Reflecting in only one cushion gives $5$ or $\\sqrt{53}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "ED2-9", "paper": "ED2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A curve of constant width is constructed from an equilateral triangle of side $6$ cm: each side of the triangle is replaced by the minor circular arc, centred at the opposite vertex, that joins the two ends of that side. What is the area, in $\\text{cm}^2$, of the region enclosed by the three arcs?<\/p>", "opts": ["$18\\pi-27\\sqrt3$", "$18\\pi+9\\sqrt3$", "$18\\pi$", "$36\\pi-18\\sqrt3$", "$6\\pi+9\\sqrt3$", "$18\\pi-18\\sqrt3$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED2-9.svg?v=1\" alt=\"An equilateral triangle of side 6 with a 60 degree angle marked, and the curve of constant width formed by three arcs, each centred at the opposite vertex; the triangle is shaded blue and the three circular segments orange.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Each arc has radius $6$ and subtends $\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}$ at its centre, so each sector has area $\\tfrac12\\cdot36\\cdot\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}=6\\pi$, and each circular segment has area $6\\pi-9\\sqrt3$ (the triangle has area $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\cdot36=9\\sqrt3$). The region is the triangle plus three segments: $9\\sqrt3+3\\left(6\\pi-9\\sqrt3\\right)=18\\pi-18\\sqrt3$.<\/p><p>Adding three whole sectors to the triangle gives $18\\pi+9\\sqrt3$, counting the triangle four times; the three segments alone give $18\\pi-27\\sqrt3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "ED2-10", "paper": "ED2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>When the polynomial $p(x)$ is divided by $x^2-1$, the remainder is $x+3$. What is the remainder when $\\bigl(p(x)\\bigr)^3$ is divided by $x^2-1$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$28x+36$", "$36x+28$", "$27x+36$", "$28x+27$", "$x+27$", "$x^3+9x^2+27x+27$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The remainder $r(x)=ax+b$ agrees with $p(x)^3$ at the roots $x=\\pm1$ of $x^2-1$. From the given remainder, $p(1)=4$ and $p(-1)=2$, so $p(1)^3=64$ and $p(-1)^3=8$. Then $a+b=64$ and $-a+b=8$, so $b=36$ and $a=28$.<\/p><p>Equivalently, $(x+3)^3=x^3+9x^2+27x+27$ and replacing $x^2$ by $1$ gives $x+9+27x+27=28x+36$. Leaving the cube unreduced is not a remainder, since its degree is too large.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "ED2-11", "paper": "ED2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>A curve has gradient \\[\\frac{dy}{dx}=3x^2-6x\\] at every point $(x,y)$ on it, and the $x$-axis is a tangent to the curve. What is the area of the finite region enclosed between the curve and the $x$-axis?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$\\dfrac{27}{8}$", "$8$", "$6$", "$\\dfrac{27}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{27}{4}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED2-11.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The two possible curves y = x^2(x - 3), touching the x-axis at 0 and crossing at 3 with the region below the axis shaded, and y = (x + 1)(x - 2)^2, crossing at -1 and touching at 2 with the region above the axis shaded.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Integrating, $y=x^3-3x^2+c$. The $x$-axis is a tangent where $y=0$ and $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=0$, so at $x=0$ (giving $c=0$) or $x=2$ (giving $c=4$). There are two possible curves, $y=x^2(x-3)$ and $y=(x+1)(x-2)^2$.<\/p><p>For the first, the region is $0\\le x\\le3$ with area $\\left|\\int_0^3(x^3-3x^2)\\,dx\\right|=\\left|\\tfrac{81}{4}-27\\right|=\\tfrac{27}{4}$. For the second, the region is $-1\\le x\\le2$ with area $\\int_{-1}^2(x^3-3x^2+4)\\,dx=\\tfrac{27}{4}$ as well, so the answer is the same either way. Adding the two cases gives the trap $\\tfrac{27}{2}$; integrating the second curve from $0$ to $2$ gives $4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["int"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "ED2-12", "paper": "ED2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>A robot starts at the origin and moves $2$ m due east. It then turns $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise and moves $1$ m. It continues in this way forever: before each move it turns $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise, and each move is half the length of the previous move, so that the lengths of its moves form a monotonically decreasing geometric progression. The robot's position approaches a limiting point $L$. How far, in metres, is $L$ from the origin?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$2\\sqrt2$", "$\\dfrac{12}{5}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\sqrt5}{5}$", "$\\sqrt5$", "$\\dfrac85$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The moves go east $2$, north $1$, west $\\tfrac12$, south $\\tfrac14$, east $\\tfrac18$, and so on. The $x$-coordinate is $2-\\tfrac12+\\tfrac18-\\cdots$, a geometric series with ratio $-\\tfrac14$, so it tends to $\\dfrac{2}{1+\\frac14}=\\tfrac85$. Likewise $y=1-\\tfrac14+\\tfrac1{16}-\\cdots=\\tfrac45$. The distance is $\\sqrt{\\tfrac{64}{25}+\\tfrac{16}{25}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{80}}{5}=\\dfrac{4\\sqrt5}{5}$.<\/p><p>The total length travelled is $4$, which is the trap; $\\tfrac{12}{5}$ adds the coordinates instead of using Pythagoras.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "ED2-13", "paper": "ED2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>A regular polygon with $n$ sides is inscribed in a circle of radius $1$. What is the smallest value of $n$ for which the area of the polygon is greater than $3$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$", "$14$", "$13$", "$10$", "$11$", "$12$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The polygon splits into $n$ isosceles triangles with two sides $1$ and apex angle $\\tfrac{2\\pi}{n}$, so its area is $\\tfrac{n}{2}\\sin\\tfrac{2\\pi}{n}$. The areas increase with $n$ (each polygon gets closer to filling the circle), and for $n=12$ the area is exactly $6\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}=3$. So the first polygon with area greater than $3$ has $13$ sides.<\/p><p>No estimate of $\\pi$ is needed; the trap is to stop at $12$, where the area equals $3$ but does not exceed it.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "ED2-14", "paper": "ED2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A calculator display shows the number $2$. The calculator has only two working buttons: button $S$ replaces the number $x$ on the display by $x^2$, and button $H$ replaces it by $\\dfrac{x}{2}$. What is the smallest number of button presses that will make the display show $2^{100}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$9$", "$8$", "$10$", "$12$", "$7$", "$11$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Track $L=\\log_2(\\text{display})$, which starts at $1$. Button $S$ doubles $L$ and button $H$ subtracts $1$. Work backwards from $L=100$: an even value may come from half of it (by $S$) or from one more (by $H$), and an odd value can only come from one more. The efficient route is \\[1\\to2\\to4\\to8\\to7\\to14\\to13\\to26\\to25\\to50\\to100,\\] which takes $10$ presses.<\/p><p>Fewer is impossible. Six doublings reach at most $2^6=64$, so at least seven are needed. A subtraction made when $j$ doublings remain lowers the final value of $L$ by $2^j$, so with seven doublings the subtractions must account for $128-100=28=16+8+4$, which needs at least three of them (three $1$s in binary): $7+3=10$. More doublings only make things worse, since $256-100=156$ already needs four. An increment button would allow $8$ presses via $100=1100100_2$, but there is no increment here.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "ED2-15", "paper": "ED2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>The tangent to the curve $y=4-x^2$ at a point $P$ in the first quadrant (so both coordinates of $P$ are positive) forms a triangle with the positive coordinate axes. What is the least possible area of this triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{32\\sqrt3}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{64\\sqrt3}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{25}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{16}{3}$", "$8$", "$\\dfrac{16\\sqrt3}{9}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED2-15.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The curve y = 4 - x^2 in the first quadrant with the tangent at P(a, 4 - a^2) meeting the axes at a^2 + 4 and (a^2 + 4)/(2a), and the triangle it forms with the axes shaded.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>At $P\\left(a,4-a^2\\right)$ with $0&lt;a&lt;2$, the tangent is $y=-2ax+a^2+4$. Its intercepts are $a^2+4$ on the $y$-axis and $\\dfrac{a^2+4}{2a}$ on the $x$-axis, so the area is \\[A=\\frac{(a^2+4)^2}{4a}=\\frac{a^3}{4}+2a+\\frac4a.\\] Then $\\dfrac{dA}{da}=\\tfrac34a^2+2-\\dfrac{4}{a^2}=0$ gives $3a^4+8a^2-16=0$, so $(3a^2-4)(a^2+4)=0$ and $a^2=\\tfrac43$. This gives $A=\\dfrac{(16/3)^2}{8/\\sqrt3}=\\dfrac{32\\sqrt3}{9}$.<\/p><p>Expanding into powers of $a$ avoids the quotient rule. The tempting $a=1$ gives $\\tfrac{25}{4}$, which is only slightly bigger.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "ED2-16", "paper": "ED2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>For which values of the real constant $c$ does the equation \\[\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{5-x}=c\\] have exactly two real solutions?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt2\\le c&lt;4$", "$c&lt;4$", "$0&lt;c&lt;4$", "$2\\sqrt2&lt;c&lt;4$", "$2\\le c&lt;4$", "$2\\sqrt2\\le c\\le4$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The left side $f(x)$ is defined for $-3\\le x\\le5$ and is positive, and \\[f(x)^2=8+2\\sqrt{(x+3)(5-x)}=8+2\\sqrt{16-(x-1)^2}.\\] So $f$ is symmetric about $x=1$, rises from $f(-3)=2\\sqrt2$ to its maximum $f(1)=4$, then falls back to $f(5)=2\\sqrt2$. Each value of $c$ with $2\\sqrt2\\le c&lt;4$ is taken exactly twice (once on each side of $x=1$), $c=4$ once, and other values not at all.<\/p><p>The value $c=2\\sqrt2$ is included: it is attained at both endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=5$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg", "graphs"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "ED2-17", "paper": "ED2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>The region $R$ consists of all points $(x,y)$ in the plane satisfying \\[\\sqrt{|x|}+\\sqrt{|y|}\\le 2.\\] What is the area of $R$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$32$", "$\\dfrac83$", "$\\dfrac{16}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{32}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{64}{3}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED2-17.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The four-pointed star-shaped region root|x| + root|y| <= 2 with vertices at (plus or minus 4, 0) and (0, plus or minus 4), its first-quadrant quarter highlighted under the curve y = (2 - root x)^2 through (1,1), inside the dashed square |x| + |y| = 4.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The region is symmetric in both axes, so find the part in the first quadrant and multiply by $4$. There, $\\sqrt y\\le 2-\\sqrt x$ with $0\\le x\\le4$, that is $y\\le\\left(2-\\sqrt x\\right)^2=4-4x^{1/2}+x$. The area is \\[\\int_0^4\\left(4-4x^{1/2}+x\\right)dx=16-\\tfrac83\\cdot8+8=\\tfrac83,\\] so the total is $\\tfrac{32}{3}$.<\/p><p>The boundary curves inwards (the point $(1,1)$ is on it), so the area is less than that of the square $|x|+|y|\\le4$, which is $32$. Forgetting the factor $4$ gives $\\tfrac83$.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "graphs"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "ED2-18", "paper": "ED2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>$P$ and $Q$ are points on the parabola $y=x^2$ such that the tangents to the parabola at $P$ and at $Q$ are perpendicular to each other. What is the least possible length of the chord $PQ$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\sqrt2$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}$", "$\\dfrac14$", "$2$", "$1$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED2-18.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The parabola y = x^2 with a chord PQ whose tangents at P and Q meet at a right angle, the chord passing through the focus (0, 1/4), and the dashed horizontal chord from (-1/2, 1/4) to (1/2, 1/4).\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>In the figure, the dashed chord is the shortest one, with p = 1/2 and q = -1/2.<\/p><p>Let $P=(p,p^2)$ and $Q=(q,q^2)$. The tangent gradients are $2p$ and $2q$, so $4pq=-1$. Then \\[PQ^2=(p-q)^2\\left(1+(p+q)^2\\right).\\] Writing $s=p+q$, we have $(p-q)^2=s^2-4pq=s^2+1$, so $PQ^2=(1+s^2)^2$ and $PQ=1+s^2\\ge1$, with equality when $p=\\tfrac12$, $q=-\\tfrac12$.<\/p><p>This is the chord through the focus $\\left(0,\\tfrac14\\right)$ perpendicular to the axis: every such chord passes through the focus.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "diff"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "ED2-19", "paper": "ED2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>For a real constant $k$, consider the graph of \\[y=\\left|x^4-8x^2-k\\right|.\\] For how many integer values of $k$ does this graph have exactly three local maximum points?<\/p>", "opts": ["$17$", "$30$", "$14$", "$31$", "$15$", "$16$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED2-19.svg?v=1\" alt=\"For the example k = -8, the graph of y = |x^4 - 8x^2 + 8| with local maxima at (-2,8), (0,8) and (2,8), and the dashed parts of x^4 - 8x^2 + 8 below the axis with minima at (-2,-8) and (2,-8) that are reflected upwards.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The figure shows the case k = -8, which lies in the range found.<\/p><p>Let $g(x)=x^4-8x^2-k$. Since $g'(x)=4x(x^2-4)$, $g$ has a local maximum $g(0)=-k$ and local minima $g(\\pm2)=-16-k$. Taking the modulus keeps a local maximum of $g$ where $g>0$, turns a local minimum of $g$ where $g&lt;0$ into a local maximum, and creates only minima (corners) where $g$ crosses zero.<\/p><p>So $x=0$ gives a local maximum of $|g|$ exactly when $-k>0$, and $x=\\pm2$ give two exactly when $-16-k&lt;0$. Three maxima need $-16&lt;k&lt;0$, giving the $15$ integers $-15,\\dots,-1$. At $k=0$ the point $x=0$ becomes a minimum of $|g|$ (only $2$ maxima), and at $k=-16$ the minima touch the axis (only $1$).<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs", "diff"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "ED2-20", "paper": "ED2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>The polynomial $p(x)$ has degree $3$. It is known that $p(x)-1$ is divisible by $(x-1)^2$ and that $p(x)+1$ is divisible by $(x+1)^2$. What is the value of $p(3)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$-6$", "$18$", "$-\\dfrac{27}{2}$", "$-9$", "$9$", "$-18$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>A repeated factor $(x-1)^2$ of $p(x)-1$ means $p(1)=1$ and $p'(1)=0$; similarly $p(-1)=-1$ and $p'(-1)=0$. So the quadratic $p'(x)$ has roots $\\pm1$: $p'(x)=k(1-x^2)$ and $p(x)=k\\left(x-\\tfrac{x^3}{3}\\right)+d$. The conditions $p(1)=1$, $p(-1)=-1$ give $d=0$ and $\\tfrac{2k}{3}=1$, so $p(x)=\\tfrac12\\left(3x-x^3\\right)$ and $p(3)=\\tfrac12(9-27)=-9$.<\/p><p>A sign slip in $k$ gives $\\tfrac12\\left(x^3-3x\\right)$ and the answer $9$, but that polynomial has $p(1)=-1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg", "diff"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "ED2-21", "paper": "ED2", "n": 21, "q": "<p>The point $A_1(1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=x^3$. The tangent to the curve at $A_1$ meets the $x$-axis at $B_1$; the vertical line through $B_1$ meets the curve at $A_2$; the tangent at $A_2$ meets the $x$-axis at $B_2$; the vertical line through $B_2$ meets the curve at $A_3$; and so on indefinitely. For each $n$, let $C_n$ be the point on the $x$-axis directly below $A_n$, and let $T_n$ be the triangle $A_nB_nC_n$. What is the total area of all the triangles $T_1,T_2,T_3,\\dots$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{27}{130}$", "$\\dfrac16$", "$\\dfrac{9}{38}$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac{3}{10}$", "$\\dfrac14$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED2-21.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The curve y = x^3 for 0 <= x <= 1 with the shaded triangles T1, T2, T3 under it: each has vertex A_n on the curve, B_n where the tangent at A_n meets the x-axis, and C_n directly below A_n, with B1 = C2 and B2 = C3.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>At $A=(a,a^3)$ the tangent $y=a^3+3a^2(x-a)$ meets $y=0$ at $x=a-\\tfrac a3=\\tfrac{2a}{3}$. So the $x$-coordinates are $1,\\tfrac23,\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^2,\\dots$, and the triangle at $A=(a,a^3)$ has base $\\tfrac a3$ and height $a^3$, with area $\\tfrac{a^4}{6}$.<\/p><p>The areas form a geometric series with first term $\\tfrac16$ and ratio $\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^4=\\tfrac{16}{81}$, so the total is $\\dfrac{1/6}{1-16/81}=\\dfrac16\\cdot\\dfrac{81}{65}=\\dfrac{27}{130}$. As a check, this is less than $\\int_0^1x^3\\,dx=\\tfrac14$, since the triangles lie under the curve. Using ratio $\\tfrac23$ or $\\tfrac49$ gives $\\tfrac12$ or $\\tfrac{3}{10}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "seq"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "ED2-22", "paper": "ED2", "n": 22, "q": "<p>An analogue clock has an hour hand of length $3$ cm and a minute hand of length $5$ cm, both pivoted at the centre of the face and both turning continuously at constant rates. Consider the $12$-hour period that starts at noon, including the instant of noon but not the instant of midnight. On how many separate occasions during this period is the distance between the tips of the two hands a whole number of centimetres?<\/p>", "opts": ["$121$", "$132$", "$154$", "$110$", "$144$", "$77$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>If $\\theta$ is the angle between the hands, the cosine rule gives the distance $d$ with $d^2=34-30\\cos\\theta$, so $2\\le d\\le8$. The minute hand gains $11$ full turns on the hour hand in $12$ hours, so $\\theta$ goes through $11$ complete cycles.<\/p><p>In each cycle, $d$ takes each value strictly between $2$ and $8$ twice and the values $2$ and $8$ once each. The whole numbers $3,4,5,6,7$ therefore occur $22$ times each, and $2$ and $8$ occur $11$ times each (the hands coincide at noon but not at midnight within the period). The total is $5\\times22+2\\times11=132$.<\/p><p>Counting every whole number $22$ times gives $154$; leaving out $2$ and $8$ gives $110$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "ED2-23", "paper": "ED2", "n": 23, "q": "<p>A machine accepts a polynomial $p(x)$ and outputs the polynomial $p(x+1)-p(x)$. The polynomial $\\left(x^2+x\\right)^3$ is fed into the machine, the output is fed back into the machine, and this is repeated until the machine has been used five times in total. The final output is a linear polynomial $ax+b$. What is the value of $b$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1800$", "$720$", "$2880$", "$360$", "$1440$", "$2160$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The final output at $x=0$ is $b$, and it can be read off a difference table. The values of $p(x)=\\left(x(x+1)\\right)^3$ at $x=0,1,2,3,4,5$ are $0,8,216,1728,8000,27000$. Taking differences five times: \\[8,\\ 208,\\ 1512,\\ 6272,\\ 19000;\\quad 200,\\ 1304,\\ 4760,\\ 12728;\\] \\[1104,\\ 3456,\\ 7968;\\quad 2352,\\ 4512;\\quad 2160.\\] So $b=2160$.<\/p><p>Alternatively, the machine lowers the degree by one, sends $x^6$ to a polynomial that becomes $720x+1800$ after five uses, and sends $3x^5$ to the constant $3\\times120=360$ after five uses, while $3x^4+x^3$ disappears; again $b=1800+360=2160$. The leading term alone gives $1800$; $a=720$ is the trap of reporting the wrong coefficient.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 8.3}, {"id": "ED2-24", "paper": "ED2", "n": 24, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=x^3-21x$. For each real number $a\\ge0$, let $M(a)$ denote the greatest value taken by $f(x)$ on the interval $a\\le x\\le a+3$. What is the least value of $M(a)$ over all $a\\ge0$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$-14\\sqrt7$", "$-36$", "$-16$", "$-22$", "$-20$", "$0$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Since $f'(x)=3x^2-21$, $f$ decreases on $\\left[0,\\sqrt7\\right]$ and increases for $x\\ge\\sqrt7$. If $a>\\sqrt7$ then $f$ increases on the whole interval and $M(a)=f(a+3)>f\\left(\\sqrt7+3\\right)$, which is larger than values found below. For $0\\le a\\le\\sqrt7$ the interval contains $\\sqrt7$, so the greatest value is at an end: $M(a)=\\max\\bigl(f(a),f(a+3)\\bigr)$.<\/p><p>As $a$ increases, $f(a)$ falls and $f(a+3)$ rises, so the larger of the two is least where they are equal. Now $f(a+3)-f(a)=9a^2+27a-36=9(a-1)(a+4)$, so they are equal at $a=1$, and $M(1)=f(1)=f(4)=-20$.<\/p><p>The trap is the minimum value of $f$ itself, $f\\left(\\sqrt7\\right)=-14\\sqrt7$: that is the least value on the interval, not the greatest. Taking $a=0$ and reading off $f(3)=-36$ makes the same mistake.<\/p>", "tags": ["diff", "graphs"], "rating": 8.3}, {"id": "ED2-25", "paper": "ED2", "n": 25, "q": "<p>You may use the fact that $\\log_{10}2=0.30103$, correct to $5$ decimal places. How many of the $100$ numbers \\[2^1,\\ 2^2,\\ 2^3,\\ \\dots,\\ 2^{100}\\] have first (leftmost) digit equal to $1$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$33$", "$25$", "$31$", "$30$", "$10$", "$29$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Doubling a number with $d$ digits gives a number with $d$ or $d+1$ digits, and in the second case the new number lies between $10^d$ and $2\\times10^d$, so it starts with $1$. Conversely, if $2^n$ lies between $10^d$ and $2\\times10^d$, then $2^{n-1}$ lies between $5\\times10^{d-1}$ and $10^d$, so it has one digit fewer. Hence among $2^2,\\dots,2^{100}$ the powers starting with $1$ are exactly those where the digit count increases.<\/p><p>Since $100\\log_{10}2=30.103$, $2^{100}$ has $31$ digits, while $2^1$ has $1$ digit. The digit count rises $30$ times, so the answer is $30$. Benford's law suggests about $30\\%$, but the exact count needs this argument; $31$ is the digit count of $2^{100}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "seq"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "ED2-26", "paper": "ED2", "n": 26, "q": "<p>A thin glass plate has two parallel faces. Whenever light meets either face, from either side, a fraction $\\tfrac13$ of its intensity is reflected and the remaining $\\tfrac23$ is transmitted; no light is absorbed, and the intensities of separate beams simply add. Two such plates are mounted parallel to each other with an air gap between them, and a beam of light strikes the first plate at right angles. The light may bounce back and forth between the four faces any number of times. What fraction of the original intensity eventually emerges beyond the second plate?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac25$", "$\\dfrac13$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac{16}{81}$", "$\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac{9}{32}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>First treat one plate. Light gets through by crossing both faces, possibly after an even number of internal reflections, so the fraction transmitted is \\[T=\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^2\\left(1+\\tfrac19+\\tfrac1{81}+\\cdots\\right)=\\tfrac49\\cdot\\tfrac98=\\tfrac12,\\] and since nothing is absorbed, the plate reflects $R=\\tfrac12$ overall (directly, $\\tfrac13+\\tfrac49\\cdot\\tfrac13\\cdot\\tfrac98=\\tfrac12$).<\/p><p>Now treat each plate as a single unit with $T=R=\\tfrac12$. Light passes the first plate, then either passes the second plate or is reflected back and forth between the plates: \\[\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12\\left(1+\\tfrac14+\\tfrac1{16}+\\cdots\\right)=\\tfrac14\\cdot\\tfrac43=\\tfrac13.\\]<\/p><p>Ignoring the repeated reflections gives $\\left(\\tfrac23\\right)^4=\\tfrac{16}{81}$, or $\\tfrac14$ at plate level. Using the single-face value $\\tfrac13$ for reflection between the plates gives $\\tfrac{9}{32}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 8.5}, {"id": "ED2-27", "paper": "ED2", "n": 27, "q": "<p>A square has vertices $(4,4)$, $(-4,4)$, $(-4,-4)$ and $(4,-4)$. The region $R$ consists of all points inside the square that are closer to the centre $(0,0)$ than to every one of the four sides of the square. What is the area of $R$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{8\\left(4\\sqrt2-5\\right)}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{32\\left(4\\sqrt2-5\\right)}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{64\\left(4\\sqrt2-5\\right)}{3}$", "$16$", "$\\dfrac{32}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{64}{3}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/ED2-27.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The square with vertices (plus or minus 4, plus or minus 4) with its diagonals dashed and the region R closer to the centre than to every side shaded, bounded by four parabolic arcs meeting the diagonals at (h,h) and its reflections; the eighth of R between the x-axis and the diagonal y = x is highlighted.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>By symmetry, $R$ is made of eight congruent pieces, one in each of the triangles cut off by the axes and the diagonals. In the triangle $0\\le y\\le x$ the nearest side is $x=4$, so the condition is $x^2+y^2&lt;(4-x)^2$, that is $x&lt;2-\\tfrac18y^2$. The boundary is the parabola with focus at the centre and directrix $x=4$.<\/p><p>The parabola meets the diagonal $y=x$ where $x^2+8x-16=0$, so at $h=4\\sqrt2-4$. The piece is $0\\le y\\le h$, $y\\le x&lt;2-\\tfrac18y^2$, with area \\[\\int_0^h\\left(2-y-\\tfrac18y^2\\right)dy=2h-\\tfrac12h^2-\\tfrac1{24}h^3.\\] With $h^2=48-32\\sqrt2$ and $h^3=320\\sqrt2-448$, this is $\\dfrac{32\\sqrt2-40}{3}=\\dfrac{8\\left(4\\sqrt2-5\\right)}{3}$. Hence the area of $R$ is $\\dfrac{64\\left(4\\sqrt2-5\\right)}{3}\\approx14.0$.<\/p><p>Using only the two sides $x=\\pm4$ gives the region between two parabolas, of area $\\tfrac{64}{3}$. Forgetting to multiply by $8$ gives $\\tfrac{8\\left(4\\sqrt2-5\\right)}{3}$, and multiplying by $4$ instead gives $\\tfrac{32\\left(4\\sqrt2-5\\right)}{3}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "int"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "EC1-1", "paper": "EC1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>A garden hose has a circular internal cross-section of diameter $2\\text{ cm}$. Water flows out of it at a constant speed of $5.4\\text{ km/h}$, filling the whole cross-section. The hose is used to fill an empty paddling pool, which is a cylinder of internal diameter $2\\text{ m}$ standing on level ground, to a depth of $30\\text{ cm}$. How long does this take?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$ hours $13$ minutes $20$ seconds", "$33$ minutes $20$ seconds", "$5$ hours $33$ minutes $20$ seconds", "$3$ minutes $20$ seconds", "$8$ minutes $20$ seconds", "$1$ hour $6$ minutes $40$ seconds"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>First, $5.4\\text{ km/h}=5400\\text{ m}\\div3600\\text{ s}=1.5\\text{ m/s}$. The hose delivers $\\pi(0.01)^2\\times1.5=1.5\\times10^{-4}\\pi\\text{ m}^3$ per second, and the pool needs $\\pi(1)^2\\times0.3=0.3\\pi\\text{ m}^3$. The factors of $\\pi$ cancel, and the time is $0.3\\div(1.5\\times10^{-4})=2000$ seconds, which is $33$ minutes $20$ seconds. Using the hose diameter as its radius gives $8$ minutes $20$ seconds; using the pool diameter as its radius gives $2$ hours $13$ minutes $20$ seconds; a slip of a factor of $10$ in the speed conversion gives $3$ minutes $20$ seconds or $5$ hours $33$ minutes $20$ seconds.<\/p>", "tags": ["units"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EC1-2", "paper": "EC1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>A delivery firm improves the fuel economy of four of its vans, measured in miles per gallon (mpg). Van $P$ goes from $24$ mpg to $32$ mpg; van $Q$ from $40$ mpg to $60$ mpg; van $R$ from $12$ mpg to $14$ mpg; and van $S$ from $30$ mpg to $36$ mpg. Next year all four vans will be driven the same distance. Which list orders the vans by the amount of fuel saved as a result of the improvements, greatest saving first?<\/p>", "opts": ["$Q,\\ P,\\ S,\\ R$", "$R,\\ P,\\ S,\\ Q$", "$R,\\ Q,\\ P,\\ S$", "$Q,\\ S,\\ P,\\ R$", "$P,\\ R,\\ S,\\ Q$", "$R,\\ P,\\ Q,\\ S$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Over a distance of $d$ miles a van doing $m$ mpg uses $\\dfrac dm$ gallons, so the saving is $d\\left(\\dfrac1{m_1}-\\dfrac1{m_2}\\right)$. This gives $\\dfrac{d}{84}$ for $R$, $\\dfrac{d}{96}$ for $P$, $\\dfrac{d}{120}$ for $Q$ and $\\dfrac{d}{180}$ for $S$, so the order is $R,\\ P,\\ Q,\\ S$. The trap $Q,\\ P,\\ S,\\ R$ orders the vans by the gain in mpg (which here is also the order of percentage gain). Fuel used is the reciprocal of the mpg figure, so a small gain at a low mpg saves far more fuel than a large gain at a high one.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "ratio"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EC1-3", "paper": "EC1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>A welder makes the skeleton of a closed cuboid from twelve straight steel rods, one along each edge, and the total length of the rods is $76$ dm. The longest straight rod that fits inside the cuboid, running from one corner to the opposite corner, has length $13$ dm. The six faces of the cuboid are then covered exactly with sheet metal. What is the total area of sheet metal used?<\/p>", "opts": ["$192\\text{ dm}^2$", "$96\\text{ dm}^2$", "$264\\text{ dm}^2$", "$169\\text{ dm}^2$", "$361\\text{ dm}^2$", "$384\\text{ dm}^2$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>With edges $a$, $b$, $c$ we have $4(a+b+c)=76$, so $a+b+c=19$, and by Pythagoras in three dimensions $a^2+b^2+c^2=13^2=169$. The surface area is \\[2(ab+bc+ca)=(a+b+c)^2-\\left(a^2+b^2+c^2\\right)=361-169=192\\text{ dm}^2.\\] There is no need to find the edges (they are $3$, $4$ and $12$ dm). The option $96$ is $ab+bc+ca$, forgetting that each face appears twice, and $384$ doubles once too often.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "algebra"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EC1-4", "paper": "EC1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A solid concrete block is a cuboid measuring $2\\text{ m}$ by $1\\text{ m}$ by $0.5\\text{ m}$. When it rests on one of its largest faces on level ground, it exerts a pressure of $12\\text{ kPa}$ on the ground. A second solid block, made of the same concrete, is an enlargement of the first with scale factor $1.5$. What pressure does the second block exert on level ground when it rests on one of its smallest faces?<\/p>", "opts": ["$18\\text{ kPa}$", "$48\\text{ kPa}$", "$72\\text{ kPa}$", "$108\\text{ kPa}$", "$40.5\\text{ kPa}$", "$27\\text{ kPa}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Pressure is weight divided by area. For a uniform cuboid the weight is (weight per unit volume) $\\times$ (area of the face it rests on) $\\times$ (height), so the area cancels and the pressure is proportional to the height alone. The first block stands $0.5$ m tall, giving $12$ kPa, which is $24$ kPa per metre of height. The second block measures $3$ m by $1.5$ m by $0.75$ m and on its smallest face it stands $3$ m tall, so the pressure is $72$ kPa. Equivalently, the weight is multiplied by $1.5^3=3.375$ and the area changes from $2\\text{ m}^2$ to $1.125\\text{ m}^2$. The option $18$ kPa applies the enlargement but not the change of face, and $48$ kPa changes the face but forgets the enlargement.<\/p>", "tags": ["units", "ratio"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EC1-5", "paper": "EC1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>A rectangular plot $ABCD$ has $AB=6\\text{ m}$ and $BC=8\\text{ m}$. Its four corners lie on a circular fence, so that $ABCD$ is a cyclic quadrilateral. A lamp is fixed at a point $P$ on the fence, different from $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$. What is the value of $PA^2+PB^2+PC^2+PD^2$, in $\\text{m}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$400$", "$196$", "$200$", "It depends on the position of $P$.", "$100$", "$250$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC1-5.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Rectangle ABCD with AB = 6 and BC = 8 inscribed in a circle, its diagonals AC and BD drawn as diameters, and a point P on the circle joined to all four vertices with a right angle marked at P in triangle APC.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Since $\\angle ABC=90^\\circ$, the diagonal $AC$ is a diameter of the circle (converse of the angle in a semicircle), and so is $BD$; each has length $\\sqrt{6^2+8^2}=10$. Now $P$ is on the circle, so $\\angle APC=90^\\circ$ and $PA^2+PC^2=AC^2=100$; likewise $PB^2+PD^2=BD^2=100$. The total is $200$, wherever $P$ is. The option $100$ uses only one of the two diameters.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EC1-6", "paper": "EC1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>A technician records seven consecutive terms of a quadratic sequence, starting with the first term: \\[3,\\quad 9,\\quad 17,\\quad 30,\\quad 47,\\quad 68,\\quad 93.\\] Exactly one of these seven values has been recorded incorrectly. What is the correct value of the $10$th term of the sequence?<\/p>", "opts": ["$197$", "$201$", "$190$", "$129$", "$192$", "$188$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The first differences are $6, 8, 13, 17, 21, 25$ and the second differences are $2, 5, 4, 4, 4$. A single wrong term disturbs only the second differences next to it, so the true second difference is $4$, and the terms $17, 30, 47, 68, 93$ fit $u_n=2n^2-n+2$. This also gives $u_1=3$ but $u_2=8$, so the $9$ is the error (no single change elsewhere makes all seven values quadratic). Then $u_{10}=200-10+2=192$. The trap $129$ comes from trusting the first three values: $3, 9, 17$ lead to $n^2+3n-1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EC1-7", "paper": "EC1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>Tutor group $A$ has $12$ students, whose test scores have arithmetic mean $65$. Tutor group $B$ has $18$ students, whose scores have mean $50$. All scores are whole numbers. One student moves from group $A$ to group $B$, taking their score with them, and as a result the mean score of <em>each<\/em> group rises by at least $0.4$. How many different scores could the student who moved have?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$5$", "$3$", "$14$", "$4$", "$2$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Group $A$ has total $780$ and group $B$ has total $900$. If the student scored $s$, then $\\dfrac{780-s}{11}\\ge65.4$ gives $s\\le60.6$, and $\\dfrac{900+s}{19}\\ge50.4$ gives $s\\ge57.6$. So $s=58$, $59$ or $60$: three scores. The tempting answer $0$ argues that the overall mean of all $30$ students cannot change, so both means cannot rise; but moving a score that is below $A$'s mean and above $B$'s mean raises both. The option $14$ counts every score from $51$ to $64$, which only makes both means rise by some amount.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EC1-8", "paper": "EC1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Two archers, Xavi and Yara, take part in outdoor and indoor sessions. Outdoors, Xavi hits the target with $12$ of his $20$ arrows and Yara hits it with $5$ of her $10$ arrows. Indoors, Xavi hits the target with $9$ of his $10$ arrows and Yara hits it with $n$ of her $30$ arrows, where $n$ is a whole number. For how many values of $n$ is Xavi's proportion of hits higher than Yara's in each type of session, while Yara's proportion of hits over all of her arrows is higher than Xavi's over all of his?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$4$", "$0$", "$3$", "$6$", "$2$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Outdoors Xavi has $60\\%$ against Yara's $50\\%$ regardless of $n$. Indoors we need $\\dfrac n{30}&lt;\\dfrac9{10}$, so $n\\le26$. Overall Xavi has $\\dfrac{21}{30}=70\\%$, and Yara needs $\\dfrac{5+n}{40}>\\dfrac7{10}$, so $n>23$. Hence $n=24$, $25$ or $26$. This is Simpson's paradox: Yara shoots most of her arrows indoors, where hits are easier, and Xavi most of his outdoors. The option $0$ is the intuition that winning both sessions must mean winning overall.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats", "ratio"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EC1-9", "paper": "EC1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A caterer prepares $x$ trays of hot food and $y$ trays of cold food for an event, where $x$ and $y$ are whole numbers. Each hot tray needs $2$ hours of staff time and each cold tray needs $3$ hours, and $24$ hours of staff time are available. The client insists on at least $2$ cold trays, and on at least as many hot trays as cold trays. The caterer makes a profit of $4$ pounds on each hot tray and $7$ pounds on each cold tray. What is the greatest possible profit, in pounds?<\/p>", "opts": ["$49$", "$44$", "$50$", "$55$", "$52$", "$52.80$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The constraints $2x+3y\\le24$, $y\\ge2$ and $x\\ge y$ describe the triangle with vertices $(2,2)$, $(9,2)$ and $(4.8,4.8)$. The profit $4x+7y$ would be largest at $(4.8,4.8)$, giving $52.80$, but that is not a whole-number plan, and rounding to $(5,5)$ needs $25$ hours. Checking the best whole-number point in each row: $y=4$ allows $x\\le6$, giving $24+28=52$; $y=3$ allows $x\\le7$, giving $49$; $y=2$ allows $x\\le9$, giving $50$; and $y\\ge5$ is impossible. So the greatest profit is $52$ pounds, at $(6,4)$. The option $44$ comes from rounding the vertex down to $(4,4)$.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EC1-10", "paper": "EC1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=x^2-6x+11$. For how many integers $x$ is $f\\big(f(x)\\big)&lt;27$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5$", "$6$", "$4$", "$7$", "$9$", "$3$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Completing the square, $f(t)=(t-3)^2+2$. So $f\\big(f(x)\\big)&lt;27$ means $\\big(f(x)-3\\big)^2&lt;25$, that is $-2&lt;f(x)&lt;8$. The left-hand inequality always holds because $f(x)\\ge2$, so we need $(x-3)^2+2&lt;8$, that is $(x-3)^2&lt;6$. This allows $x-3\\in\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}$, giving the five integers $1,2,3,4,5$. The option $3$ comes from solving $(x-3)^2&lt;4$, an error in the inner step.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EC1-11", "paper": "EC1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>For each positive integer $n$, Ali works out $\\sqrt{n}$ and rounds it to the nearest whole number, while Bea works out $\\dfrac{n}{10}$ and rounds it to the nearest whole number, rounding halves up. For how many positive integers $n$ do Ali and Bea obtain the same answer?<\/p>", "opts": ["$14$", "$30$", "$10$", "$16$", "$24$", "$20$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Ali gets $k$ when $\\left(k-\\tfrac12\\right)^2\\le n&lt;\\left(k+\\tfrac12\\right)^2$, that is (for integers) $k^2-k+1\\le n\\le k^2+k$. Bea gets $k$ when $10k-5\\le n\\le10k+4$. For $k\\le8$ Ali's range lies wholly below Bea's ($72&lt;75$ when $k=8$), and for $k\\ge12$ wholly above ($133>124$ when $k=12$). For $k=9$ the ranges $73$ to $90$ and $85$ to $94$ share $6$ values; for $k=10$, $91$ to $110$ and $95$ to $104$ share $10$; for $k=11$, $111$ to $132$ and $105$ to $114$ share $4$. The total is $20$. The trap $10$ only looks near $n=100$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EC1-12", "paper": "EC1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>A list of numbers begins $4,\\ 10$. Further numbers are then added to the end of the list, one at a time, and each new number is chosen so that the mean of all the numbers now in the list is exactly $1$ more than it was before. What is the $20$th number in the list?<\/p>", "opts": ["$42$", "$46$", "$44$", "$25$", "$26$", "$40$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The mean of the first two numbers is $7$, so after $n$ numbers the mean is $n+5$ and the total is $S_n=n(n+5)$. The $20$th number is $S_{20}-S_{19}=500-456=44$. In general the $n$th number is $S_n-S_{n-1}=2n+4$ for $n\\ge2$; for example the third is $10$, and $4, 10, 10$ has mean $8$. The option $25$ is the mean after $20$ numbers, not the $20$th number.<\/p>", "tags": ["stats", "algebra"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EC1-13", "paper": "EC1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>A bucket has the shape of a right circular frustum, open at the top: its circular base has radius $10\\text{ cm}$ and its circular rim has radius $20\\text{ cm}$. The bucket stands on its base on level ground, and water is poured in until its depth is exactly half the depth of the bucket. What fraction of the capacity of the bucket is filled?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{19}{56}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{37}{56}$", "$\\dfrac{27}{64}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC1-13.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Cross-section of the bucket extended downwards to a cone with vertex V: base radius 10 at height h, the shaded water surface at height three halves h, and the rim radius 20 at height 2h.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Extend the bucket downwards to a complete cone. The radius is proportional to the distance from the vertex, so if the base is at distance $h$ from the vertex then the rim is at $2h$ and the water surface, half way up, is at $\\tfrac32h$. Volumes of similar cones scale with the cube of the distance, so the bucket is proportional to $2^3-1=7$ and the water to $\\left(\\tfrac32\\right)^3-1=\\tfrac{19}{8}$. The fraction is $\\tfrac{19}{8}\\div7=\\tfrac{19}{56}$. The option $\\tfrac{37}{56}$ is the answer for the wide end at the bottom, $\\tfrac{5}{12}$ uses areas instead of volumes, and $\\tfrac{27}{64}$ treats the water as a cone similar to the whole bucket.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "ratio"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EC1-14", "paper": "EC1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A school corridor has $60$ lockers, numbered $1$ to $60$, all of which are closed. For each prime number $p$ less than $60$, one student walks along the corridor and changes the state of every locker whose number is a multiple of $p$, opening it if it is closed and closing it if it is open. After all of these students have passed, how many of the lockers are open?<\/p>", "opts": ["$17$", "$30$", "$7$", "$28$", "$25$", "$31$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Locker $n$ is changed once for each distinct prime factor of $n$, so it ends up open exactly when $n$ has an odd number of distinct prime factors. Numbers with one distinct prime factor are the prime powers: the $17$ primes below $60$ together with $4, 8, 16, 32, 9, 27, 25, 49$, which is $25$ lockers. Numbers up to $60$ with three distinct prime factors are $30$, $42$ and $60$, and four would need at least $2\\times3\\times5\\times7=210$. The total is $28$. The trap $7$ is the answer to the classic version in which every divisor (not just every prime) has a student, leaving the perfect squares open; $25$ forgets the numbers with three prime factors.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EC1-15", "paper": "EC1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>A bag contains $1$ red counter and $1$ blue counter. A counter is taken out at random, and then it is put back into the bag together with one extra counter of the same colour. This process is carried out $5$ times in total, so that the bag finally holds $7$ counters. What is the probability that at least $5$ of the $7$ counters are red?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{16}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{3}{16}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Take any order of draws with $k$ reds and $5-k$ blues. The denominators are always $2,3,4,5,6$, and the numerators for the reds are $1,2,\\dots,k$ and for the blues $1,2,\\dots,5-k$, whatever the order. So each such order has probability $\\dfrac{k!\\,(5-k)!}{6!}$, and there are $\\dbinom5k$ orders, giving $\\dfrac{5!}{6!}=\\dfrac16$ for every $k$ from $0$ to $5$. At least $5$ red counters at the end means at least $4$ red draws, so the probability is $\\tfrac26=\\tfrac13$. The trap $\\tfrac3{16}$ treats the draws as independent with probability $\\tfrac12$ each, but every red draw makes the next red draw more likely.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EC1-16", "paper": "EC1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>A party hat is the curved surface of a right circular cone with slant height $6\\text{ cm}$ and base radius $2\\text{ cm}$; it has no base. An ant stands at a point $A$ on the circular rim of the hat and walks, on the outside of the curved surface, to the point $B$ on the rim that is diametrically opposite $A$. What is the length of the shortest possible route?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6\\sqrt{3}\\text{ cm}$", "$3\\pi\\text{ cm}$", "$4\\text{ cm}$", "$2\\sqrt{10}\\text{ cm}$", "$2\\pi\\text{ cm}$", "$6\\text{ cm}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC1-16.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The unrolled hat: a sector with vertex V, radius 6, angle 120 degrees and arc 4 pi, with A and B on the arc 60 degrees apart and the shaded equilateral triangle VAB whose side AB is 6.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>In the net shown, the shortest route is the segment AB.<\/p><p>Cut the hat along a slant line and unroll it into a sector of radius $6$ whose arc is the rim, of length $4\\pi$. The full circle of radius $6$ has circumference $12\\pi$, so the sector angle is $\\tfrac13\\times360^\\circ=120^\\circ$. Halfway round the rim corresponds to half of this, so in the net $A$ and $B$ are $60^\\circ$ apart as seen from the vertex $V$. The shortest route is the straight segment $AB$ in the net, and triangle $VAB$ has $VA=VB=6$ with a $60^\\circ$ angle between them, so it is equilateral and $AB=6$. This beats walking round the rim ($2\\pi\\approx6.28$). The $4$ cm diameter is not available because the hat has no base, and $6\\sqrt3$ uses the full $120^\\circ$.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EC1-17", "paper": "EC1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>In special relativity, speeds are measured as fractions of the speed of light. If an object moves at speed $u$ relative to a second object, which itself moves at speed $v$ relative to an observer in the same direction, then the first object moves at speed \\[u\\oplus v=\\frac{u+v}{1+uv}\\] relative to the observer. A space station launches craft $C_1$ at speed $\\tfrac12$ relative to the station. Then, for each $k=1,2,\\dots,7$, craft $C_k$ launches craft $C_{k+1}$ at speed $\\dfrac{1}{k+2}$ relative to $C_k$, all in the same direction. What is the speed of craft $C_8$ relative to the station?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{44}{45}$", "$\\dfrac{35}{37}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{22}{23}$", "$\\dfrac{9}{10}$", "$\\dfrac{21}{22}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>We need $\\tfrac12\\oplus\\tfrac13\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus\\tfrac19$. Working forwards, $\\tfrac12\\oplus\\tfrac13=\\tfrac57$, then $\\tfrac57\\oplus\\tfrac14=\\tfrac9{11}$, then $\\tfrac9{11}\\oplus\\tfrac15=\\tfrac{14}{16}$: the results are $\\dfrac{T-1}{T+1}$ with $T=6,10,15,\\dots$ the triangular numbers. The reason is the identity \\[\\frac{1+u\\oplus v}{1-u\\oplus v}=\\frac{1+u+v+uv}{1-u-v+uv}=\\frac{1+u}{1-u}\\times\\frac{1+v}{1-v},\\] so the quantity $\\dfrac{1+w}{1-w}$ multiplies. For $w=\\tfrac1k$ it equals $\\dfrac{k+1}{k-1}$, and the product for $k=2,\\dots,9$ telescopes to $\\dfrac{9\\times10}{1\\times2}=45$. Solving $\\dfrac{1+w}{1-w}=45$ gives $w=\\dfrac{44}{46}=\\dfrac{22}{23}$. The option $\\tfrac{44}{45}$ comes from stopping at $T-1$ over $T$, and $1$ from expecting the speeds to reach light speed.<\/p>", "tags": ["algebra", "number"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EC1-18", "paper": "EC1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>A large floor is tiled with equilateral triangles of side $6\\sqrt{3}\\text{ cm}$, and the lines between the tiles have negligible width. A coin of radius $1\\text{ cm}$ is tossed onto the floor and lands flat, with its centre equally likely to be at any point of the floor. What is the probability that the coin lies entirely inside one tile, touching none of the lines?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{16}{27}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{9}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{9}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC1-18.svg?v=1\" alt=\"An equilateral tile of side 6 root 3 with a smaller shaded equilateral triangle inside, sharing its incentre, at distance 1 from each side; the inradius 3 is split as 2 plus 1 and a coin of radius 1 is shown with its centre on the inner triangle.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>By symmetry we may consider the tile containing the centre. The coin avoids the lines when its centre is more than $1$ cm from all three sides of the tile. These points form a smaller equilateral triangle with the same incentre and sides parallel to the tile's. The tile's inscribed circle has radius $\\dfrac{6\\sqrt3}{2\\sqrt3}=3$, so the small triangle has inradius $3-1=2$. The triangles are similar with length ratio $\\tfrac23$, so the area ratio, which is the probability, is $\\tfrac49$. The trap $\\tfrac23$ is the length ratio.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob", "geometry"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EC1-19", "paper": "EC1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>Two warning lights flash at regular intervals: one every $a$ seconds and the other every $b$ seconds, where $a$ and $b$ are positive integers with $a&lt;b$. They flash together at noon, and the next time after noon that they flash together is exactly $72$ seconds later. How many possible pairs $(a,b)$ are there?<\/p>", "opts": ["$17$", "$35$", "$34$", "$12$", "$18$", "$15$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The lights next coincide after the lowest common multiple of $a$ and $b$ seconds, so we need $\\operatorname{lcm}(a,b)=72=2^3\\times3^2$. Both $a$ and $b$ divide $72$. For the prime $2$, the pair of exponents must have larger value $3$: of the $4\\times4$ pairs from $\\{0,1,2,3\\}$, the $3\\times3$ with both below $3$ fail, leaving $7$. For the prime $3$, similarly $3\\times3-2\\times2=5$. That gives $35$ ordered pairs, including $a=b=72$. Removing that one and halving, the number with $a&lt;b$ is $17$. The trap $35$ forgets $a&lt;b$, and $18$ forgets to remove the pair $(72,72)$.<\/p>", "tags": ["number"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EC1-20", "paper": "EC1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>Sixteen players, including Ann and Bea, enter a knockout tennis tournament. Their names are placed at random into the sixteen slots of a fixed draw: in round $1$ the players in slots $1$ and $2$ play each other, those in slots $3$ and $4$ play each other, and so on; the winners of the first two matches meet in round $2$, and so on, until the last two players meet in the final. Each player is equally likely to win any match they play, independently of all other matches. What is the probability that Ann and Bea play each other at some point in the tournament?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{15}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{15}{64}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{15}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{16}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Each match eliminates one player, so exactly $15$ matches are played. All $\\dbinom{16}{2}=120$ pairs of players are equally likely to be a pair who meet, by symmetry, and the expected number of pairs who meet is $15$, so each pair meets with probability $\\tfrac{15}{120}=\\tfrac18$. As a check, fix Ann's slot: Bea is in the same first-round match with probability $\\tfrac1{15}$ (they meet for certain), in the same group of four but not that match with probability $\\tfrac2{15}$ (they meet with probability $\\tfrac14$), in the same half but not that group with probability $\\tfrac4{15}$ (probability $\\tfrac1{16}$), and in the other half with probability $\\tfrac8{15}$ (probability $\\tfrac1{64}$), totalling $\\tfrac1{15}\\left(1+\\tfrac12+\\tfrac14+\\tfrac18\\right)=\\tfrac18$. The trap $\\tfrac1{15}$ counts only round $1$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EC1-21", "paper": "EC1", "n": 21, "q": "<p>A flower bed is in the shape of a cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$, with its vertices in that order around a circle. Triangle $ABD$ is equilateral, and the diagonal $AC$ has length $10\\text{ m}$. What is the area of the flower bed?<\/p>", "opts": ["$50\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m}^2$", "$\\dfrac{100\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\text{ m}^2$", "$\\dfrac{75\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\text{ m}^2$", "$50\\text{ m}^2$", "$25\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m}^2$", "It cannot be determined from the information given."], "ans": "E", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC1-21.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Cyclic quadrilateral ABCD with ABD equilateral and diagonal AC; E lies on CA with CE = CB, the 60 degree angles ACB and ACD are marked, and the congruent triangles BEA and BCD are shaded with EA = CD marked.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Angles in the same segment give $\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB=60^\\circ$ and $\\angle ACD=\\angle ABD=60^\\circ$, so the area is $\\tfrac12\\,AC\\cdot CB\\sin60^\\circ+\\tfrac12\\,AC\\cdot CD\\sin60^\\circ=\\dfrac{5\\sqrt3}{2}(CB+CD)$. To find $CB+CD$, mark $E$ on $CA$ with $CE=CB$; triangle $BCE$ has a $60^\\circ$ angle between equal sides, so it is equilateral. Then triangles $BEA$ and $BCD$ are congruent ($BE=BC$, $BA=BD$, and $\\angle EBA=60^\\circ-\\angle DBE=\\angle CBD$), so $EA=CD$ and $CB+CD=CE+EA=CA=10$. The area is $25\\sqrt3\\text{ m}^2$, the same for every position of $C$. A quick check: when $C$ is the midpoint of arc $BD$, $AC$ is a diameter and the bed is a kite with diagonals $10$ and $5\\sqrt3$, area $25\\sqrt3$. The option $\\tfrac{75\\sqrt3}{4}$ is the area of triangle $ABD$ alone in that case.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.1}, {"id": "EC1-22", "paper": "EC1", "n": 22, "q": "<p>At a fair, a player tosses a fair coin at most $6$ times. She wins a prize as soon as she gets heads on two consecutive tosses, and she stops tossing at that point; if this has not happened after $6$ tosses, she wins nothing. What is the probability that she wins a prize?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{21}{64}$", "$\\dfrac{781}{1024}$", "$\\dfrac{43}{64}$", "$\\dfrac{39}{64}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{11}{16}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Imagine she always makes all $6$ tosses; she wins exactly when the sequence contains two consecutive heads. Let $a_n$ be the number of sequences of $n$ tosses with no two consecutive heads. Such a sequence either ends in a tail after a good sequence of length $n-1$, or ends in tail then head after a good sequence of length $n-2$, so $a_n=a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}$. With $a_1=2$ and $a_2=3$ we get $5, 8, 13, 21$, so $a_6=21$. The probability of no prize is $\\tfrac{21}{64}$, and of a prize $\\tfrac{43}{64}$. The trap $\\tfrac{781}{1024}=1-\\left(\\tfrac34\\right)^5$ treats the five overlapping pairs of tosses as independent.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EC1-23", "paper": "EC1", "n": 23, "q": "<p>In a board game, a counter starts on square $0$ of a long straight track whose squares are numbered $0, 1, 2, 3, \\dots$. On each turn a fair six-sided die is rolled and the counter moves forward by the number shown. Square $4$ holds a trap. What is the probability that the counter lands on square $4$ at some point?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{343}{1296}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{2}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{2401}{7776}$", "$\\dfrac{49}{216}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The counter lands on square $4$ exactly when the first few rolls add up to $4$, and these events for different numbers of rolls cannot overlap. The ordered ways to make $4$ are: $4$ ($1$ way with one roll); $1+3$, $3+1$, $2+2$ ($3$ ways with two rolls); $1+1+2$ and its rearrangements ($3$ ways with three rolls); and $1+1+1+1$. So the probability is \\[\\frac16+\\frac3{36}+\\frac3{216}+\\frac1{1296}=\\frac{216+108+18+1}{1296}=\\frac{343}{1296}=\\frac16\\left(\\frac76\\right)^3.\\] The trap $\\tfrac27$ is the long-run chance for a square far along the track ($1$ divided by the mean roll $3.5$), and $\\tfrac{49}{216}$ is the answer for square $3$.<\/p>", "tags": ["prob"], "rating": 8.3}, {"id": "EC1-24", "paper": "EC1", "n": 24, "q": "<p>A carpenter has a large solid wooden cuboid. With a single straight saw cut along a plane, she removes one corner of the block: the cut crosses the three edges that meet at that corner, and the new face it creates is a triangle with sides $5\\text{ cm}$, $6\\text{ cm}$ and $7\\text{ cm}$. What is the volume of the piece that is removed?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\sqrt{95}\\text{ cm}^3$", "$35\\text{ cm}^3$", "$6\\sqrt{95}\\text{ cm}^3$", "$6\\sqrt{6}\\text{ cm}^3$", "$\\sqrt{210}\\text{ cm}^3$", "$\\sqrt{95}\\text{ cm}^3$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC1-24.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The removed corner piece: three mutually perpendicular edges p, q and r meeting at the corner of the block, with the triangular cut face having sides 5, 6 and 7.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The piece is a pyramid whose three edges at the corner, $p$, $q$ and $r$, are mutually perpendicular. Pythagoras on its three right-angled faces gives $p^2+q^2=25$, $q^2+r^2=36$ and $r^2+p^2=49$. Adding, $p^2+q^2+r^2=55$, so $r^2=30$, $p^2=19$ and $q^2=6$. Taking a right-angled face as base, the volume is $\\tfrac13\\times\\tfrac12pq\\times r=\\dfrac{pqr}{6}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{19\\times6\\times30}}{6}=\\dfrac{6\\sqrt{95}}{6}=\\sqrt{95}\\text{ cm}^3$. The option $6\\sqrt{95}$ forgets the $\\tfrac16$, and $6\\sqrt6$ is the area of the triangular face.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "EC1-25", "paper": "EC1", "n": 25, "q": "<p>A model is built from identical cubes on a $4$ by $4$ square grid. On each grid square there is a single vertical stack of cubes (possibly with no cubes), and every cube rests either on the grid or on another cube. Viewed from the front, the tallest stacks in the four columns have heights $3$, $1$, $4$ and $2$, from left to right. Viewed from the right-hand side, the tallest stacks in the four rows have heights $4$, $4$, $2$ and $1$, from front to back. Let $M$ and $m$ be the greatest and least possible numbers of cubes in the model. What is $M-m$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$17$", "$15$", "$21$", "$20$", "$16$", "$11$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The stack in a given column and row can be no taller than the smaller of the two required maxima, and taking every stack exactly that tall shows both views correctly (each column and each row contains a square where its maximum is reached, because some row and some column allow height $4$). So $M$ is the sum of these minima: $9+4+11+7=31$. For $m$, each of the eight required heights must be shown by some stack, and one stack of height $h$ can serve a column and a row that both need $h$. Pair the column needing $4$ with a row needing $4$, the $2$ with the $2$, and the $1$ with the $1$. The column needing $3$ and the other row needing $4$ remain, and need their own stacks (a $3$ in a row allowing it, and a $4$ in the column of height $4$). So $m=4+2+1+3+4=14$, and $M-m=17$. The option $21$ takes $m=10$ by looking at the front view only, and $20$ uses $m=11$ from the side view only.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "number"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "EC1-26", "paper": "EC1", "n": 26, "q": "<p>A long crate with a square cross-section of side $2\\text{ m}$ lies on level ground. It is rolled forwards without slipping: each time, it is tipped through $90^\\circ$ about its leading bottom edge, and this is repeated until the crate has turned through one complete revolution. In the cross-section, the corner $P$ of the square that starts on the ground at the back of the crate traces out a curve, ending on the ground again. What is the area of the region enclosed between this curve and the ground?<\/p>", "opts": ["$(8+4\\pi)\\text{ m}^2$", "$(4+4\\pi)\\text{ m}^2$", "$(8+2\\pi)\\text{ m}^2$", "$(4+2\\pi)\\text{ m}^2$", "$(2+4\\pi)\\text{ m}^2$", "$4\\pi\\text{ m}^2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC1-26.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The crate's starting square with corner P at the origin and the shaded region under the path of P: quarter circles of radius 2 centred at (2,0) and (6,0), a quarter circle of radius 2 root 2 centred at (4,0) from (2,2) to (6,2), and the two right-angled triangles beneath.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Put $P$ at the origin with the crate occupying $0\\le x\\le2$. In the first tip $P$ turns about $(2,0)$ through a quarter circle of radius $2$, reaching $(2,2)$. In the second it turns about $(4,0)$ through a quarter circle of radius $2\\sqrt2$, reaching $(6,2)$. In the third it turns about $(6,0)$ through a quarter circle of radius $2$, landing at $(8,0)$; in the fourth tip $P$ is the pivot and does not move. The region under the curve consists of two quarter discs of radius $2$ (area $\\pi$ each), the quarter disc of radius $2\\sqrt2$ centred at $(4,0)$ between the radii to $(2,2)$ and $(6,2)$ (area $2\\pi$), and the two right-angled triangles with vertices $(2,0)$, $(4,0)$, $(2,2)$ and $(4,0)$, $(6,0)$, $(6,2)$ (area $2$ each). The total is $4\\pi+4$. The option $4\\pi$ forgets the triangles.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry"], "rating": 8.5}, {"id": "EC1-27", "paper": "EC1", "n": 27, "q": "<p>A triangular park $ABC$ has three straight paths. The path from $A$ goes to the point $D$ on $BC$ with $BD:DC=1:3$; the path from $B$ goes to the point $E$ on $CA$ with $CE:EA=1:3$; and the path from $C$ goes to the point $F$ on $AB$ with $AF:FB=1:3$. The three paths enclose a small triangular region in the middle of the park. What fraction of the area of the park is this region?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{3}{13}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{13}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{7}$", "$\\dfrac{4}{13}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{5}{16}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC1-27.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Triangle with A(0,0), B(4,0), C(0,4), the paths AD, BE and CF to D(3,1), E(0,3) and F(1,0), and the small shaded triangle they enclose.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Stretching or shearing the triangle in a fixed direction scales all areas by the same factor and keeps the ratios along lines, so we may take $A(0,0)$, $B(4,0)$, $C(0,4)$, with area $8$. Then $D=(3,1)$, $E=(0,3)$ and $F=(1,0)$, and the paths are $AD$: $y=\\tfrac13x$, $BE$: $3x+4y=12$, and $CF$: $4x+y=4$. Their pairwise intersections are $\\left(\\tfrac{36}{13},\\tfrac{12}{13}\\right)$, $\\left(\\tfrac4{13},\\tfrac{36}{13}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\tfrac{12}{13},\\tfrac4{13}\\right)$. The shoelace formula gives area $\\tfrac12\\times\\dfrac{36\\times32-4\\times8-12\\times24}{169}=\\dfrac{416}{169}=\\dfrac{32}{13}$, which is $\\tfrac{4}{13}$ of $8$. The option $\\tfrac17$ is the well-known answer when the points are one third of the way along the sides.<\/p>", "tags": ["geometry", "ratio"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "EC2-1", "paper": "EC2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>An ornamental pond has a circular boundary which passes through the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(7,1)$, and the centre of the circle lies on the line $y=2x-3$. Coordinates are in metres. A straight path starts at the origin $O$, which lies outside the pond, and crosses the pond, meeting the boundary first at $P$ and then at $Q$. Which of the following is true of the product $OP\\times OQ$, in $\\text{m}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["It depends on the direction of the path, and its greatest possible value is $41$.", "It equals $25$ for every such path.", "It equals $9$ for every such path.", "It equals $41$ for every such path.", "It equals $16$ for every such path.", "It depends on the direction of the path, and its least possible value is $16$."], "ans": "E", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC2-1.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Circle with centre C(4,5) and radius 5 through A(1,1) and B(7,1), found from the lines x = 4 and y = 2x - 3; a path from the origin O meets the circle at P and Q, and the tangent from O touches the circle at (4,0) with length 4, while OC = root 41.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The centre lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, which is $x=4$, and on $y=2x-3$, so it is $C(4,5)$, with $r^2=CA^2=9+16=25$. For any line through $O$ meeting the circle at $P$ and $Q$, the product $OP\\times OQ$ is the same (intersecting secants), and taking the tangent from $O$ shows it equals the square of the tangent length, $OC^2-r^2=41-25=16$. The value $41$ is $OC^2$ (forgetting to subtract $r^2$), and $25$ is $r^2$; the product does not depend on the direction.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "EC2-2", "paper": "EC2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>The reading on a sensor $t$ hours after it is switched on is modelled by \\[f(t)=p+q\\,r^{t},\\] where $p$, $q$ and $r$ are constants with $r>0$ and $r\\neq1$. The readings at $t=1$, $2$ and $3$ are $10$, $4$ and $1$ respectively. As $t$ increases without limit, the readings approach a limiting value. What is it?<\/p>", "opts": ["$-1$", "$\\dfrac14$", "$-\\dfrac12$", "$-4$", "$0$", "$-2$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Successive differences of $p+qr^t$ are $qr^t(r-1)$, so they are in geometric progression with ratio $r$. Here the differences are $-6$ and $-3$, so $r=\\tfrac12$. Then $qr(r-1)=-6$ gives $q\\cdot\\tfrac12\\cdot\\left(-\\tfrac12\\right)=-6$, so $q=24$, and $p=10-24\\cdot\\tfrac12=-2$. Since $0&lt;r&lt;1$, $qr^t\\to0$ and the readings tend to $p=-2$. The tempting answer $0$ assumes the readings themselves decay to zero; $-\\tfrac12$ is the next reading $f(4)$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog", "graphs"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EC2-3", "paper": "EC2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>Find the term independent of $x$ in the expansion of \\[\\left(1+x^{3}\\right)\\left(2x^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{9}.\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$-528$", "$528$", "$672$", "$816$", "$384$", "$-144$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The general term of $\\left(2x^2-\\tfrac1x\\right)^9$ is $\\binom{9}{k}(2x^2)^{9-k}\\left(-\\tfrac1x\\right)^{k}=\\binom9k 2^{9-k}(-1)^k x^{18-3k}$. The constant term of the product is the constant term of this expansion plus its coefficient of $x^{-3}$. For $x^0$, $k=6$: $\\binom96 2^3=84\\times8=672$. For $x^{-3}$, $k=7$: $\\binom97 2^2(-1)^7=-144$. The total is $672-144=528$. Forgetting the $x^3$ partner gives $672$; losing the sign of $(-1)^7$ gives $816$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "EC2-4", "paper": "EC2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>The line $y=x$ divides the circle \\[x^2+y^2-6x-2y+6=0\\] into two segments. What is the area of the minor segment?<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\pi-4$", "$3\\pi+2$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}-1$", "$2\\pi-2$", "$\\pi-1$", "$\\pi-2$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC2-4.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Circle with centre (3,1) and radius 2 cut by the line y = x at (1,1) and (3,3); the radii to these points are perpendicular, the perpendicular distance from the centre to the chord is root 2, and the minor segment is shaded.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Completing the square, $(x-3)^2+(y-1)^2=4$: centre $(3,1)$, radius $2$. The distance from the centre to $x-y=0$ is $\\tfrac{|3-1|}{\\sqrt2}=\\sqrt2$, so the half-chord is $\\sqrt{4-2}=\\sqrt2$ and the chord subtends a right angle at the centre. The minor segment has area $\\tfrac12\\cdot2^2\\cdot\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\tfrac12\\cdot2^2\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}=\\pi-2$. The value $3\\pi+2$ is the major segment.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "trig"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "EC2-5", "paper": "EC2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Find the sum of all real solutions of the equation \\[\\log_2 x\\cdot\\log_3 x=\\log_2 x+\\log_3 x.\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$6$", "$1$", "$5$", "$9$", "$7$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Write $a=\\log_2x$ and $k=\\log_23$, so $\\log_3x=\\tfrac{a}{k}$. The equation becomes $\\tfrac{a^2}{k}=a+\\tfrac ak$, that is $a\\left(\\tfrac ak-1-\\tfrac1k\\right)=0$. So $a=0$, giving $x=1$, or $a=k+1=\\log_23+\\log_22=\\log_26$, giving $x=6$. The sum is $7$. Dividing through by $\\log_2x$ at the start loses $x=1$ and leads to $6$.<\/p>", "tags": ["explog"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EC2-6", "paper": "EC2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>The equation \\[x^4-10x^2+k=0,\\] where $k$ is a constant, has four distinct real roots, and these roots, in a suitable order, form an arithmetic progression. Find $k$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$21$", "$24$", "$9$", "$\\dfrac{100}{9}$", "$25$", "$16$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The roots come in pairs $\\pm\\alpha$, $\\pm\\beta$ with $0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\beta$, and in increasing order they are $-\\beta,-\\alpha,\\alpha,\\beta$. The common difference is $2\\alpha$, so $\\beta=3\\alpha$. Then $\\alpha^2$ and $9\\alpha^2$ are the roots of $u^2-10u+k=0$, so $10\\alpha^2=10$, $\\alpha^2=1$ and $k=\\alpha^2\\cdot9\\alpha^2=9$. Assuming the roots are $\\pm a,\\pm2a$ (gaps not equal) gives $16$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg", "seq"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "EC2-7", "paper": "EC2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>A geometric series has a positive first term. Its sum to infinity is $16$, and the sum of its first four terms is $15$. Let $T$ be the sum of the terms in the odd positions (the $1$st, $3$rd, $5$th, $\\ldots$ terms). What is the sum of all possible values of $T$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{128}{3}$", "$40$", "$32$", "$\\dfrac{32}{3}$", "$16$", "$\\dfrac{64}{3}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>With first term $a$ and ratio $r$, $S_4=S_\\infty\\left(1-r^4\\right)$, so $16\\left(1-r^4\\right)=15$ and $r^4=\\tfrac1{16}$, giving $r=\\pm\\tfrac12$. If $r=\\tfrac12$ then $a=8$; if $r=-\\tfrac12$ then $a=24$, which is also positive. The odd-position terms form a geometric series with ratio $r^2=\\tfrac14$, so $T=\\tfrac{a}{1-r^2}=\\tfrac43a$, which is $\\tfrac{32}3$ or $32$. The sum is $\\tfrac{128}{3}$. Missing the negative ratio gives $\\tfrac{32}{3}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EC2-8", "paper": "EC2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Which of the following sequences of two transformations map the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ onto the curve $y=\\sqrt{9x+18}$?<\/p><ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>A translation by $2$ units in the negative $x$-direction, followed by a stretch parallel to the $y$-axis with scale factor $3$.<\/li><li>A stretch parallel to the $x$-axis with scale factor $\\tfrac19$, followed by a translation by $2$ units in the negative $x$-direction.<\/li><li>A translation by $18$ units in the negative $x$-direction, followed by a stretch parallel to the $x$-axis with scale factor $\\tfrac19$.<\/li><li>A translation by $2$ units in the negative $x$-direction, followed by a stretch parallel to the $x$-axis with scale factor $\\tfrac19$.<\/li><\/ol>", "opts": ["I and II only", "I, II and IV only", "I, II and III only", "II and III only", "I, II, III and IV", "I only", "I and IV only"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Note $\\sqrt{9x+18}=3\\sqrt{x+2}=\\sqrt{9(x+2)}$. I: $\\sqrt{x+2}$, then $3\\sqrt{x+2}$; correct. II: $\\sqrt{9x}$, then $\\sqrt{9(x+2)}$; correct. III: $\\sqrt{x+18}$, then replacing $x$ by $9x$ gives $\\sqrt{9x+18}$; correct. IV: $\\sqrt{x+2}$, then $\\sqrt{9x+2}$; wrong, because a stretch applied after the translation also scales the shift. So I, II and III only.<\/p>", "tags": ["graphs"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "EC2-9", "paper": "EC2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A thin circular wheel of radius $2\\text{ cm}$ rolls without slipping around the outside of a fixed flat plate in the shape of a regular hexagon of side $3\\text{ cm}$, lying in the same plane. The wheel stays in contact with the boundary of the plate at all times, and it makes one complete circuit, returning to its starting position for the first time. Through what total angle, in radians, has the wheel turned about its own centre?<\/p>", "opts": ["$18+4\\pi$", "$9+\\pi$", "$18+2\\pi$", "$9+2\\pi$", "$9$", "$9+4\\pi$", "$9+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC2-9.svg?v=1\" alt=\"A regular hexagon of side 3 with a wheel of radius 2 rolling along a side and another wheel swinging about a vertex through the exterior angle pi over 3; the dashed path of the wheel's centre consists of straight pieces and arcs of radius 2.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Along each side the wheel rolls a distance $3$, turning through $\\tfrac32$ radians, giving $9$ for the six sides. At each vertex the point of contact stays at the vertex while the wheel swings round it through the exterior angle $\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}$, and the wheel turns through that same angle; six vertices add $2\\pi$. The total is $9+2\\pi$. As a check, the centre travels $18+2\\pi\\cdot2$, and dividing by the radius $2$ gives $9+2\\pi$. Ignoring the corners gives $9$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EC2-10", "paper": "EC2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>A flower bed is an annular sector: the region between two concentric circular arcs, of radii $r$ and $R$ with $0&lt;r&lt;R$, and two radii which meet at the common centre at an angle of $\\theta$ radians, where $0&lt;\\theta&lt;2\\pi$. Edging for the two curved edges costs $2$ pounds per metre and edging for the two straight edges costs $5$ pounds per metre. The total edging budget is $200$ pounds, and all of it is spent. What is the greatest possible area of the bed, in $\\text{m}^2$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$500$", "$250$", "$125$", "$200$", "$\\dfrac{625}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{1250}{\\pi}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC2-10.svg?v=1\" alt=\"An annular sector centred at O with angle theta, inner radius r and outer radius R; the two straight edges have length w, and the curved edges have lengths r theta and R theta.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Let $L=\\theta(R+r)$ be the total length of the two arcs and $w=R-r$ the length of each straight edge. The area is $\\tfrac12\\theta\\left(R^2-r^2\\right)=\\tfrac12\\theta(R+r)(R-r)=\\tfrac12Lw$, like a trapezium. The budget gives $2L+10w=200$, so $L=100-5w$ and the area is $\\tfrac12w(100-5w)$, which is greatest at $w=10$, $L=50$, giving $250$. This is achievable, for example with $r=10$, $R=20$, $\\theta=\\tfrac53$. Forgetting the factor $\\tfrac12$ gives $500$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig", "diff"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "EC2-11", "paper": "EC2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of values of the constant $k$ for which the equation \\[\\sin\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)=k\\] has exactly two solutions in the interval $0\\le x\\le\\pi$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-1&lt;k&lt;1$", "$\\tfrac12&lt;k&lt;1$ only", "$-1&lt;k&lt;\\tfrac12$ only", "$-1&lt;k&lt;1$ and $k\\neq\\tfrac12$", "$-1&lt;k\\le\\tfrac12$", "$-1\\le k\\le1$ and $k\\neq\\tfrac12$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Put $u=2x+\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}$, which runs over $\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}\\le u\\le\\tfrac{13\\pi}{6}$, exactly one period. On this interval $\\sin u$ starts at $\\tfrac12$, rises to $1$, falls to $-1$ and returns to $\\tfrac12$. A horizontal line at height $k$ meets this once for $k=\\pm1$, twice for $-1&lt;k&lt;1$ with $k\\neq\\tfrac12$, and three times for $k=\\tfrac12$ (at both ends and at $u=\\tfrac{5\\pi}{6}$). The value $k=\\tfrac12$ is the trap.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig", "graphs"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EC2-12", "paper": "EC2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Three trees stand at the points $A(0,0)$, $B(8,0)$ and $C(3,2)$ in a park, with distances in metres. A circular fence is to be built so that all three trees lie inside the fence or on it. What is the smallest possible radius of the fence, in metres?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{13}{3}$", "$4$", "$\\sqrt{29}$", "$5$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{377}}{4}$", "$4\\sqrt2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Any circle containing $A$ and $B$ has a diameter at least $AB=8$, so the radius is at least $4$. The circle with $AB$ as diameter has centre $(4,0)$ and radius $4$, and $C$ is at distance $\\sqrt{1+4}=\\sqrt5&lt;4$ from its centre, so it encloses all three trees. The answer is $4$. The trap is the circumcircle: the angle $ACB$ is obtuse (since $\\overrightarrow{CA}\\cdot\\overrightarrow{CB}=-15+4&lt;0$), and for an obtuse triangle the circumcircle, of radius $\\tfrac{abc}{4\\Delta}=\\tfrac{8\\sqrt{13}\\sqrt{29}}{32}=\\tfrac{\\sqrt{377}}{4}$, is larger than necessary.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "EC2-13", "paper": "EC2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>A cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$ has sides $AB=5$, $BC=3$, $CD=3$ and $DA=8$. What is the radius of the circle through $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac72$", "$4$", "$\\dfrac{7\\sqrt3}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{14\\sqrt3}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{7\\sqrt3}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{57}}{3}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC2-13.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Cyclic quadrilateral ABCD with AB = 5, BC = 3, CD = 3 and DA = 8 in a circle with centre O and radius R; the diagonal AC = 7 is highlighted and the angle ABC of 120 degrees is marked.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary, so $\\cos D=-\\cos B$. The cosine rule for the diagonal $AC$ in triangles $ABC$ and $ADC$ gives $25+9-30\\cos B=9+64+48\\cos B$, so $\\cos B=-\\tfrac12$, $B=120^\\circ$ and $AC^2=34+15=49$, $AC=7$. The circumcircle of $ABCD$ is that of triangle $ABC$, so by the sine rule $2R=\\tfrac{7}{\\sin120^\\circ}$ and $R=\\tfrac{7}{\\sqrt3}=\\tfrac{7\\sqrt3}{3}$. A sign slip giving $\\cos B=\\tfrac12$ leads to $AC=\\sqrt{19}$ and $\\tfrac{\\sqrt{57}}{3}$; treating $AC$ as a diameter gives $\\tfrac72$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EC2-14", "paper": "EC2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>For each positive integer $n$, let $A_n$ be the area of the region between the curve $y=x^{n}(1-x)^{2}$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le1$. Find the value of \\[A_1+A_2+A_3+\\cdots.\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac23$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac1{12}$", "$\\dfrac13$", "$\\dfrac16$", "$\\dfrac14$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Expanding, $x^n(1-x)^2=x^n-2x^{n+1}+x^{n+2}$, so $A_n=\\tfrac1{n+1}-\\tfrac2{n+2}+\\tfrac1{n+3}=\\left(\\tfrac1{n+1}-\\tfrac1{n+2}\\right)-\\left(\\tfrac1{n+2}-\\tfrac1{n+3}\\right)$. Summing from $n=1$, both brackets telescope: the first gives $\\tfrac12$ and the second $\\tfrac13$, so the total is $\\tfrac16$. Starting the sum at $n=0$ by mistake adds $A_0=\\tfrac13$ and gives $\\tfrac12$.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "seq"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EC2-15", "paper": "EC2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Ana thinks of a polynomial $p(x)$ whose coefficients are all non-negative integers. She tells Ben that $p(1)=7$ and $p(10)=2041$, and claims that this is enough for him to work out $p(3)$. What is $p(3)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$73$", "$61$", "$67$", "$55$", "$85$", "$91$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The coefficients are non-negative integers with sum $p(1)=7$, so each coefficient is at most $7$, less than $10$. Hence $p(10)$ written in base ten has the coefficients as its digits: $2041$ means $p(x)=2x^3+0x^2+4x+1$. Then $p(3)=54+12+1=67$. Reading the digits as $2x^3+4x^2+1$ gives $91$.<\/p>", "tags": ["alg"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "EC2-16", "paper": "EC2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>Numbers are arranged in a triangle. Row $1$ contains the single number $1$. Each later row contains one more number than the row above it. Each number is equal to the number diagonally above it to the left plus twice the number diagonally above it to the right, where a missing number counts as $0$. So row $2$ is $2,\\ 1$ and row $3$ is $4,\\ 4,\\ 1$. What is the largest number in row $9$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$5376$", "$1792$", "$3584$", "$896$", "$1120$", "$1024$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Reading each row from left to right as the coefficients of ascending powers of $x$, the rule says row $n+1$ is row $n$ multiplied by $(2+x)$: the left neighbour contributes $x$ times, the right one $2$ times. So row $9$ lists the coefficients $\\binom8k2^{8-k}$ of $(2+x)^8$: $256, 1024, 1792, 1792, 1120, 448, 112, 16, 1$. The largest is $1792$ (it occurs twice). The value $1120=\\binom84 2^4$ comes from assuming the middle entry is largest; $5376$ is the largest entry of row $10$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EC2-17", "paper": "EC2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A park ranger starts at the point $A(1,1)$. She must walk to some point of the $x$-axis and to some point of the $y$-axis, in either order, and then on to the edge of a circular pond whose centre is $(2,3)$ and whose radius is $1$, where she stops. She walks in straight lines between these points. Distances are in kilometres. What is the shortest possible total length of her walk?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$3$", "$2\\sqrt5-1$", "$5$", "$3\\sqrt2-1$", "$\\sqrt2+\\sqrt{13}-1$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Reflect the pond in the $y$-axis and then in the $x$-axis: its centre moves to $C''(-2,-3)$. A walk $A\\to P\\to Q\\to$ pond, with $P$ on the $x$-axis and $Q$ on the $y$-axis, has the same length as the broken line from $A$ via $P$ and the reflected copy of $Q$ to the doubly reflected pond, which is at least $AC''-1=\\sqrt{9+16}-1=4$. The straight line from $A$ to $C''$ crosses the $x$-axis at $\\left(\\tfrac14,0\\right)$ and then the reflected $y$-axis, giving the real point $\\left(0,\\tfrac13\\right)$, so $4$ is achieved: $\\tfrac54+\\tfrac5{12}+\\left(\\tfrac{10}{3}-1\\right)=4$. Visiting the $y$-axis first unfolds to the same point $C''$, so it cannot do better. Going via the origin gives $\\sqrt2+\\sqrt{13}-1\\approx4.02$, slightly longer.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "EC2-18", "paper": "EC2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>The circles \\[x^2+y^2=9\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x^2+y^2-6x+4y-3=0\\] meet at two points $A$ and $B$. What is the radius of the circle that passes through $A$, $B$ and the origin?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\sqrt{13}$", "$\\dfrac92$", "$\\dfrac{13}{2}$", "$3\\sqrt5$", "$2\\sqrt{13}$", "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{13}}{2}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC2-18.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The circles x squared plus y squared = 9 and the circle with centre (3,-2) of radius 4 meeting at A and B, together with the highlighted circle x squared plus y squared minus 9x plus 6y = 0, centre (9/2, -3), passing through A, B and the origin O.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Finding $A$ and $B$ explicitly is messy. Instead, every curve $\\left(x^2+y^2-9\\right)+\\lambda\\left(x^2+y^2-6x+4y-3\\right)=0$ passes through $A$ and $B$, and for $\\lambda\\neq-1$ it is a circle. Through the origin: $-9-3\\lambda=0$, so $\\lambda=-3$, giving $-2x^2-2y^2+18x-12y=0$, that is $x^2+y^2-9x+6y=0$. Its centre is $\\left(\\tfrac92,-3\\right)$ and $r^2=\\tfrac{81}4+9=\\tfrac{117}4$, so $r=\\tfrac{3\\sqrt{13}}{2}$. (The circles do meet: their centres are $\\sqrt{13}$ apart and $1&lt;\\sqrt{13}&lt;7$.)<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EC2-19", "paper": "EC2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>The sides of a triangle have lengths $a$, $b$ and $c$, and $C$ is the angle opposite the side of length $c$. It is given that $a^2+b^2=2c^2$. Which of the following is the set of all possible values of $C$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0^\\circ&lt;C\\le60^\\circ$", "$0^\\circ&lt;C\\le90^\\circ$", "$C=60^\\circ$ only", "$0^\\circ&lt;C&lt;60^\\circ$", "$45^\\circ\\le C\\le60^\\circ$", "$60^\\circ\\le C&lt;180^\\circ$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>By the cosine rule, $\\cos C=\\dfrac{a^2+b^2-c^2}{2ab}=\\dfrac{c^2}{2ab}\\ge\\dfrac{c^2}{a^2+b^2}=\\dfrac12$, using $2ab\\le a^2+b^2$, so $C\\le60^\\circ$, with equality for the equilateral triangle. Conversely, scale so that $c=1$; then $a^2+b^2=2$ and $\\cos C=\\tfrac1{2ab}$. The triangle inequality $|a-b|&lt;1$ is equivalent to $2-2ab&lt;1$, that is $ab&gt;\\tfrac12$, and $ab$ can take every value in $\\left(\\tfrac12,1\\right]$, so $\\cos C$ takes every value in $\\left[\\tfrac12,1\\right)$. Hence $0^\\circ&lt;C\\le60^\\circ$. Spotting only the equilateral case gives $C=60^\\circ$ only.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig", "alg"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "EC2-20", "paper": "EC2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>The circle $C$ has equation $x^2+y^2-2x-4y-4=0$. For a constant $c$, consider the points $P$ on the line $x+y=c$ with the property that the two tangents from $P$ to $C$ are perpendicular to each other. For how many integer values of $c$ are there exactly two such points $P$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$9$", "$11$", "$12$", "$13$", "$5$", "$7$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC2-20.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Circle with centre O'(1,2) and radius 3, a larger highlighted concentric circle of radius 3 root 2, and the line x + y = c meeting it at two points P; from one P the perpendicular tangents touch at T1 and T2, forming a square O'T1PT2.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>$C$ has centre $O'(1,2)$ and radius $3$. If the tangents from $P$ touch $C$ at $T_1$ and $T_2$ and are perpendicular, then $O'T_1PT_2$ has four right angles and two adjacent sides equal to $3$, so it is a square and $O'P=3\\sqrt2$. Conversely every point at distance $3\\sqrt2$ from $O'$ works. So the points $P$ lie on the circle with centre $(1,2)$ and radius $3\\sqrt2$, and there are exactly two of them when the line is closer to $(1,2)$ than $3\\sqrt2$: $\\tfrac{|1+2-c|}{\\sqrt2}&lt;3\\sqrt2$, that is $|c-3|&lt;6$, so $c=-2,-1,\\ldots,8$, giving $11$ values. Using radius $3$ instead of $3\\sqrt2$ in the distance condition gives $9$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EC2-21", "paper": "EC2", "n": 21, "q": "<p>How many values of $x$ in the interval $0\\le x&lt;2\\pi$ satisfy \\[\\sin^6x+\\cos^6x=\\frac58\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$6$", "$8$", "$16$", "$4$", "$0$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let $u=\\sin^2x$, so $\\cos^2x=1-u$ with $0\\le u\\le1$. Since $s^3+c^3=(s+c)^3-3sc(s+c)$ with $s+c=1$, the left side is $1-3u(1-u)$. The equation becomes $3u-3u^2=\\tfrac38$, that is $8u^2-8u+1=0$, so $u=\\tfrac{2\\pm\\sqrt2}{4}$. Both values lie between $0$ and $1$ (and neither is $0$ or $1$), and each gives $\\sin x=\\pm\\sqrt u$, which is four distinct values of $x$ in $[0,2\\pi)$. So there are $8$ solutions. Stopping at the two values of $u$, or taking only positive square roots, gives $2$ or $4$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig", "alg"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "EC2-22", "paper": "EC2", "n": 22, "q": "<p>An equilateral triangle has one vertex on each of the three parallel lines $y=0$, $y=1$ and $y=3$. What is the area of the triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{7\\sqrt3}{4}$", "$3\\sqrt3$", "$\\dfrac{7\\sqrt3}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{13\\sqrt3}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{7\\sqrt3}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{9\\sqrt3}{4}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC2-22.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The parallel lines y = 0, y = 1 and y = 3 with a shaded equilateral triangle whose vertices (0,0), (b,1) and (c,3) lie one on each line.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>Place the vertices at $(0,0)$, $(b,1)$ and $(c,3)$. Equal sides give $b^2+1=c^2+9$ and $b^2+1=(c-b)^2+4$. The second gives $2bc=c^2+3$, so $b=\\tfrac{c^2+3}{2c}$, and substituting in $b^2=c^2+8$ gives $\\left(c^2+3\\right)^2=4c^2\\left(c^2+8\\right)$, that is $3c^4+26c^2-9=0$, or $\\left(3c^2-1\\right)\\left(c^2+9\\right)=0$. So $c^2=\\tfrac13$, the square of the side is $c^2+9=\\tfrac{28}{3}$ whatever the configuration, and the area is $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\cdot\\tfrac{28}3=\\tfrac{7\\sqrt3}{3}$. Taking the side to be $3$ (as if one side were vertical) gives $\\tfrac{9\\sqrt3}{4}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord", "alg"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EC2-23", "paper": "EC2", "n": 23, "q": "<p>Find the coefficient of $x^{50}$ in the expansion of \\[(1+x)+(1+x)^2+(1+x)^3+\\cdots+(1+x)^{100}.\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dbinom{101}{51}$", "$\\dbinom{100}{51}$", "$\\dbinom{100}{50}$", "$\\dbinom{102}{50}$", "$\\dbinom{101}{52}$", "$\\dbinom{102}{51}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Adding $51$ separate coefficients $\\binom{50}{50}+\\binom{51}{50}+\\cdots+\\binom{100}{50}$ directly is hopeless. Instead treat the sum as a geometric series with ratio $1+x$: it equals \\[\\frac{(1+x)\\left((1+x)^{100}-1\\right)}{(1+x)-1}=\\frac{(1+x)^{101}-(1+x)}{x}.\\] The coefficient of $x^{50}$ is the coefficient of $x^{51}$ in $(1+x)^{101}-(1+x)$, which is $\\binom{101}{51}$. Taking only the last term gives $\\binom{100}{50}$.<\/p>", "tags": ["seq"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "EC2-24", "paper": "EC2", "n": 24, "q": "<p>The parabola $y=x^2$ divides the disc $x^2+y^2\\le12$ into two regions. What is the area of the smaller region?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4\\pi+\\sqrt3$", "$2\\pi+3\\sqrt3$", "$4\\pi-\\sqrt3$", "$2\\pi+\\sqrt3$", "$2\\pi-\\sqrt3$", "$10\\pi-\\sqrt3$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC2-24.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The disc x squared plus y squared = 12 and the parabola y = x squared meeting at (plus or minus root 3, 3); the region above the parabola inside the disc is shaded, with the radii from O making an angle of pi over 3 and dashed verticals marking the two right-angled triangles.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The curves meet where $y+y^2=12$, so $y=3$ and $x=\\pm\\sqrt3$. The region above the parabola and inside the circle has area $\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\sqrt3}^{\\sqrt3}\\sqrt{12-x^2}\\,dx-\\int_{-\\sqrt3}^{\\sqrt3}x^2\\,dx$. The first integral cannot be found by our methods, so use geometry: the points $(\\pm\\sqrt3,3)$ are at angles $60^\\circ$ and $120^\\circ$ from the origin, so the area under the arc above $-\\sqrt3\\le x\\le\\sqrt3$ is a sector of radius $\\sqrt{12}$ and angle $\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}$, area $2\\pi$, plus two right-angled triangles with legs $\\sqrt3$ and $3$, area $3\\sqrt3$ in total. The second integral is $\\tfrac{2(\\sqrt3)^3}{3}=2\\sqrt3$. So this region has area $2\\pi+\\sqrt3\\approx8.0$, while the disc has area $12\\pi\\approx37.7$, so it is the smaller region. Forgetting to subtract the area under the parabola gives $2\\pi+3\\sqrt3$, taking the sector angle as $\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ gives $4\\pi+\\sqrt3$, and $10\\pi-\\sqrt3$ is the larger region.<\/p>", "tags": ["int", "trig"], "rating": 8.3}, {"id": "EC2-25", "paper": "EC2", "n": 25, "q": "<p>Twelve points are equally spaced around a circle of radius $1$. Any three of them are the vertices of a triangle, and there are $220$ such triangles. How many of these triangles have an area that is a rational number?<\/p>", "opts": ["$48$", "$24$", "$36$", "$12$", "$60$", "$40$", "$72$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC2-25.svg?v=1\" alt=\"Twelve equally spaced points on a circle with centre O and a shaded triangle on three of them, with radii from O splitting the full angle into central angles 30i, 30j and 30k degrees.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>The three vertices split the circle into arcs subtending $30i^\\circ$, $30j^\\circ$, $30k^\\circ$ at the centre $O$, with $i+j+k=12$. The triangle is made of the three triangles with apex $O$ (with a sign change for an obtuse triangle), and in every case its area is $\\tfrac12\\left(\\sin30i^\\circ+\\sin30j^\\circ+\\sin30k^\\circ\\right)$. The values of $\\sin30m^\\circ$ are $\\pm\\tfrac12$, $\\pm1$ or $0$, except that $m=2,4$ give $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$ and $m=8,10$ give $-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$. So the area is rational exactly when the parts equal to $2$ or $4$ are as many as the parts equal to $8$ or $10$. Of the twelve partitions of $12$ into three positive parts, only $\\{1,5,6\\}$ and $\\{3,3,6\\}$ qualify. There are $24$ triangles of the first type and $12$ of the second, so $36$ in all. Note that the triangles of type $\\{2,4,6\\}$ are right-angled but have area $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$, so &ldquo;all $60$ right-angled triangles&rdquo; is wrong.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "EC2-26", "paper": "EC2", "n": 26, "q": "<p>The circle $x^2+y^2=5$ and the line $x+2y=10$ are given. For each point $P$ on the line, the two tangents from $P$ to the circle touch it at $A$ and $B$, and $M$ is the midpoint of $AB$. As $P$ moves along the whole line, the point $M$ moves along a curve. What is the length of this curve?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\sqrt5\\,\\pi}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}$", "$2\\sqrt5\\,\\pi$", "$\\sqrt5\\,\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{15}\\,\\pi}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt5\\,\\pi}{4}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<figure class=\"sol-fig\"><img src=\"diagrams/EC2-26.svg?v=1\" alt=\"The circle x squared plus y squared = 5 and the line x + 2y = 10 with a point P on the line; the tangents from P touch at A and B, the chord AB passes through the fixed point Q(1/2, 1), M is the foot of the perpendicular from O on AB, and M lies on the highlighted small circle with diameter OQ.\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/figure><p>For $P(p,q)$, the points $A$ and $B$ lie on the circle with diameter $OP$ (the angles $OAP$ and $OBP$ are right angles), which is $x^2+y^2-px-qy=0$. Subtracting from $x^2+y^2=5$ shows that the line $AB$ is $px+qy=5$. With $p=10-2q$ this reads $(10x-5)+q(y-2x)=0$, so every chord $AB$ passes through the fixed point $Q\\left(\\tfrac12,1\\right)$. Now $M$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $O$ to $AB$, so $\\angle OMQ=90^\\circ$ and $M$ lies on the circle with diameter $OQ=\\tfrac{\\sqrt5}{2}$; every point of that circle except $O$ itself occurs. The length is $\\pi\\cdot\\tfrac{\\sqrt5}2$. Using the distance $2\\sqrt5$ from $O$ to the line as a diameter gives $2\\sqrt5\\,\\pi$.<\/p>", "tags": ["coord"], "rating": 8.5}, {"id": "EC2-27", "paper": "EC2", "n": 27, "q": "<p>How many values of $x$ in the interval $0\\le x&lt;2\\pi$ satisfy \\[\\cos\\left(\\pi\\sin x\\right)=\\sin\\left(\\pi\\cos x\\right)\\,?\\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$3$", "$6$", "$4$", "$0$", "$2$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Write the left side as $\\sin\\left(\\tfrac{\\pi}2-\\pi\\sin x\\right)$. Then $\\sin A=\\sin B$ means $A=B+2k\\pi$ or $A=\\pi-B+2k\\pi$ for an integer $k$. With $A=\\pi\\cos x$ and $B=\\tfrac\\pi2-\\pi\\sin x$ this gives $\\cos x+\\sin x=\\tfrac12+2k$ or $\\cos x-\\sin x=\\tfrac12+2k$. Writing $u=\\cos x$, $v=\\sin x$, the point $(u,v)$ is on the unit circle, where $u\\pm v$ takes only values between $-\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt2$; so only $\\tfrac12$ is possible ($-\\tfrac32&lt;-\\sqrt2$ and $\\tfrac52>\\sqrt2$). Each of the lines $u+v=\\tfrac12$ and $u-v=\\tfrac12$ is at distance $\\tfrac{1}{2\\sqrt2}&lt;1$ from the origin, so meets the unit circle twice, and the two lines share only the point $\\left(\\tfrac12,0\\right)$, which is not on the circle. Each point of the circle corresponds to one $x$ in $[0,2\\pi)$, so there are $4$ solutions. Including $u+v=-\\tfrac32$ as if it were attainable gives $6$ or $8$.<\/p>", "tags": ["trig"], "rating": 8.6}];
